      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>"HE is ageing very rapidly, is he not?" "Well —at the ususl rate. Sixty seconds to the minute." EDWIN: "Will your mother consent, do you think?' Angelina: "Yes, I can fix that. I'll get papa to oppose it." SOPHT : "But very fewpeople get what they deserve in this world." Miss Caustique (who has been bored for a long time) : "Aren't you glad?" A GREAT deal can be said about the sagacity of a dog. Any dog is sagacious if it knows enough to keeps away from the dog-stealer of the present time. TOM: "When shall I pay this money back to you?" Jack: "Oh, whenever it is convenient." Tom: "Thanks fur the gift, Jack." "GENTLEMEN of the jury," said a lawyer, "there were just 36 hogs. Please min.-tuber the fact—just three times as many as El the jury-box, gentlemen !" "WHAT can give suc,l a finish to a room as a tender woman's face?" asks a writer. "A tender woman's scrubbing- brush." THE greater liar a man is, the more apt he is to have a little wife who believes everything he says. And the more truthful he is the greater liar she will believe him to be.</p>
        <p>THE WORST ALWAYS HAPPENS. Merritt: "Johnnie fell overboard." Core: "Is that what he's crying about?" Merritt: "No; he had all his fire-crackers in his pocket." IN THE SHOW BUSINESS. —"Are you a cannibal?" she asked of the man in the tent. "1 was in my own country, ma'am. I don't think you'll find any working cannibals over here." RAPID SHORTHANDERS. —Gazzam Graffik is a, smart atenogtapimr. He can take one hun.lred and twenty words a tniitule." Maddox : So can I." Gazzam "But Graffik can read his short- hand writing. ' No Usz FOR. IT.—Kid: "Say, mister, this can• non you sold me is DO good." Dealer: "How s that?" Kid : "I loaded it up to the muzzle and it never burst." "GOOD morning, Uncle Charles! Did you sleep wet!? I'm afraid your bed was rather hard and uneven, but____" "Oh, it was all ri,fht, thank you. I got up now and then during the night and rested a bit, you know !" HUSBAND: "to you know that every time a woman gets angry she adds a new wrinkle to her face? " Wife : "No, I did not ; but if it is so I presume it is a wise provision of Nature to let the world know what sort of a husband a woman has." MISS SMILER : "Why have you thrown ap Mr. Trader?" Miss Cashunter : "Because he has one great failing. ' Miss Smiler: " Whet is that?" Miss Cashunter : "He's just been made bank. rupt !" "I LIKE Sunday-school better than any other school," said Willie. "1 ain pleased to bear that, my little man, ' said the minister. "Now, will you tell me why?"' "Yea, sir; it only COIIIJ9 once a week." '</p>
        <p>A womarr will face a frowning world, and cling to the man she loves through the most bitter adversity; but she wouldn't wear a bonnet that was out of fashion to save the Government. HOTEL Paoreancron "1 see you have given our fineet, suite of rooms to a man called Bilkins. Are you elite he can pay the price?" Clerk: " Yes ; he's Immensely rich." Proprietor: "How do you know ?I' Clerk: "He is old and ugly, and his wife is young and pretty." " OENTLEMAN "I thought you were a blind hepar ?" Beggar: "That's my lay, guv'nor." Gentleman "Well, you are not blind now." Beggar (indignantly) : "Well, sir, can't a poor fellow take a day off occasionally?" A LAWYER s clerk in London, being requested the other day by his employer to show a gentleman the " lions " of the Metropolis, took him to atl the public-houses designated by the title of "The Red Lion," "The Black Lion, "The White Lion," etc., the gentleman enjoying the joke. The result was that the clerk returned to the office in a state of inebriation. HAD To Be.—Aunt Surplice: "How peacefully atilt and solemn it always is on Sunday ! ' Little Nephew: "Yes m; that's because so many child- ren s papas is at home." ZEICE "Say, Uncle Mose, yo' know my brudder Sam wot set himself up as a s'ciety gern'n? Well, he s done gone bin 'rested fo' stealin' oh a suit oh clothes." Uncle Mose "Well, if be ain't demos' ignerantest nigger I ebber seed! Why didn't he buy a suit and nebber pay fo' it, just like de white s'eiety gem'n do?" AN INDIGNATION MEETING. First Barber: "What do you think of people who shave them- selves?" Second Barber: "They ought to be compelled to cut their own hair." COUNSEL: "Well, after the prisoner gave you s blow, what happened?" Prosecutor : gave rue a third one. Counsel: "You mean a second one." Prosecutor: "No, sir. I landed him the second one !" " WHAT'S in a name?" - Ah, well, you don't know everything, that's certain. Salt can be bought, for a few coppers .% block;, but call it chloride of sodium, and the chemist will mulct you to the tune of half-a-crown for one poor scruple. COUNTRYMAN "I reckon they're makin' all that fuss over some great prince er king from some foreign country, ain't they ? " Stranger: "You're wrong, Mr. Hayseed; they're giving a welcome to a bicycle rider who has just broken the record." AN old bachelor recently gave utterance to the following jeu d'esprit : He was introdeced to a beautiful widow of tha same name as himself. The introduction was in this wise: --e" Mr. Evans, permit me to introduce you to Mrs. Evans." "Mrs. Evans!" exclaimed the spirited bachelor; "the very lady I have been in search of for the last forty years !" STUDENT : "How would you advise me to go about collecting a library ?" Professor : "Well, I'll tell you how I managed it. When I was young I bought books and lent them. Now I borrow books and keep them." JOHNNY: "Pa, what does it mean in the book when it says, The woman dissolved into tears ?' " His Pa (of wide experience) : " It means that slis asked for anew dress and didn't get it." "ore women make me very weary," said the agitatress. "I asked one woman if she believed in woman's suffrage, and she didn't know; she'd have to ask her husband." "Did you find out how long she had been married ?" asked the second agitatress. "Yes. Three weeks." "Oh, never mind. I guess shall do to call on again in a year or so." Nov TO BE RETURNED.—Briggs : " Gander seems to be very happy in his newly-married life." Griggs : "He ought to be. All of his wedding presents were given him by people already married." A TEST OF AFFECTION.—Mr. Billing (earnestly): " Ah, Miss Laura, would that there were some- thing I might do, however difficult, to show the ardent affection—" Miro Cooing: "There is; but I'm afraid you'll find it too hard." Mr. Billing "Oh, name it; I will be only too happy—" Miss Cooing: Well, just stop talking nonsense, please." "E AM fearful that we are going to have a long' and very cold winter," suggested a middle-aged mei to the ,tranger who sat beside him in a raii- w carriage. No response came from the stranger, who wore a tired look on his face and did not appear to be sociable. "I say lam afraid we will have a long and very cold winter," repeated the soeis.ble passenger. "Well, if that is worrying you," said the other man, arousing himself out Or: his reverie with a nervous jerk, "just go and give somebody a promissory note for four months for an amount larger than you can hope to pay. Then you'll find that this winter will pass away so quick that you won't have a chance to get cold." r- HE (after the wedding tour): "Urn—my love, these bills are piling up at a fearful rate; but, of course, my angel, with your large income, you are willing and no doubt anxious to help me pay them. ' She: "I? Why, my dear, I haven't a cent." " Wha"— "On my second marriage, all my money went to my late husband's relatives." "Eh? Wha—why didn't you say so 9" "You particularly requested me never to mention my late husband 80-o-o—in your presence."</p>
