      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MY DEAREST NELLIE,</p>
        <p>With all the illness one sees about, I ought tie warn you to be careful not to leave off warm clothing too soon. There is plenty of variety in spring clothing. Some of the new orepons are exceediogly pretty, and make up well. Bodices continue greatly in favour, and are most fantastic, greatly in contrast with the severe simplicity of the full skirts, few of which have any trimming at all. Many of the bodies diflr both in colour and material from the skirt and often the sleeves. The Figaro will no doubt be as much worn this season as it was last. Capes are sure to be much worn, but the very smart ones will be short, reaching only to the waist. Coats and jackets are also likely to be fashionable, and are made to be worn either open or closed, whicil will afford the wearer an opportunity fot displaying pretty blouses. Sleeve 4 are not worn sticking out above the shoulders. The fulness falls downwards towards the elbows, and this exaggeration, though nob artisac, is not neariy so much as that we were accustomed to see a few months since. From the elbows to the wrists all are tight and olose-fitting for morning wear.</p>
        <p>I have seen some delighffnl coats in velveteen, One of these was of dark R )yal blue velvet, turned back with graceful revers of the same, fairly wide at the top, and graduating off to nothing at the waist. Tle basques were very full, standing out in regale- ilates, and each sown we; oovered with narrow jet gimp, the same trimming edging the revers. The sleeves were of the gigot type, not exaggerated in s'zo, and finished with a neat turned back cuff of white satin, covered with jet, the plain vest being also of white satin. A chiffon ruffle was worn round the neck. The following is a handsome spring, dress. It is in Hungarian-blue cloth with band ( f -„al)e.o brown fur and velvet round the full skirt. The over cape, which is of velvet, edged fur, has some par- ticularly becoming revers of the cloth edged with brown waved galon, which continues down the front of the bodice, and is repeated on the collar. Considerable effect is given to the whole by the pretty lace bow and jtbot which forms the vest. The sleeve is very simply made, and is cut in one piece with shaped velvet cuff.</p>
        <p>As regards heati.gear, thrre are some remdtkably pretty hats and t,)ques to be seen. A graceful and easily made toque has a crown of turquoise blue brocade—any odd peu o ilk or velvet will serve the purpose—and the brim of golden•brown villvet and ribbon, the latter fashioned in large loops at the back. In front is one of those new Greek patterned steel ornaments, fastened with knots of ribbon, and wings to match the velvet in colour. Large feathers are being very much worn, as you have doubtless noticed. A very nice hat I saw lately was made of black crinoline straw with a white lace bow at the back, trimmed with black feathers and a huge paradise plume.</p>
        <p>I think you will llkn to know how to make a pretty lamp shale. Tile one lam going to tell you of can be made easily la teu minutes. Got two rolls of crinkled paper, one white, and the other deep yellow. 'lnes° will cost you each, and are all that is rEci tired for the shade, it you already postess a wire fr,..we with brass fitting. If not, one can be bought for lees than a aouple of shillings, large enough ior a full Blvd duplex lamp. Open the rol:s of paper, and lay the white under yellow with the top edges j 'thing. Now gather up five inches b3low the top edge, piercing through with a stiletto, and pass throng, the hole a bodkin threaded with white tape. When the two rolls are gathered up, fasten them by toe string round the brass rings; arrange the Iwnees evenly and firmly; next separate the upper dgee of the paper, press- ing them down here and ti-ero to form a graoeful ruche. Turn up the yellow paper at the bottom, and press it slightly in place. Tie a piece of yellow satin ribbon under the ruche, and the shade Is complete.</p>
        <p>Youra lovingly,</p>
