      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 13. 1895.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 13. 1895.</p>
        <p>The Queen's secretaries at Cimiez work all day.</p>
        <p>Three thousand mirriages are performed every day al over the world. preparation of human hair for the market gives employment to 7,000 Parisians. At an Japanese banquet it is a compliment to ask to exchange cups with a friend. The Duchess of Albany has becom a patroness of the Church Army Needlework Guild. It is said that the prospective Mrs. George Curzon is to have a dowry of one hundred thousand pounds. Australia is a country without orphans or an orhan- age. Every waif is taken to the receiving house, where it is kept until a country home is found for it. A mathematician has discovered that a bicyclist can travel fifteen miles over a good road on his wheel with less exertion than he can walk three miles. •</p>
        <p>The</p>
        <p>We are, says a writer in the" Sun," about to beat the record in inventions according to the statement of a friend of mine whose veracity, of course, I do no guar- antee. He tells me of a "coiffure" machine to be patented, by which, placing a shilling in the slot and cautiously (I presume very cautiously) inserting the head backwards into a frame, the hair is cut, shampooned, dyed to any desired tint, and dressed to any desired style. In fact, the lady who has the hardihood to brave conseluences and stand by her advanced ideas upon fashion may, for the small fee of one shilline, and with one touch of a hand-wheel, be transformed instantane- ously into a "New Woman."</p>
        <p>Over-Eating. When an individual over-eats there is all the more necessity of heavy work to work up in a legitimate way the food elements that are taken into the system. Over- eating. coupled with too little exercise of the muscles, produces a combination that will surely result in an im- poverished system,a weakening of the brain, and decay. Peore are everywhere dying of physical excesses, of useless expenditure of vital force, and we think it would not be too large an estimate to say that probably live- sixths of the constitutional vigour of every man and woman is wasted upon luxurious living and idleness ; and, as a result, the last twenty or thirty years of their life are eked out miserably, sometimes in pain and dis- comfort. A proper amount of food spent in legitimate excercise of the body will perpetuate vigour and fresh- ness and lead to rich old age.</p>
        <p>Well-bred. We often hear the term " well-bred " used in speaking of people,and I have sometimes wondered what is really included in that word, especially when it is applied to a woman or a girl. It includes so much. Well-bred means that a person has a pleasant,gracious manner,is cordial at all times, and speaks and tries to think kindly of everyone the is slow to believe evil, and never sees what another would hide. She does not show that she Is trying to rival Cr eclipse hericompanions. Girls need to be taught the" secret of happiness" quite as much as being taught what is generally termed " accomplish- ments." One great secret of happiness is making others happy, and to do this we need to be trained early to kindness. It was once said of a charming girl that : If she were on a throne she would make every subject feel that she had abdicated in his favour for the day."</p>
        <p>Ringworm in a Family. Where ringworm breaks out in a family, the greatest care should, says the "Family Physician,' be taken to prevent it from spreading. Absolute cleanliness, in- cluding frequent washing with soap and hot water, is essential. The affected child should be isolated, and must on no account be brought in contact with other children. Such things as towels, brushes and combs. comforters, caps, and so on, must never be used in coin- mon hr the affected and the healthy. A dry state of the head and hair is found to be favourable to infection, whilst the presence of greasy applications has the oppo- site effect, so that it is a good plan to use pomade freely. These who object te pomade may use a lotion made as follows :—Stron? acetic, half an ounce ; gly- cerine, two drachms: carbolic acid, half a drachm ; elder-flower water, three ounces; rose water to six ounces. Make a lotion. For external application only. It may he sponged over the head once or twice a day, and will be found very efficacious in warding off infec- tion.</p>
        <p>JOTTINGS.</p>
        <p>"EMY dear friends," said the nervous clergy. man, "Jacob sold his birthright for a pot of mes-</p>
        <p>—sage:" "Au," said the old lady, illustrating moral depravity, "I have seen little children that could neither walk nor talk, run about the streets</p>
        <p>blaspheming." BANKER (finding a burglar in his office): "Thieves I Help Help 1" Burglar: "You'd better stop that row, man, else I'll tell everybody I found nothing in your safe." ANDERSON: My memory is getting weaker and weaker every day. Biggs: 'I can give you a. remedy." What is it,?" " Lend me a</p>
        <p>sovereign. "SQUIRES is perfectly foolish about the safety of his baby, isn't he?" "Why do you think so!" "Well, every time the nurse takes the baby out for an airing there's a policeman with her." A FEARFUL SonisirsmoN.—Fred (to chum) : "I dreamt about you last night, Bob I' Bob: I hope it was pleasant," Fred: "Oh, yes very pleasant while it lasted. I dreamt that you paid the £lO you owed me." 'ran YOUNG LADIES' PROGRESS. - Feeding. bottles corals pink bows—pinaf ores—dolls— scholastic friendships—juvenile parties—false hair jewellery balls —par ti es—bazaars—concerts— flirtatious—eugagemen t-- quarrels reconciliation</p>
        <p>—matrimony. SPUDRINS, who has been refused by eleven girls, says he thinks of becoming a photographer, because he is so successful in producing negatives. "How do 'you like your new lady-help, Mrs. Todgers ?" "1 should like here good. deal more, Mitt. Bodgers, if she was a little less the lady and a little mr.re of a help." " AIN'T is time you paid me that five pounds you owe me 't" asked a farmer of .his neighbour. "'Tain't due," was the reply. "But you promised to pay me when you got back from London." "Well, I hain't been there, he replied. • "REMEMBER , Lots wife !" reiterated the clergyman nine times over. "Remetnber Lot's wife,' cried the exasperated parishioner, "why, its absolutely impossible for one ever to forget</p>
        <p>her.' A VERY brave soldier had both his arms carried off in battle, 'after which his colonel offered him half-a-crown. "Colonel," said the soldier, "you think I have lost a pair of gloves." • MUTUAL RECRIMINATION.—Corn "I hear you keep bad company." Jack (fiercely): "Tell me who said it, and I'll blict,t 'em I I'd rather be slandered myself than to have one talk about you being bad, darling!" AMBIGUOUS.—The Fiancée: "I overheard Clara Murray saying that you do not wear your own hair. Tell me is it true ?" The Financee (indignantly) " : It's false I " BACHELOR'S LOGIC.—" Why don't you get mar- ried ?" said X—to a friend of his. "Because, in the first place I detest women on principle; secondly and chiefly, because marriage would interfere with my literary work." " What class of work?" "I am writing love stories." MAY: "Don't you think your landlady's little boy is an angel ?' Frank: "Not yet; but I have hopes!" JASPAR "Did you ever catch an electric eel ? " Jumpuppe : "I am not sure. I had an experience with one once, but whether I caught him or he caught me I never could determine." A CERTAIN debating society is discussing the question as to which is the angrier—the husband who goes home and finds that dinner is not ready, or the wife who has dinner ready and whose hus- band does not come home. It is believed that the debate will end in a draw. TRIG RULING PASSION. —Host (proudly) : "And this is a masterpiece of Rembrandt? " Visitor: "Great Scot, man! What is that unsightly hole in the corner? It spoils the whole picture." Host: " Well, you see, my wife is an autograph' collector, and she wouldn't rest until she had cut the signature out and pasted it in her album." IT takes a white to get used to a woman's ways. When a young husband steals up behind his wife while she stands at lidt dressing-case add suddenly bends f.,rward and prints an unexpected kiss on her lips, he gains the knowledge that a woman holds about 197 pins in her mouth when she is dressing. GUIDE: "In this castle, gentlemen, lived the Knight Dagobert and his beautiful wife. The knight's prowess was well—" Tourists: "Oh, •do.spare us a' long-winded story. " Tell no the conclusion, and that will be enough." Guide: "All right. Here is the conclusion: And now, gentleman, as I have told you such a thrilling tale, I lupe you will give me a trifle with which to drink your health." PRACTICAL AUNT: "Do you think you are qualified to become the wife of a poor man?" Sweet Girl : "01,, yes, it's all fixed. We are to live in a cottage, and I know how to make cottage pudding." Wioonts : "Let's go into this restaurant." Parrott: " Whew I How can we lunch on such a day as this? ' Wiggins: " Well, I propose simply to fan myself with the bill of fare IN A HOLE. —" I am having great difficulty in settling a bet." What are the circemstances ?" "1 lost." A LESSON.—EIsie: "A fortune-teller has pre- dicted that Lucy will be married," Ethel : "That ought to be enough to cure her of superstition." THE Bxsr EVlDENCE.—Cobwigger : "Is it long since he boil, ht this suburban residence?' Merritt: "It can't be. He hasn't tried to sell it yet." LATIN' VERE "Oh,, you will take me down to supper, won't you, Mr. Blinks : " (Delight of Mr. Blinks). Blinks: "But pray tell me Lady Vera, why I am so honoured." Lady Vera : "Well, you are the only man my husband isn't likely to be jealous of." FIRST YOUNG MAN (at seaside) : "I have met a good many amateur photographers, but I never saw one quite so devoted to the fad as you are." Second Young Man: "I never take photographs I" First Young Man: Eh? Then why do you carry that detective camera about with you all day, week in and week out?" Second Young Man: "Because when the girls see me coming all the ugly ones run 'sway, and all the kretty ones stay." • How TO BECOME PERFECT.-10BOW the advice you give to others. A REGRETTED DEMISE. Bengal : "Hoots, Dom)? ; have you heard about ta accident?" Donal' : No ; what is it? ' Dougal : " McPher- son v drooned last nicht." Donal' "What a peety ! ' (Sudden recollection searching his pockets), "Bat, gracious me man, he's away we ma knife!" "Now, isn't he an angel?" said the fond mother as she seated the little fellow in his high chair at the table for the first time. "A. sort of destroying angel,' remarked the cynical father, as he saw a Costly decante'r go spinning to the floor with a crash. A SLIGHT DIFFERENCE.—" Donnerwetter, what a sight you are.' - "Just as I was leaving the house to come to the Glub, my wife pelted me with flowers." "But that doesn't account for your bruised and battered appearance." "You see, she forget to take them out of the pots!" lir every Man's cat was as big a nuisance as his neighbour's eat, there would'nt be a cat in the world. A Goon EXCUSE. Customer (to Waiter): "How can you give me such a dirty serviette?" Waiter: "Beg your lairden, sir, it is only folded the wrong way!' LADY: "Ca,, you cook, wash, and iron, and do chamber work ?" Bridget : " Yes, indade." Lady : " Wel., I am willing to take you on trial." Bridget : "Gi couldn't think of vimrkin' fur yez widout first lookid. at your recomrnitniations from the ladies what has been in your service." THE addresses of a young man having been declined by a young lady, he paid court to her sister. "How much you resemble your sister," said he, the evening of the first call. "You have the same hair, the same forehead, and the same eyes—" "And the same noes I" she added, quickly. He has iitopped calling at that house. SHORT-SIGHTED gentleman chooses a pair of spectacles. "These glasses," he says "are not strong enough for me." "Eat, sir, they are No. 2." "What have ,you next to No. 2? " "No. 1." "And after that?" "After No. I air, you will want a dog I" "Now my Bali: girls and boys," said a teacher, "I want you to be very still—so still that you can hear a pin drop." In a moment all was silent, when alittle boy shrieked out "Let her drop!"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>NAPOLEONA AND LOVE.</head>
        <p>NAPOLEONA AND LOVE.</p>
        <p>Men have not forgotten that Buonaparte once de- nounced love as a hurtful passion from which God should protect his creatures ; and they have, for this, among other reasons, pronounced him incapable of dis- interested affection. But it is also true that he likewise denounced Buttaftinco for having. among other crimes committed by him, " married to extend his influence ;" and we are forced to ask which Of the two sentiments is genuine and characteristic. Probably both and neither, according, to the mood of the man. Outward caprice is, in great natures, often the mask af inward persistency, especially among the unprincipled who suit their lan- gunge to their present purpose. in fine disdain of corn- Mon-place consistency. The primitive Corsican was both rude and gentle, easily move to tears at one time, insensate at another; selfish at one moment, lavish at another; and yet he had a consistent character. Althou 7h disliking in later life to be called a Corsican, Napoleon was nevertheless typical of-his race :he could depise love,vet render h;m- self its willing slave: he was fierce and dictatorial, vet, as Josephine said" tenderer and weaker than anybody dreamed." And thus it was in the matter of his mar- riage ; there were elements in it of romantic, abandoned hut likewise of shrewd, calculating selfishness. In his callow youth his relations to the other sex han been either childish, morbid. or immoral. During his earliest manhood he appeared like eite who desired the training rather than the substance of gallantry. As a Jacobin he sought such suppert as he could find in the goodwill of the women related to men in power ;as a French patriot he put forth strenuous efforts to secure an influential alliance through matrimony, and it is cer- tain that he made advances for the hand of the rich and beautiful Desire,. Clary. He appears to have addressed Mme. Permon, whose fertune, despite her advanced ()fn., would have been a great relief to h:s destitution. Re- fused by both, he was in a disordered and desperate motional state until military and political success gave him Suffi rient self-confidence to try once more.—Prof. Sloane's Life of Napoleon in the " Century."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>_ _ r THE LIVERPOOL MURDER.</head>
        <p>_ _ r THE LIVERPOOL MURDER.</p>
        <p>The inquest on tt.e body of Richard Moyse, a witdor of secondhand books, who was murdered in his hOuse in Rederoas-street on the 19th February, was concluded on Monday before the Liverpool Coroner. Among the wit- nesses was an office-cleaner named Mrs. Catherine Davies, who identified the prisoner Miller as a man who between 9.30 and 9.40 on the morning of the murder stopped her In South Castle-rtreet, nearly oppesite Redeross•street, and conversed about the murder. After several other witnesses had been examined, the jury returned a ver- dict of wilful murder against Miller.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>WE PARTED AT THE ALTAR;</head>
        <p>WE PARTED AT THE ALTAR;</p>
        <p>BY LAURA JEAN LIBBEY,</p>
        <p>Author of "A Mad Betrothal," "lone," "Parted by Fate," " Floribet Lover," etc., etc.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XVII. THE PANGS OF JEALOUSY. Frederick Thornton gazed with painful intensity into the git l's beautiful face. "Just such a face has haunted me in my dreams," he muttered, hoarsely. "I have a dim recollection of having seen such a face before, but where I cannot imagine. Since that accident, which nearly cost me my life, there seem to be strange leaks in my brain. "It must be only fancy that I have seen this girl before. How strangely ray heart thrilled as I touched her hand, like an electric shock. She is a beautiful. little creature. If my heart were not already Vivian's, I—l—should be in danger of losing it. My mother's young companion is cer- tainly charming." He laid his lovely burden down upon the velvet divan in his mother's boudoir, and rang the bell for one of the servants. "Miss Carlisle has fainted, Patty," he said to the maid who soon made her appearance in answer to his summons. "I will leave her in your charge." He had scarcely closed the door behind him ere Doris's blue eyes fluttered open. "Leave me, Patty," she cried, as she raised her eyes to the anxious face bending over her. "You can do nothing for me. Nothing." And when she was left alone, she turned her face to the wall with moans piteous to hear. It never occurred to her to inquire how she came there. She quite believed one of the servants had brought her here when they found her lying in a a dead faint in the corridor below. The sweet, joyous strains of the dance music and gay laughter floated up to her as she lay there, with her face buried in the pillow. Should she lie there in such wretched abandon while Vivian, the beauty, and Frederick danced and laughed the hours away so gaily down below in the rose-embowered ball- room No, no; she could not. It would drive her mad. Silently Doris rose from the divan and crept down into the corridor again. As she passed the conservatory, glancing in, she saw the two for whom she was searching standing by the fountain. "Should she turn away, or walk in and face them?" Doris asked herself. She could not; she would not bear it. She was deadly sick and faint with the bitterest pain of jealousy that could burn or stab a human heart. She could not endure to see Frederick Thornton bend his handsome head over Vivian. Be was hers --her husband—not Vivian's. It was hard and cruel. Her face flushed hotly, and her lips quivered. She felt that she must cry out to them—that she must say: "Oh, Frederick, my love, leave her and come and comfort me, or I shall go mad I" Should she cross over to Vivian's side, and cry out, bittetly : "He is not yours! Do not look at Lim or smile at him ! Cease to try to win him! He is mine, not yours! He can never be yours, for he has married me !" The impulse was so strong to utter the words that Doris bit her lips until the pain became insup- portable. She saw on the face of her lovely rival a look Vivian's face had never worn before: What was he saying to her that brought those blushes to her cheeks and the glad smile into her dazzling dark eyes? Dare he—who was not free to woo and win Vivian—dare he breathe to her one word of love? Doris knew that she ought to go; that is was an I intrusion on her part to remain, but she could not I tear herself away. The blood was boiling in her I veins, her heart was beating fast. At that moment a waltz struck up again, and with a smile Frederick drew Vivian's little gloved hand through his arm and led her back to the ball- room by another door. And Doris crept close to a great branching mag- nolia near the entrance and watched them. Vivian seemed to float through the mazes of the graceful waltz like a veritable fairy. Doris did not take her eyes from her husband and Vivian, her beautiful rival, no matter in what part of the ball-room they happened to be. She saw Frederick hold close to his heart his fair partner, and it seemed to Doris's distorted imagina- tion that he held her more closely than the occa- sion demanded. At last—oh I deliverance from torture !—the music ceased; the waltz was over, Frederick ofiered Vivian his arm then, and they promenaded around the room. "I should go away from here," muttered Doris, as she watched them. "Ah I my curse is that I love him. I could not live away from him. I love him with the bitterest love—a thousand times more cruel than hate." While she stood there Mrs. Thornton approached. She saw Doris and stopped short. The pallor on the girl's face startled her. "You do not look well, Miss Carlisle," she said, gently. "Had you not better go into the grounds and walk awhile in the cool air, or go up to your room and rest? I shall not need you any more to-night: you look tired." Then Mrs. Thornton caught sight of her son and Vivian. She turned to Doris *ith a pleased smile. "What a handsome couple they make," she said—"my son and Vivian —do they not, Miss Carlisle?" Doris tried to murmur some answer, but her voice died away on her white lips. "The dearest wish ot my heart will be gratified when I see them married, Mrs. Thornton went on. A sudden impulse, an irresistible, mad longing, urged her to turn to Frederick's mother, and say : "He can never marry that proud beauty P • he is already married. I am his wife." But this was *neither the time nor the place for such revelation. Without waiting for Doris to reply, Mrs. Thorn- ton moved on, and Doris resumed her patient watch again. Some one had claimed Vivian fbr a quadrille, and Doris noticed with bitter pain that Frederick did not attempt to seek another partner. He stood quite alone where she had left him. Of what was he thinking that he looked so dreamily at the pro- gramme he held! Was he thinking of that other ball, and the youpg girl who had accompanied him there, to her bitter cost? Was he thinking of the pitiful sequel that had followed on the heels'of it? Doris imagised he was, and again that old perplex- ing question recurred to I,er : Why had he wedded .her, if he meant to desert her? Would anyone, looking into his dark, handsome face, believe he cold be guilty of so cruelly deserting the bri,te heliad married, almost at the very altar? She remembered the words of the poet, who has said: "That one may smile, and smile, and be villain." Did he have no thought of the young heart he had broken, the young life he had blighted. "If he could have but loved her, how different life might have been for her," she toll herself. "He does not love me; he cares only for 'Vivian," she murmured. "If I die to-morrow," she cried, with a bitter moan, "who would grieve for me? Not Frederick Thornton. If he heard I was dead, it would be a great relief to him. He could look up calmly, and say She is out of my way for ever.' If he did not any it; that is the thought that would flit through his mind. "God forgive me I Ido not want to be wicked, but I think Heaven has laid a great curse upon my life. lam cursed- by fate. I was so lonely, my childhood and girlhood were so unhappy; my one prayer to Heaven was to send me some one who would love me. I had no gentle mother, no loving sister, no kind father—no kindred on the great, wide, cruel earth. I was so desolate I craved love</p>
        <p>—only love—and it has been denied me. What is there left to live for? He will never, never love me 1" .</p>
        <p>_.. . As she watched, she saw Frederick cross to where Vivian sat, and bend over her a moment. Then she rose, took his arm, and together they passed out of the ball-room into the moonlit, rose- bordered terrace. The poor wretched young wife stood motionless . beside the crimson clove carnations, her hands vi, Hai: CAUSES i'livlrt.Ss ? ueegging ot Cho pi,res pressed tigiltli over her heart. Should she follow temptationshado shadow was a them or not. blackhead, e. The only pr yen. iv eis Tmorerieetoltebaanftteiikethceoaud llgareinsiisatg. Thlhaecklitletaled,pl„urgoli,:t,trh%otteeritdoree of the pimple is called a, Silenhetlyas CUTICVRA SOAP, the most effective skin purifying anti .. the tar h down tlaienehiaV beach-walk tablekr to door.th e riversT bey b hadria wandered Doris sweetest for toilet and nursery. It is the only proweri- beautifyimr wtap in the. world, as welt as the pur-t a' d cahn- tive of piinT.lea, blackheads, red, rough ai d oily ski. , red, rough hands, with s could soe the glimmer of Vivian's white dress hapelefs nails, dry, thin a et falling hair, a1..l simple baby bletnisliee, tecause the through the trees as the patches of moonlight fell only preventive of inflammation and clogging of poris, upon' it. Thera was a deep silence among the the cu uses of Minor affections of the skin,scalp, and hair, trees, as she went swiftly along, that it seemed to Sale sre.t 0, than the combined sales of all other skin Doris that they were waiting, silent, motionless, for and cern pl,:t6..tn erap.. . the pitiful scene _whieh_W.,.s 1.4 foP.OW. Sometimes</p>
