      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>CAPITAL" CHAT.</head>
        <p>CAPITAL" CHAT.</p>
        <p>Young Mr. Charles Russell, son of the Lord chier Justice, has received congratulations from all quarters on the way in which, as solicitor to Lord Queensberry, he prepared the case for his lordship's defence. Curiously enough, the success of the de- fence was largely due to a piece of luck. It appears that one of the principal witnesses who was to have been called to support Lord Quetinsberry's case left his lodgings in London some time ago rather hurriedly, leaving his rent unpaid. An agent of Mr. Russell called to inquire after the missing lodger, and was told that nothing was known of him, but on payment of the rent due lie could have a box which had been left behind. Needless to say, the rent was paid and the box taken away in triumph. When opened it was found to contain a score or so of important letters.</p>
        <p>Another legacy, amounting to four thousand pounds, has come to Mr. Henry George from one oi his American admirers. A similar windfall came to him not long ago ; but Mr. George found himself a hundred pounds out of pocket, in baying to defend the testator's sanity in the Law Courts.</p>
        <p>Rather more than two years ago some particulars were given of an extremely interesting experiment that was being made by the Belgian Cnpin y that has undertaken the construction of a railway around the long series of rapids that blocks tini magnificent Congo waterway a short distance from its mouth. The Company had experienced the greatest difficulty in obtaining a sufficient supply of native labour, and had even resorted to mea- sures which so closely resembled slave-trading that public opinion in Europe had put a peremptory stop to such modes of recruiting labourers. In their extremity the directors of the railway be- thought themselves of the Chinese coolies, whose docility and patient perseverance is provsrbial, and in November, 1892, LI: ere were landed at Matadi a body of Chinese labourers destined to work on the construction of the new line. The experiment was a dismal failure. It was found that the coolies could not support the trying climate of tropical Africa, at any rate under the conditions of hard labour and unaccustomed food to which they were subjected. Disease carried them off in large num- bers. Many hundreds died, and the remnant be- came extremely dejected and almost useless tor the purposes for which they had been brought so far away from their homes.</p>
        <p>The sequel was pathetic in the last degree. One morning it was found that the majority of the sur- vivors had disappeared,aud inquiry elicited the fact that, driven to despair by the misery of their sur- roundings, they had set out to walk to China. They knew that their home was in the East, and accord- ingly they bad determined to walk across Africa as the first stage on the journey. How many perished and how—whether by starvation or at the hands of the native tribes through whose country they attempted to make their way—will never be known, but a few succeeded in reaching stations on the middle river, where they found employment as cooks and gardeners. It is a curious story—one of the most curious in the whole 'history ot African colonisation, but the attempt to utilise Chinese labour on the Congo is not likely to be repeated—at any rate for the present.</p>
        <p>Mr. Sweetman's resignation can hardly be des- cribed as a blow to his colleagues in the Nationalist party. The member for East Wicklow has rarely ben seen in the House of late, and since he de- clared for an immediate dissolution on Home Rule has virtually ceased to discharge any of the duties of a representative. His resignation is a little awk- ward, for East Wicklow is perhaps the one consti- tuency in which a combination of the Tory and the Parnellite vote could succeed in replacing an Anti- Psrnellite by a Parnellite or a Tory It will be in- teresting to see whether this union of forces will take place—all in the sacred cause of the Union.</p>
        <p>The present Lord Chancellor and the late Tory, Lord Chancellor are having a kind of parliamentary steeplechase with two Bills, each of which would enable an accused person, and a husband and wife, to give evidence in criminal cases. The Lords are passing both Bills, and sending them down to the Commons, the sole point of difference between them of any moment being that the Lord Chancellor's Bill does not include Ireland in its scope, and Lord Halsbury's does. When a Bill with the same objects was before the Commons last year the Irish members objected to the inclusion of Ireland,which was accordingly struck out, and by a sort of freak of humour on the part of Lord Salisbury the two Bills are being sent to the Commons as they stand, that the Commons may make a choice. It is an absurd way of legislating; but as neither of the high legal gentlemen is disposed to withdraw his Bill, it will be left for the Commons to dispose of them, where, of course, some Conservative member will move the inclusion of Ireland in the Lord Chancellor's Bill. The amendment will be rejected and then Lord Halshury's Bill will disappear from the order paper.</p>
        <p>-- The Tory " Sheffield Daily Telegraph" says of the Leamington squabble :—" The ultimate outcome, of course, will be the withdrawal of Mr. George Peel. His party cannot afford to keep up a candidate who is not likely to poll more than four hundred or five hundred votes. In the meantime, Mr. Chamberlain must be enjoying some galling moments. He was warned that if he persisted he would come to grief. He has persisted, and disaster faces him."</p>
        <p>We confess says tbe London "Daily Chronicle,' our agreement with Mr. Sydney Grundy's com- plaint in a contemporary of the blotting of Mr. Wilde's name from the bills and programmes of his plays. Either one thing or the other—drop the plays, or give the author the credit of them. The course ac' inlly taken is doubtless the most con- vient to the management, but it does not satisfy either the moral or the artistic conscience. London has accepted these plays as harmless and amusing productions, and has drawn a good deal of pleasure out of them. If they are so to be judged, and if the management is to continue to profit by them, let the author have his share of the credit. Judge. a man by what he is, and an artist by what he pro- duces. Even with regard to the man, we surely do not err in following the example'of the Recording Angel, and setting down his good as well as his bad deeds.</p>
        <p>lam able to say on excellent authority that the Duke of Cambridge at last meditates retiring. He believes, it is said, that all danger of the abolition of the post of Commander-in-Chief has now passed away, and that no Ministry would seek to discon- tinue an office which he avers to be necessaiy to the well-being of the service.</p>
        <p>Many members of the House of Commons observed with regret on Tuesday that there was an extremely meagre attendance of peers in the Upper louse when the present Speaker attended there for the last time to hear the Royal assent given to certain bills. This feeling has found expression in a motion which Mr. Brunner has this evening howled in, to move an address to the Queen "praying Her Majesty to direct that the practice of summoning this House to attend in the House of Lords for the purpose of hearing the declaration of the Royal assent to bills passed by bathhouses be discontinued, and that the Royal assent, to bills be in future de- clared to the House of Commons in the Commons Chamber."</p>
        <p>The grievances of telegraph clerks were discussed at a meeting held in Manchester on Safurday,and a resolu- tion was passed, among others, expressing indignation that the Postmaster-General showed no signs of giving a fair scale of Wages or removing or modifying the system of classification. A North-East Lancashire correspondent states that the cotton trade of the county is in a much better posi- tion than a month ago, a much greater portion of machinery being in full work.</p>
        <p>EPPS'S COCOA,—GRATWIM AND COMFORTING. —" By thorough knowledge of the natural laws which govern the operations Of digestion and nutrition, and by a careful appli- cation of the fine properties of well-selected COCOA. Mr. Epps has provided for our breakfast and supper a delicately flavoured beverage which may save us many heavy doctors bills. It is by the judicious use of suet articles of diet that a constitution may be gradually built up until strong enough to resist every tendency t ) disease. We may escape many a fatal shaft keeping ourselves well fortified with pure blood and a prop.aly nourished frame."—Cieil 8 r ice Gazette.— Made simply with boiling water or milk.—Fold only in packets, by Grocers, la'oelleel—" JAMES EPPS &amp; Co., Ltd., Homceopathic Chemists, London."—Also Makers of Epps's Cocoaine or Cocoa-Nib Extract: Tea-like: A thin beverage of full flavour, now se, ilh msnv beneficially taking the place of tea. Its active princi de being a gentle nerve stimulant. supplies the needed energy without unduly exciting the system.—ildrt.</p>
        <p>T E IN DIT6T di AL S Tu ATE) s.</p>
        <p>(BY MR. H. WHITHAm.) " What is the worker.; honest, share .)t the :Wealth hislahohr producea ? "—J. PODMORE JUNR. " Examiner " February, 1895.</p>
        <p>I was vet.; pleased to read the above question ,so nicely put by our young friend gr. l'odinore in his note to the " 1 was pleased for two rrli- sons. The name of Pod more is one deer to all old Warringtonians. Especially is it dear to those who had a close acquaintance with the tardy olil re- former in whom was found no guile. Again,it seems right that such 'a question is fittingly put by, if I am right, the grandson of honest George. I am not intending to make any -ffort at giving a straight answer to the above question. Allowtne to give, right here, some sort. of an explanation for writing at all, and then we will attend to the busi- ness in hand. .</p>
        <p>I work five days a week, that gives me one full day a week, and about four hours each evening all to myself. i have no a^quaintances. There are several well stocked libraries at my disposal. I do a lot of yeading, and now, dear reader, you can see that this writing of mine is neither more nor less that: a nicc quit,: way of blowing off toe steam. It has always been to me a matter of great concern as to how men who did no reading passed their spare time. Possibly they make up for it by doing a heal) of thinking. And yet I don't, know about that either. 1 cannot fancy the thoughts of a thinker who does no reading can be of much use to anyone. In the good old days when workingmen, and of course, everybody else, were so much better off there was lots of time for thinking. This must be so, because Mr. Hunter Watts says that " the worker S are in a far worse state now than they were in the middle ages." In those dear regrettable days a man had the privilege of being told to re- main and die a contented death in the village where he was born. If an of my readers doubt this happy state of things let them read the Cob- den tract "Our old poor laws." What a deal of high thinking could be done by the general ruck of men while anxiously waiting for the first stroke of the curfew bell. Lights out and into bed-: Na- ture's 'sweet restorer would in those good old days he wooed by the thoughtful mind only after the invigorating exercise of some twelve or fourteen hours passed amassing the wealth which built up " Merrie England," and signs of which we see in the splendid ruins of castle and abbey that dot the miserable and unhappy England of to-day. Will someone kindly point out to me the author who had written Freeman-like, of those good old days when the worker received his honest share of the wealth he produced. We were ignorant, very ignorant in those dear old Middle Ages, yet we must allow that, then, as now, an occasional giant was ; born. A John Ball would step out to the front and tell men of their duty towards each other, and drop an occasional hint of the good old days that had even then been.left far behind. My solid opinion just now, and at this time of writing, is that we are better off at this latter end of the 19th century than we have ever been since the day when, according to Professor Drummond, the Congo worm wriggled ashore and started man off in his struggle for an existence. We know now that all men ought to have equal opportunities. We know that all men are only born equal in a certain sense. We also know that the physically and mentally infirm would not, in a just and humane society be punished, as they are, for the infirmities they are not responsible for. We know that out of the present condition of things the alum has its rise, and that out of the slum comes the misery. We also know that out of the present state ot things the rich man is enabled to evolve a palace that hears no comparison with the humble stable in which a carpenter's son was once born. Better than all, we know that the rich man of to-day is a standing menace to the world, and that his eyes are being opened to the fact. Who shall say we are not better off than our fore- fathers were? Had they this knowledge? Cer- tainly not. Possessing this fund of information we are made capable of looking around and adding to our already rich stores The more we know of the ; conditions of life obtaining through the past ages, and the more satisfied we shall be that it only depends upon ourselves to grapple with them, as they are now before us to-day, and bring them into the service of man. - .</p>
        <p>We workers at not receiving an honest share of the wealth we produce. lam disposed to think we do foolishly in this. And yet I must confess I keep on doing it myself. I, like my fellows, never stop to inquire who are the workers, nor what would be my share if I could receive it in a lump sum each week. I am quite sure that no revolution • we could possibly bring about would make Gladstone, Ruskin, or Spencer, a worker in the same sense that a pail carrier is one, hut I do think it possible for us to make it impossible for a man to die worth as much as, say Gilbert Greenall or A. B. Walker were. In the wills of these two MEM it was clearly proven to the workers of War- rington how selfish, how 'sublimely selfidi men cou'd be. and yet die in all the odour of sanctity, whatever that may mean. These men produced nothing that was of any real good to the com- munity. No great talents did either of them pos- sess. The world i 4 no poorer now they are gone. It was no richer for their presence. I bear them no ill-will. They were the simple excrescencee. nature uses for slapping us in the face. .</p>
        <p>„ I have often wondered how it would be if any one of our employers was to come to one of use physical workers and invite WI to take a share in the works. Take a share in every sense—in respon- sibility, anxiety and worry. What should we do? am inclined to think that we should be willing to risk our very soul's salvation for the sake of the prospective profits. 19 there one among us now, having a thrifty spirit in him, and ten talents to invest, does not seek to make it into twenty. So, we are almost forced to the conclusion that we ought to try to alter character, and conditions will alter themselves. In Plato's ” Republic " one of the d;sputants, and an intellectual bully, imposes upon his unwilling listeners the brutal paradox that the reallN wise men is he " that is perfect in injustice." Pew men in these day would be willing to take his place in the market and urge such a doctrine, and yet it is fair to say that it is this very perfection in injustice that makes countless thousands mourn.</p>
        <p>Workingmen themselves support the system of interest. They, with the spirit of thrift in them, prefer to buy in the cheapest market and sell in the dearest. How then can they, and be consistent, complain when their employers do the same thing? We acknowledge the right of any one man to own an acre of land, then why object to a mere handful of Englishmen owning 200,000,000 acres of Ameri- can soil, besides millions of acres in England ? £160,000,000 is taken by the land owning class of England in the shape of lands rent and royalties from the nation. They give back from this the paltry sum of £400,000, and then these gentry look with serene satisfaction upon the noble effarts made by the workingman to he thrifty, and defend his home and country by maintaining an immense standing army, and subscribing liberally towards building iron clads that often prove to be coffin ships. The question of building iron clods and making cannon and ball that will pierce them is about the hugest farce this progressive age has seen. I am often amused at the way Americans refer to England. The tight little island is so small that you have to be careful lest you fall off. So small in fact, that you have to buy your very bread from the biggest nation in the world. Yes it is true you have to buy your bread of us, but how it comes about passes my comprehension. Mr. Pringle re- cently presented an exhaustive report to the Royal Commission on Agriculture in which he states that 28,000 acres .of land are out of cultivation in Essex alone. Why it must he clear then that, England is too big. You have more land than you know what to do with. I should say the patriotic Tory who, in Warrington always votes straight is not quite satisfied when he reads that in one parish 2,800 acres of land are in the hands of six farmers, who are rated on the average at 104. 61. per acre, and 220 acres are let in 70 tenancies to small work- ing farmers, who are rated at £2 10s. per acre, I am in a fairly optimistic humour just now, so shall not use any hard words, but how any KIWI can claim to be patriot or a lover of his fellow-man, and vote for a party of swells who Merely batten on the proceeds of such a condition ot things is past</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>Tx EXANIINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 13, 1895.</head>
        <p>Tx EXANIINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 13, 1895.</p>
        <p>rily comprehension. In the coming ages few men will be anxious to claim a Tory ancestry. The theory of heredity will be perfected that a an exhibiting a weak intellect, or a grasping disposi- tion will be known as the descendant of the old tossilised Tories. He who expects to receive his share of the wealth produced by his industry under the present condition of things is unwise. We are like an engineer who knowing some of the wheels of his engine are out of order still expects his en- gine to run smoothly. Let us look at a few of the things that are out of o-der, and depend 'upon us to put them in order, and also at a few of the things that are grinding us slowly, yet surely. into shape. Standing right at the front and showing a very bold face is the church, or let me say religion, and then I may not be suspected of pointing to any par- ticular party. It is a fact that cannot be gainsaid that the people as a body are drifting further and further away from all religion. It is also a fairly well attested fact that the great majority of those who attend places of worship are not Christians in the sense of being Christ-like. "Where your tree- sure is, "Jesus says," there will your heart be." Let any one visit a fashionable church, or indeed any place of worship, other than the little Bethels or Salvation Army places, and judge if the attendants are willing to bear the burdens of their poorer brethren. I know all about the exceptions to this rule, and have made a thorough study of the ques- tion. Wealth in a church is the greatest curse it can be afflicted with. Take all this wealth away and the church may then have a chance to place itself in line with the requirements of the age. All the talk about confiscation, robbery and so forth is so much nonsense. The robbery started ages ago, and it was the strong who robbed the weak. Jus- tice and equity will put these matters on a firm basis, and religion will become then what the heart of man intends it to be, a something that will bene- fit us here and prepare us for all possibilities. No man with any thought about him, can look at the great structures scattered over the land without a fellow-feeling of sorrow at their present utter use- lessness. Think of a homeless mortal crouching under a church wall. What sort of feelings are likely to rise in such a one's breast when he knows both shelter and gold in abundance are close to him and yet he, like One before him, has not where to lay his head. This is the first stone in the edifice that the dark ages erected that must be overthrown. The religion of to-day is out of the order of progress. It has failed to move on, therefore the harmony of the whole has been disturbed hence the greatest cause for our suffering. An effete and worn-out doctrine has been taught to the people to such an extent that it has developed the sordid side of our natures. Men have looked after their own pitiful soul's salvation on the exact lines on which they have looked after their banker's account. As a con- sequence of this the greatest criminals are always church goers. If my friends doubt this let them commence only with. the Glasgow Bank frauds and count right along to the present and they will be surprised at the little hold religion has upon the mind corrupted with a base and false religion. My experience during the past few years has been sufficient to justify me in looking forward to and hoping for a religion that shall change the heart of man. if Jesus Christ came on earth again, I think he would certainly repudiate the present State ' church system, and a very large proportion of the regular chapel attenders. At all events the people I are doing it, sure. The people have not been taught even that religion was a reasonable thing. Mind lam not attacking what might fairly be called true religion, simply that which has made men and women the callous hard hearted wretches that now look upon the misery of the world un- moved. I hope to offend no one, but I will give two extracts from preachers who were popular in their time. Rev. Jonathan Edwards taught that "When the saints in heaven look upon the damned in hell, with how much enlargement of the heart will they praise Jesus who was pleased so to dis- tinguish them," "who deserved no better than they." Think what a man taust be who really took this into his head and acted upon it. John Milton so materialized spiritual things that re- ligion is to-day the gross thing we see it, and man is simply struggling to throw off its baneful in- fluence. My next extract is is from Spurgeon whose effort in depicting the Heavenly Father's work is simply horrible," In fire exactly like that we have on earth thy body will die, asbestos-like, for ever unconsumed ' • all thy veins roads for the feet of pain to travel On; every nerve a string on which the devil shall for ever play his diabolical tune of hell's unutterable lament.' YAW Spurgeon preached twenty years to immense audiences, and more than twenty million copies of his sermons have been cir- sulated in the various languages of Christendom, and yet I feel I would much rather take my chances through all the eternities with hell as an abode, and the devil for a companion than merely have the acquaintance of such a man. Such teaching is responsible for our measurable distance to revolu- tionary anarchy. To be fair all round I will give another extract this time from Father Furniss' tracts for children. "Come into this room, you see it is very small. But see, in the midst of it there is a girl, perhaps, about eighteen years old. What a terrible dress she has on—her dress is made of fire. On her head she wears a bonnet of fire. It is pressed down close all over head, it burns into the skin, it scorches the bone of the skull and makes it smoke."</p>
        <p>Two of these preachers are looked up to with great respect and yet who among us can look upon them of anything else but arrant liars, and ignorant babblers of things they knew nothing of. With all Oils I should have no concern were it not thee! am quite sure that men and women trained up under such horrid influences can never have healthy brains, and are never likely to grasp the burning questions of the day. I hope to be one of the last to interfere with any man's religion, but when lean see, as I certainly do, that religion, or creeds and sectarianism block the way, why then I say, root it up, and "crush the wretch." I might fairly leave this part of the subj,-ct alone, because I have received so much true loving kindness from men who are proud to be known as Christians, but duty calls every man to the front, and as I think this false tinsel thing which men miscall religion is the chief offender in depraving men's minds and ren- dering them unfit for the great responsibilities of life, why I say again, "root it up." 807 Page-st , San Francisco. (To be Continue d.)</p>
        <p>O'Donovan Hosea, lecturing on Sunday afternoon to a crowded meeting. of Irishmen in Manchester said he be- lieved Irishmen were able to gain their independence in spite of Englishmen. He did not want blood and thunder murder ; all he wanted was his rights, and he would have them from those who robbed him of them.</p>
        <p>At Clonwl on Saturday all the prisoners in the " witch-I4urning" case were returned for trial on the capital charge.</p>
      </div>
