      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>,111</head>
        <p>,111</p>
        <p>TOW:, COUNCIL NOTES.</p>
        <p>The proceedings at the Warrington Town Council on Tuesday evening last week were of a severely business character, with a dis- tinct bias towards finance. The Committees had all prepared their estimates of ex- penditure for the new financial year, and the several chairmen seemed to feel under an obliga- tion to explain more or less clearly how it come about that the respective sums specified were re- quired. Judging by these statements all the Com- mittees of the Corporation are models of economic administration. It is true that, in some cases the demands upon the public purse were larger than in previous years. but this was of course always due to causes over which the Committees had no control.</p>
        <p>The first matter on th 3 agenda was, however, a reference to the death of Mr. Jo:epla Davies, whose long service in the Council and in connection with public affairs generally was alluded to by the Mayor in brief but suitable terms. Councillor Shaw Green seconded the resolution of condolence with the widow and family,and spoke of the well-known and genial "Alderman "from a life-long acquaint- quaintance, having, as he incidentaiiy men- tioned, followed him as a youth into an office in which he had spent an earlier part of his career. Councillor Bennett and Alderman Sutton also spoke from a pretty extensive and complete private and public knowledge of the deceabed gentleman, and the resolution was unanimously passed.</p>
        <p>The minutes of the Committees were then fairly entered upon and for a time were despatched with unusual promptitude. The minutes of the Street Improvement Committee showed that plans of no 11 fewer than forty-five new houses—to say nothing of a circus—had been passed, and from this circum- stance Alderman Sutton, the chairman, very legi- timately drew the conclusion that Warrington was not " standing still" although trade was said to be bad. In moving the minutes of the Health Committee, the same Alderman,—who is, by the way, generally pretty much in evidence at Council meetings—alluded to the Medical officers report and to the satisfactory death-rate—the "lowest of the century." Council Bennett admitted that the report showed that something had been done, especially in the way of the demolition of in- sanitary property, but still, he argued, there was much to be done. What about the property that was still allowed to remain to contaminate the atmosphere of the town ? and as to the public abat- toirs, what was being done with regard to that? On the subject of the emission of black smoke he pointed out that only nine observations had been made during the year, and this he thought was utterly inadequate, considering the amount of smoke that generally overhangs the town. And then there was the ventilation of the sewers, a subject which he had previously raised in that Council. An:admirable list of reforms eurely,but it must be admitted that the Council did not mani- fest any reciprocal enthusiasm for this catalogue of improvements. Councillor R. T. Fairclough next Complained that the medical officer's report really contained too many facts—or, rather, too many figures. It was impossible, he said to digest all this mass of statistics, and he suggested that a few charts would be more edifying and instructive. In replying to these critics Alderman Sutton dis- played his usual sang froid. He asseverated that Councillor Bennett was saddling upon the Health Committee the supposed shortcomings of something like half-a-dozen committees. As for Councillor Fairclough's criticisms, how was a poor committee to please all these captious councillors? If they gave a lot of figures it was complained that they were giving too many, but if they left any of them out then it was asserted that they did not give enough. "I complain," retorted Councillor Fair- clough, "that we cannot understand what is given." " Ali, well, but you see," replied the alder- man in hissuavest tones, " we cannot help that." At which there was much laughter, and it was ad- mitted that the chairman of the committee had scored.</p>
        <p>After this there was another rapid passage of minutes. In connection with the Sanitary Works Committee, Councillor Monks said the committee had, leaving out of account certain exceptional ex- penditure, really spent during the past year less than itsl estimates by between £4OO and £5OO, a result, he hinted, previously unknown in the history of the committee. On the Gas Committee minutes it was pointed out that a rebate had been allowed the Street Improvement Committee amounting to £656, which, together with another sum of £4BO remitted in connection with the light- ing of public lamps, amounted to £1,136, and this, it appeared, was the benefit the town had derived from the gas works during the year. But human nature is ungrateful. Even this propitiatory offering did not save the committee from open allegations and ungenerous inuendos. Alderman Bolton cast doubts upon the official notification that the gas averaged an illuminating power of 1889 candles, and asserted that on one occasion he had " to light a candle to see the gas." Then Councillor Woods, speaking as a churchwarden, declared that there had been a lack of light—gas-light, of course—in the Parish Church, of all places, and on a Sunday night ; and what was worse, Councillor R. T. Fair- dough, another churchwarden and chairman of the Gas Committee, had not been there to see it. But the Gas Committee is hardened, and criticism falls away from it like water from a duck's back. It is always being girded at, but it takes little heed of its critics.</p>
        <p>On the Rivers Committee's minutes, Councillor Roberts stated in a few weighty and deliberate words that he was not prepared at that moment to give any information with regard to the recent conferences with the Ship Canal Com- pany, and with regard to the estimate of expendi- ture by the Committee he asked the Council to vote it with the assurance would use the money for the best advantage of the town. The last business of the Council, but certainly not the least, was the financial statement made by Councillor Shaw Green, the Chairman of the Finance and Estate Committee, a report of which will be found in another column. It is sufficient to say here that Councillor Green was able to make the gratifying announcement that the rates for the present year will be 8,1. in the pound less than they were last year, and that,speaking with regard to the finances, he looks forward hopefully to the future. This, in view of all the projected improvements, is some- thing to be thankful for.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>STACK FIRE AT ARTLEY.</head>
        <p>THE STACK FIRE AT ARTLEY.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Court on Thursday, last week before his Honour Judge Wynne Ffoulkes and a jury, the case in which Joseph Lawrenson, Old-road, Latchford, sued William Griffith. contractor of the Ship Canal at Arpley Meadows, for £25 compensation for the loss of a stack of straw which was burnt down on December 22nd, it being alleged by the plaintiff to have been due to the sparks emitted from an engine belonging to the Ship Canal, was resumed. Mr. Arthur Browne appeared for the plaintiff, and Mr. Collingwood Hope for the defend- ' ant. -Three witnesses were called for the defence one of whom, a surveyor, proved that the gradient of the tip bank past the stack was one in 300, and another witness said that the Cadoaton and the North wich engines were 330 horse power, and with only seven waggons behind would not emitsparks.—Cross-examined by Mr. Browne, he admitted that if the rails were greasy the engine might emit sparks, but it was not probable. Mr. Collingwood Hope for the defence said that the plaintiff's case was only one of suspicion. No one had been called who saw the stack set on fire, the witnesses merely saying that they saw sparks coming from the funnel of the engiue and going in the direction and past the stacks. The onus probandi lay upon the plaintiff, and it was not for the defence to prove that the stack was not set on fire by the sparks from the engine. Mr, Browne contended that the case was practically undefended inasmuch as the whole of the evidence called by the defendant came from the mouths of defendant's own employees, with the exception of one who proved nothim; material. On the other hand, the two witnesses Robinson, and Johnsen. called by the plaintiff were ab- solutely independent,and they spoke to seeing the sparks going in the direction of the stack. Further, when plaintiff mentioned the matter to Mrs. Griffiths, that gentleman told him that the Ship Canal Company were responsible and sent him on a wild goose chase to Man- chester about it. That admission of Mr. Griffiths had been absolutely uncontradicted,as'Mr.Griffiths had never been put into the box. He therefore asked for a verdict of £25 for the plaintiff. His Honour in a lengthy summing up, said that the jury would have to find whether the stack was set on fire by the sparks from the engine, then whether it was through the negligence of Mr. Griffiths, and they would also have to find, if so, w hether there was any contri- butary negligence on the part of the plaintiff. He might say that he did not think there was any .contributary neglioenee, as the stack was in plaintiff's own field, and he had.a right to put it where he liked.</p>
        <p>, The jury retired and after being out for an hour, they returned into the court, and in reply to the usual ques- tions, the foreman said that they thought that they were not justified by the evidence adduced in finding that the stack was ser on fire by the sparks from the defend- ant's engine.</p>
        <p>_ His Honour : That means a verdict for the defend- ant?</p>
        <p>The Foremen : Yes, practically.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>MAID AND THE MILL.</head>
        <p>THE MAID AND THE MILL.</p>
        <p>INTERESrING AFFAIR AT BRADFORD.</p>
        <p>THE "Bradford Argus" describes an interesting occur- rence in the populous district surrounding the vast mills of Messrs. Lister and Co., at Manningham. Alice Monk- man, who was employed at this great productive em- porium, is the youngest but one of the daughters of a widow, Mrs. Mary Monkman, 49. Springeliffe-street, Bradford, within a gunshot of the huge mills which Lord Masham has erected. The " Bradford Daily Argus " interviewed mother and daughter, and they told a re- markable story. Alice was a winder at Lord Mastiam's' mill. Last June she had a fainting fit in the mill, which caused her to fall on the end of a machine. She was carried home,and a skilful doctor called in ; but though he attended her for nearly seven months could do noth- ing. Asked the reason for this, Mrs. Monkham replied, ' in homely fashion : Well, ever after the fall she was subject to fits ; some said they were epileptic and some hysterical. We had the doctor, but it was not much good. Then we got her to the Bradford Children's Hospital, but still she had these fits, and sometimes when she was in convul- sions she was really terrible. The doctor thought they were simply hysterical fits, and one would come perhaps about every three weeks. Anyhow she did not get any better, and then a lady took her, and she was only there a week when she had another fit and came home." Mrs. Monkman added : " She was never what you might have called a right strong girl. She is nothing ex- tra now, but she has never had any of these fits since. I was telling a visitor how ill Alice was. That I think would be about a week after Christmas. He asked at once whether we had tried Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People, saying that he knew some people who had been greatly benefited by them, and that he himself would not have been able to do his work if he had not had them. I therefore pro- cured a box, and before they were gone they seemed to have done Alice good. I got, therefore, , another, and altogether, we had four boxes. She has ' not had another fit since. She has gone back to her old place, she is stronger in every way, and if they had done her nothing else they have given her a good appetite,and that is what lam pleased at." There is nothing much further to record, except that the elder sisters present endorsed what is written ; were emphatic that Dr. Williams' Pink Pills had restored their sister to active working life ; and were willing that the statement should be published in the " Bradford Daily Argus,"but " did not want too much making of it." With the assur- ance that the" Argus " man would only produce the plain statements, he withdrew, after a pleasant and courteous interview with this respected and intelligent</p>
        <p>These, then, are the simple facts of the case. Similar remarkible cures have been reported by representatives of all the greatest newspapers in this country, showing the extraordinary interest aroused by the introduction and impartial trial of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People, which are now recognised as one of the greatest modern medicines, a perfect blood and nerve restorer, and an unfailing cure for influenza, rheumatism, sciatica, neuralgia, partial paralysis, locometor ataxy, St. Vitus' dance, nervous headache, prostration, &amp;c. Dr. Williams' Pink Pills are not a purgative, but a tonic and strengthening cotnpousd. They restore pale and sallow complexions to the glow of health, and are a specifie for all troubles peculiar to the female sax ; while in the cases of men they effect a radical cure in all cases arising from mental worry, overwork, or excesses of whatever nature.</p>
        <p>The Pills are sold by all chemists, or may be had direct from Dr. Williams' Medicine Company, of 46, Holborn-viaduct, London, at 2s. 9d. a box, or six boxes for 13s. 9d. Dr. Willimas' Pink Pills are never sold loose ; the wooden box must be in a pink wrappe bearing the full name, Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>BLOODHOUNDS AS DETECTIVES.</head>
        <p>BLOODHOUNDS AS DETECTIVES.</p>
        <p>The " Windsor Magazine" for April contains a very striking article on this question, in the course of which the writer, Mr. A. Croxton Smith, reminds us " six or seven years ago, when the scare occasioned by the terrible •Whitechapel murders was at its height, the mystic word 'bloodhound' was mooted with bated breath, many feeling that here was an agent that might succeed in detecting the criminal where all the skiled upholders of the law had failed, Others, who knew little about the subject, pooh-poohed the idea, characterising the modern representative of St. Hobert's breed as nothing but a magnificent show animal, a brute minus all those special faculties for which it was erstwhile famous. It is not difficult, however, to demonstrate the absurdity of this contention. The bloodhound is as capable of running anyone down to-day as ever he was, his scent- ing powers having in no way deteriorated, despite what detractors of the patrician of the show bench may have to say to the country. In plenty of cases, I am con- vinoed, bloodhounds would prove invaluable auxiliaries to the police in tracking crimin,ls, especially in rural districts where the scent would lie well. Mr. Edwin Brough, our greatest living authority on the breed, has proved this beyond refutation, inost of his hounds being trained to hunt the clean boot with ease. Moreover, it is not long since they were instrumental in detecting poachers and similar wild fowl.' Forty or fifty years ago there were bloodhounds that could hunt the scent of a man twenty-four, or even thirty-six, hours old, and since then breeders have vastly improved the charac- teristics which denote scent, the long, narrow, peaked head and immense flews always associated with this faculty having been developed to an extent never known I before. Mr. Brough has letters from two men having charge of hounds attached to penitentiaries in Texas, containing wonderful accounts of the capture of convicts, although the rogues had in some instances a start of a day and a half. In one case a man was run down forty miles from home." " When we come to speak of the use of bloodhounds in town, we are treading on more delicate ground, several difficulties at once confronting us. It may seem strange, but it is none the less true, that we have to deal with rather nervous animals, but this defect could probably be overcome if only those of b-lder disposition were selected and accustomed from earliest puppyhood to the sights and sounds incidental to town life. A more seri- ous obstacle would be experienced in finding the line of the right man, half-a-dozen people possibly having gone away in the same direction. Mr. Brough believes that in time a hound could be taught to follow a foot-scent on stone, but the difficulty I have referred to would mast likely render his services abortive unless the crime were committed during the quiet hours of the night,and he were brought to the spot promptly. When the Whitechapel murders were about, Mr. Brough,at the in- ‘vitation of Sir Charles Warren, sent his champion Baraby,' and ' Burg Ito 'to Lopdon,and successful trials were carried on in Hyde Park, but soon after the crowded streets were reached the couple became at fault. No opportunity of using them in the East End was afforded, the fiendish ghoul having ceased his prac- tices during the eight or nine weeks they were in town."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>HOW TO TRAIN BLOODHOUNDS.</head>
        <p>HOW TO TRAIN BLOODHOUNDS.</p>
        <p>" Mr. Brough advises that puppies should be entered early to the work, beginning first lessons when only three or four months old, and in no case should they be allowed to hunt anything but the clean boot. He sug- gests that the runner should caress and make much of the pups. Let them see him start away and then he should get out of sight as quickly as possible and run a few hundred yards up wind on grass land in a'straight line. The man who hunts the puppies should take them over the exact line encouraging them in their work until they come up to the runner, who should reward them with pieces of meat. A prettier sight cannot be imagined than seeing two or three couples of hounds accurately following the trail and giving tongue to the loveliest music as they hunt. I larlieve at one time Mr. Brough found some of his hounds running mute, but the introduction of a bassett soon effeccted a change. Very little practice teaches the hounds to try back themselves if they overrun the line, and so keen are their noses that on a good sconting day some in- mates of the VVyndvate kennels have been seen running hard fifty yards or more to leeward of the line taken. Not long ago prizes were given at Warwick for blood- honnd trails, and one wishes they had been continued in order to encourage proprietors of hounds to train them. This is a point that might with advantage be brought under the consideration of the Kennel Club, for if a stimulous were given to private persons to use their hounds for the purposes intended by nature, the inactivity of the police would be still more inexcusable." See " The Bloodhound as a Detective," by A. Croxton Smith, in the "Windsor Magazine " for April.</p>
