      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <p>DEBATE ON VACCINATION. LIVELY MEETING AT PENBETH. Four weeks ago a paper on " Compulsory Vaccina- tion " was read at the meeting of the Penketh Wesleyan Mutual Improvt merit Society. The subject proved of such absorbing interest that a special meeting of the society was called to further debate toe question, and arrangements were made to secure the attendance of re- presentative gentlemen, viz., air. Walter Millington (of the Warrington and District Anti-Compulsory Vaccina- tion Society), and Dr. J. Gue.it Guinan (assistant medi- cal officer of health for the borou;11 of Warrington). The meeting was held in the Wesleyan school,Penketh, on Wednesday evening, and proved higbly interesting. Dr. Murray, of Penketh. occupied the chair, and there were also present in addition to the debaters—Mr. and Mrs. R. Garnett, J. P., Mr and Mrs. C. E. Parker, Mr. John Peers, Mr. John S. Green, and a full attendance of members.</p>
        <p>Mr. Walter Millington opened the debate on the nega- tive side,witl, a paper on the "Elum bug of Vaccination." He said that in this best of all possible worlds this most enlightened of centuries,this freest of countries,the wor- shipper at the shrine of history will in the future discover with a shudder of dismay the most unwarrantable inter- ference with personal liberty which has ever been re- corded by the muse. After referring to the struggles for liberty, he said the strength of England's character had for years lain in the sturdy Nonconformists, and vet it was so necessary to appeal to these very dissenters who laugh to scorn the plea for establishment of religious dogmas, that they should he consistent and make the same demand for disestahlishment of this gross supersti- tious humbug of a medical dogma called vaccination. Britain to-day pays for the practice and propagation of . a medical religion with the observances -if which every father is forced to comply, and which, beyond any shadow of a doubt, a vast mejority of Englishmen would prefer to see effaced from the statute book of the realm. They might enquire why this ceremony was enforced by law with some reason. He proceeded to show that the practice was first prominently promulgated by one Edward Jenner and he asked would it be believed that the wonderful discevery, the result, according to Bacon, of 30 years' painstaking inquiry and experiment—the masterpiece of the ind active philosophy—was proposed by Jenne e on the strength of eine dubieus experiment ! After dealing at some length with the history of vacci- nation, Mr.'Milliugton said it was believed in some fashion that vaccination (a) prevented smallpox, (b) temporarily prevented it, or (co mitigated the disease caught. Mr. Millington then traversed these three con- tentions and in an able manner contended that there could be no shadow of doubt that smallpox after vacci- nation was both abundant and world wide. The Jem- nerians' first line' of defence was broken down. Did it prevent smallpox for a time P Well let him know what time. He could produce evidence of experts that vacci- nation protected for the whole life, for 14 16 years, for , 10 years for seven years, for one year, scarcely at all, and not at all. Let the doctors define the period, and hard facts shall disprove it. Sheffield gave cases for every period from 14 days tolo years. Warrington pro- vided half-a-dozen cases after re-vaccination,lo 16 days, and one death, vaccinated 10 days before rash. The speaker quoted the testimony of several doctors of cases within 10 years, and said if these 6.000 failures of vaccination to protect children up to 10 year!, failed to convince, nothing less than a sledge hammer would, and logic had lost its use. But though the second line of defence had followed the first, vacciniets were secure—their third line was impregn- able. Vaccination would ameliorate the disease. If they were vaccinated they would have a milder smallpox than they would have unvaccinated. He wondered whether doctors thought we were really mostly fools. They must wear their tongues in their cheeks when malt leg this claim. How did they know what kind of small pox the vaccinated would have had,if they had remained unvaccinated P There was mild, very mild smallpox before vaccination. le went on to show that 18 persona , out of every 100 who had smallpox before vaccination came into vogue died, and he asked had vaccination tempered the severity of thq disease P He gave statistics to show that smallpox was over the whole number of cases just as severe as it always was,killing 1 in 6 of the afflicted. But hospital returns, whiefi we owe to persons interested in maintaining vaccination, gave death rates for the unvaccinated from 25 to 83 percent. As 18 per cent. of the total cases died while one was unvaccinated I only 18 per cent, of the unvaccinated wotrel die now by smallpox. Who were the doctors prepared to charge with killing the reineining 7 to 65 percent. P Without direct evidence, the conclusion was inevit- able that the returns, were cooked. Consciously or unconsciously, the vaccinated deaths were transferred to the unvaccinated column. If then the death rate on large aggregates now was as heavy as ever, vaccination certainly had not mitigated smallpox. He thought he had demonstrated the humbug of vaccination. Its more serious side they could not stay to touch. Suffice it to say there was no longer any doubt of the invaccination of certain inoculable diseases. There was no known test for the purity of lymph, which had its genesis in a score of filthy sources. No medical man could guarantee that the lymph might not cause syphilis, leprosy, erysipelas, jaundice, glanders, tuberculosis, cancer, lockjaw. In conclusion the speaker maintained that vaccination neither prevented nor ameliorated smallpox, but sowed the seeds of numerous foul loathsome diseases, the full effects of which, as yet, no man could guage. (Applause.) Dr. J. Guest Gornall, who took the affirmative side, was greeted with applause. He said it was impossible for medical men to speak with anything like the confid- ence of anti-vaccinators (hear, hear) as the doctors knew too much of the subject to adopt the tone of assurance that people ignorant of the question of vaccination and its beneficial effects assumed. What was vaccine virus? No one knew—(hear, bear)—but he believed that we were on the eve of a grand scientific discovery. But it did not follow that because WP did not understand a thing we should not use it, when its advantages were so !palpable. People used many things they did not under- stand, and it was no argument that they must not Use vaccine virus because they did not know what it was. He had recently had to deal with a severe epidemic of smallpox, and from that experience he maintained that vaccination did tend to prevent stuallpox,that it did tend to mitigate smallpox (as any well vaccinated man was net so liable to have smallpox) and,in the third place, he maintained that vaccination did tend to prevent death. Vaccinatien had saved more lives than any discovery ever made. He referred to instances in which vaccina- tion had, undoubtedly, not oniy saved werkpeople from smallpox, but prevented the other attendant evils —the destitution and starvation of their families. Their experience among reveccinated workpeonle was borne out by what they had seen among co.: tin sseS of the population, and among people specially exposed to infection in one way or another, Thee, tit tee troops at Orford Barracks, all of whom were ex- amined for satisfactory marks, no cases oecurred in a total of 824 persons. N. case occurred among 117 re. vaccinated persons in the Postal service; no case occurred in the police force,whoee members were all revaccinated and the same tnieht be said of the hospital staff. Having referred to the 34 deaths in the vaccinated claps aid the 26 in the unvaccinated class, the doctor proceeded to quote from his report dee the epidemic of smallpox in the years 1892-3 with a view to proving the mitigation theory, and he maintained that his experience amply justified all that was claimed for vaccination. He Was perfectly familiar with all the stock arguments of the auti-vaccinators. There was the anti-vaccinator who was opposed to all compulsion,and who never gave a thought to his duty to his fellow-townsmen ; and there was the sentimental anti-vaccinator who objected to having any animal matter put into his system. He evert °ejected to such animal matter as beef, for it was invariably found that anti-vaccinators were vegetarians. (Laughter.) Well now, he put that to them, were they going to sacrifice their children by following the advice of ignorant anti-vacc nators, who knew nothing from practical experience, or were they willing to accept the statements of people who had to deal with this question as part of their professional day? To his mind one ounce of practice was worth a ton of theory. If they were still unconvinced, he had several pictures to show them which he thought would effect their conversion. These pictures were obtained at the hospital and lithe anti-vaccinators had had them, they would have highly coloured them to delude an ignor- ant audience. He, however, would show them in their unadorned condition, and leave them to speak for them- selves. The lanternist Mr. P. C. Garnett then threw upon the screen the photo of a girl showing a successful vaccination—viz., the four marks of regulation size as prescribed by the Local Government Beard. The next ex- hibited was of a woman suffering from confluent small- pox. She had one vaccination mark, and recovered. Then followed a photo of a man, which wes envaccin- ated and died from confluent smallpox. The 4th view. showed a boy suffering from smallpox and by the bed- side a girl who, to the horror of the medical staff, was found to be free from smallpox having been removed in error from the fever hospital. She was at once vaccin- ated and sat on the bedside to show that she was prod against the contagion. Mr John G.Green also addressed the meeting at some length, declaring that one 'could rise from the study of Dr. Crieghton's book on " Jenner and Vaccination" without feeling convinced that vaccination as a protec- tive of small-pox was the greatest fraud ever perpetrated upon a credulous people. Whilst it had failed to achieve its object, vaccination hail succeeded in doubling the mortality frcm synhilia, cancer, wasting diseases, eloud poisoning, and skin diseases, and if they wished to re- verse this mortality they must abandon the practise of vaccination, and seek their immunity from small-pox, in temperate and cleanly habits. (Applause.) Mr. Millington having replied, Mr. Robert Garnett said that notwithstanding the arguments and statistics Mr. Millington had eddue.d, be inclined to the stnewit oft)-. Gornall. - . .</p>
        <p>Mr. John Peers ros, to Pinpo;e aVON,ni th.nks to speakers. As On, who was "n the t. Th;,..,,• said thnv had been highly interosted and .InAlunt..l the detiate, S ,me of the ar:2EIMe-PtS had tl-4-:: thin.and he ttemght one M. them was that with refel e to animal matter. He should like to aak Dr. whether they would take putrid bee f Mr. C. E. Parker cordially seconded the proposition, which was heartily agreed to, and the various s;-..eakera aving replied, 4 most interesting meeting concluded at is o'clock,</p>
