        <p>THE "Red Van" report for 1694 Rural England. of the Land Restoration LealVet with a copy of which sve been faveured, is on the whole an interestingi"—,, publication. It throws a good deal of light le', rural life in the more southerly counties England. The "red vans," it should be explained; are utilised by the League for the purpose spending the doctrines of land nationalizsti(* They are each "manned," so to speak, with a lee-r turer and a driver, and all through the months of the year they travel from village village, the lecturer discoursing wherever an I whenever an opportunity occurs upon the Psi*, of" restitution," as it is called, and endeavournle to make a still deeper impression upon the lab°L/Dr: era by the dissemination of suitable literature° the formation of some permanent form of orgsir isation. Without the vans, indeed, the Leg° would be very much crippled in its opersci tions. It is needless to say that its ereBa of laud nationalisation is not regard° with favour by the " powers that be he in country districts, and that no facilities fa giving of lectures or the accommodation of th. lecturers could be expected. But with a vvel,l; : stocked vehicle the representative, of the Leagi.i are comparatively independent of the lords the manor and hostile rectors and vicars, alouge'd as it is the reception that is occasionally accordre thorn is rather too warm, to he pleasant. told that at one meeting in Wilts a well-knel local Tory rode into a meeting of labourers aline began laying about him with a long whiP• was subsequently summoned before the Jeci. Bench for cutting a labourer named W1U11: under the eye with the whip, but the illagistr,,ae: seemed to be mere in sympathy with the toes"— e than the assailed, and only imposed a fine of shilling, leaving the aggrieved labourer to PAY own costs. On the other hand,a lecturer was ill;ne ten shillings for holding a meetin, at the junet.-11 acCOl3l- - two roads after being refused modation at the local inn. But, .in °Pr of little impediments of this kind, the L°3ga-9 reports progress. It began its erasade in I,vuie with one van—an old parcels posts van---bll%- 1803 there were five vans in the field. Suce-5,. has, as usual, been followed by imitation, rather in this case, by partial imitation. ' Liberty and Property Defence League seat °lli two lecturers to counteract the ()darts of the rla van orators, but instead of placing the° le separate vans they were given a tandem tricycof to ride upon. For the success of this wo plo, „o_ campaign perfect harmony was, of course, between the two defenders of "Liberty and Pcfls. perty," but unfortunately they appear to have agreed. They were evidently in too close °ger munion with each other on a " tricycle wade two," and their partnership was suddenly 6110„ solved and the lecturing tour brought to an .tlet timely end. Turning, however, to the anbie,„, matter of the "Red Van" report, it eeecb clear that there was groat need for P6r,.1.0e Councils in some of the southern ecia/ of England, and that there is ample se-i.,110 re for their reforming zeal now that hi ogrio established. The sanitary condition ot sd, it of tile villages is deplorable in the extrema), au io is said, opens up one of tho darkest chapters is the dark history of rural landlordism." Itdoubt, further alleged that in many parishes, it is ful whether the village slums are not even iaots worse condition to-day than when Cl-it,3l KINt;SLEY sang of "The Poacher's Widow, gl are .00. 1849. Some suggestive illustrations One of these is a view of a landlord's house,l 0 stately and substantial stone structure of anTrk dimensions, "surrounded by a well-timbecasea"red ft.„ ~„ inn which deer roam by lake and On the opposite page are given of the exterior and the interior of a labouorr cottage on the Izanio estate, and the trast is, to say ti,e least, very photograrp;</p>
