        <p>And these illustrations, it is alleged, were not' selected because the landlord in question is worse than other landlords who might be mentioned, but because the cottage happened to be available for illustration. On other subjects, Bgoll as the assessment of land and other I):°PertY) and •the provision of allotments :°aae interesting infor:nation is also given. '. ', att?downers have generally taken care that ! . oheir mansions and pleasure grounds are not , ver-aFsessed for rating purposes, and some re- 4nlarkable figures have been occasionally published -li these subjects. In the report under consider- ation, one instance is given of a parish in Wilt- , Imre which contains the residence and large deer ' alatiz of the local landowner, together with the re- " Ici,ence of the vicar and other substantial houses, 11 7.'4 Yet is only assessed at the rate of fourteen .4411liags and fourpence per acre, while the neigh- ' ili which is without mansions has an 'average assessment . shillings and sixpence per acre. ,cies of this stamp should certainly Closest scrutiny of the local authorities now scattered all over the country. . to allotments some curious rules are lich we venture to think will not be ' those Parish Councils which avail of their power in this respect under the rnment Act. Oao very common con- prohibit the holder from using any lent than a spade in the cultivation land ; ho must not use a horse or the penalty of immediate eviction .ation. Then, again, the tenant work at all ori his allotment on a r between six a.m, and six p.m. on Without the consent of the person he is employed. The condition iich the allotment holder in a of Warwickshire was to at- regularly under penalty of losing lot and crops has now, it is said, been . the Duke of BEDFORD does specify occupiers " will be expected to con- Ives with sobriety at all times, and to '' families in a decent and orderly</p>
