        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>WESTON POOR RATE.</p>
        <p>Messrs. J. firundtit and T. Handley have affixed their signatures tbe a new poor rate of is. 4d. in the £ for the t.wnship of Weston. The rate is estimated to produce £BO7 19s.</p>
        <p>THB SCHOOLS' QUESTION AT RUNCORN. To-day (Thursday) a special meeting of the members of the Runcorn School Board will be held for the purpose of conferring with Mr. Kynnersley, her Majesty's In- spector of schools, with regard to the accommodation which will be necessary in consequence of the closing of the Wesleyan Day Schools after the forthcoming examination. THE RUNCORN SHIP CANAL AGREEMENT.</p>
        <p>Mr. Lake, law clerk to the Runcorn Urban Council, has received from the solicitors to the Manchester Ship Canal Company a written agreement which confirms the arrangement come to between to two bodies with respect to additional lay-by accommodation and other shipping facilities in connection with the Ship Canal at Run- corn.</p>
        <p>Sr. revues BIBLE CLASS, RUNCORN. A meeting of this class was held on Sinday under the presidency of Mr. B. Kniveton. In addition to giving the bible lesson, Mr. Kuiveton delivered a short and in- teresting address on the " Sufferings of Christ." The nsusical portion of the service was enhanced by a solo given by Mr. B. Crosby, entitled" What Christ has done tor me.' The organist, Mr. G. Rowley, performed an organ solo. THE MYSTERIOUS LOSS OF SHEEP AT ASTON.</p>
        <p>After inVe-s-t-fgaii-m, the disease from which so many sheep died at the farm of Messrs. John and George Percival, Parkside farm, Aston, near War- rington, him been pronounced as anthrax. Nearly one hundred sheep have died from the disease. In order to stamp the disease out the farm buildings have been thoroughly disinfected. Sr. JOHN'S PRESBYTERIAN CHURCH, RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday evening, a meeting of this church was held for the purpose of selecting three additional dea- cons. The Rev. J. Williams, of Chester, and Mr. Jones were the delegates appointed by the Pi eshytery to take the votes of the members. During the evening, remarks were made by the delegates and the pastor (Rev. J Pulford Williams). The three additional deacons elected were : Messrs. J. E. Warburton, James Richardson, and James Steele. These will act co-jointly with the present deacons :—Messrs. R. Baxter and T. Hewitt. BRUNSWICK WESLEYAN SUNDAY SCHOOL, RUNCORN. In this school on Sunday, an interim Foreign Mission- ary meeting was held undor the presidency of Mr. George H. Kirkham. Mr. J. Unsworth read the report for the past five months, and Mr. J. Leach, kin., gave a short address, and appealed for the amount for the year to be made up to £lOO. He thought this could he done if more attention to details in collection was given. The Rev. W. G..orge afterwards delivered an interesting address. The sum collected by the scholars during six five months amount to over £34. The collection at the close realised £6, making a total of £4O, or an increase on the amount raised in the corresponding period last year. A RUNCORN MERCHANT'S WILL.</p>
        <p>Probst of—the-WllK—Sat';;(37tObiW 14th, 1892, of Mr. William Hayes, of Ashton Hayes, Chester, who carried on business as a merchant at Runcorn, and wir) died on the 71.11 August last, leaving personalty of the net value of £71,440 9+. 7d., has been granted to his daughter, Miss Annie Hayes, of Ashton Hayes. The testator charges his estate with the payment of a life annuity of £5OO to his friend, Mr. George Skey, of Wyborne Lodge, Milvern, and he leaves certain land at Frodsham upon trusts for his nephew, John Higssn Hayes. All the re- sidue of his property Mr. Hayes leaves to his daughter, Miss Ann Hayes. ANOTHER RUNCORN MAN DROWNED.</p>
        <p>On Friday night a telegram was received by the Run- corn police announcing that John Simpson, of Wood's Cottages, Flalton-road, Runcorn, had been drowned at King's Lynn. The deceased was 39 years of age, and was an unmarried man. The news was at once con- veyed to the deceased's mother, who is over 70 years of age; and the intelligence was the more painful to her, in that she had just received a letter from the deceased, enclosing a postal order, and stating that he would be home in a week. It appears that the deceasedewas em- ployed by a sub-contractor to Mr. Kirkham, contractor, Runcorn, who had a contract in hand for same work at King's Lynn. The deceased entered upon the work on New Year's day. An inquest was held on the body on Saturday morning. THE DROWNING OF A RUNCORN MAN AT EASTHAM.</p>
        <p>Mr. Churton, coroner. held an inquest at the Eistham Dote', Eastham, on Monday morning, relative to the death of James Gregory Marsh, 25 years of age, of Mount Pleasant, Runcorn. The evidence given was to the effect that whilst the deceased was following his employment in constructing some dolphins by which to moor vessels in the Ship Canal at Easthamon Thursday, he overbalance/A himself and fell into the Mersey.. Efforts to restore him were of no avail. The body was found at two o'clock on Friday afternoon. A verdict of "Accidentally Drowned" was recorded. The body was subsequently conveyed to Runcorn on a packet,and was interred in Runcorn Cemetery on Tuesday:,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>RUNCORN VOLUNTERR- SHOOTING CLUB.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN VOLUNTERR- SHOOTING CLUB.</p>
        <p>On Saturday,the second round in the gold medal com- petition took place at Runcorn Heath. The light con- tinmed good throughout, but a gale of wind from the left front, varying very considerably in strength, and very cold withal, had a bad effect on the totals made by the thirteen competitors. The following are the scores made at the distances named, with sevcn rounds at each -</p>
        <p>220 500</p>
        <p>60) To-</p>
        <p>Rank and Names. yds. yds. yds. tal. Sergeant W. D. Harvey 3l ... 26 ... 28 ... 85 Sergeant D. Edwards Private J. Houghton Sergeant J. A. Rushton Colour-Sergeant J. Mort 3O ... 19 ... 23 ... 72 Private S. Joinson Lance-Corporal R. Foulkes Private J. Edward&amp; 26 ... 25 ... 16 ... 67 Sergeant G. Joynson Band-Sergeant J. Gittens 3O ... 14 ... 15 ... 59 Private S. Stubbs 24 ... 19 ... 13 ... 56 Sergeant 3W. Woodward 3O ... 16 absent 46 Bugler J. Mort ANOTHER RIINCORNIAN DROWNED.</p>
        <p>Shortly after eleven o'clock on Tuesday morning, Her- bert G. lledgecock, 21 years of age, of 46. (mell-lane, Runcorn, was found drowned in Garston D,-,ck. The deceased was engaged on the flat Tagus, bid inging to the Ship Canal Company, which on Saturday, March 30,was lying at the docks,Garston. He was missed on the date named and dragging operations were eominenced. Oil Tuesday morning, Mr. William Shaw, missionary, Seamen's Institute,Rancorn,was successful, by means of his dragging apparatus, in finding the body. An inquest was held the same day at the Mersey flottl, Garston Deck, and a verdict of " Found Drowned" was re- turned. The Coroner complimented Mr. Shaw upon his excellent apparatus used in grappling, by which means lie had been able the previous day, to find another body that of a Norwegian sailor, who had been missing three weeks. He suggested that similar apparatas for dragg- ing should be kept by the Company. Mr. Shaw was assisted in his endeavours, by Mr. Ellison, stepfather to the deceased, and several fiatmen. BETHIsSDA LITERARY AND SOCIAL CLASS, RUNCORN. Mr. William Handley presided at a moat interesting entertainment given in cminection with the above men- tioned class on Tuesday evening. There was a good . attendance. A programme of vocal and instrumental music engaged toe attention of the audience during the first portion of the evening. The vocalists were Miss Heatherington, Messrs. J. Nickson, E. Worrall and El. Mason, and all sang in such good style as to receive hearty receptions. The coster songs given by Mr. Mason were very popular with the audience. Miss Forester performed a pianobrte solo, and a recitation " The fireman's wedding " was contributed be Miss A. Rogers. The entertainment concluded with a mock election of M.P. for the borongh of Rocksavage which was taken part in by members of Brunswick-st. Wesleyan Mutual Improvement Society. The role was as follows :—Mayor, Mr. J. D. Jones; Borough Clerk, Mr. F. Ellison ; Conservative Candidate (Hon. Timothy Wagstaffe), Mr. W. Milling- ton; Liberal Candidate (Paul Slapdash, Esq.), Mr. J. H. Darlington; Bolus Soapwall, Mr. G. Hayes; Peter Blunt. Mr. T. Saunders; Overdose Gamnion, Mr. R. Eden; Abraham Pugging, Mr. J. H. Pickthall ; and (11) Usher, Mr. Nancollis. A vote of thanks to those who bad taken part in the programme was passed on the motion of Mr. W. Handley, seconded by Mr. Harold Chad wick. Mr. J. D. Jones acknowledged the compliment, and prcposed U vote of thanks to the Chairman. This was seconded y Mr. J. J. Speakman. and passed with enthusiasm. RUNCORN 'NEW TRADES' HALL.</p>
        <p>Mr. Brunner, M.P., visited Runcorn on Saturday for the purpose of holding a conference witit represent-a- tives of the Urban District Council and the Trades' and Friendly Societies in the town. The conference was held in the new hall. Mr. Speakman, J P., CM., presided, and among those present were Messrs. S. Taylor, J.P., C.C., James Pritchard (of the Urban District Council), F. A. Lake (la* clerk),E. Gatley, E. Aston,J. Billineron, John Pennington, M. Lightfoot, W. Cousins, G. H. Kirk- ham, J. Stubbs, S. Johnson, J. Taylor (of the Trades' Societies), It. Wright, Cringle, James Lea, T. Leach, Quinn, T. Riley, Williamson, W. E. Dudley, W. Milling- tot, (Friendly Societies), and W. Handley. With refer- ence to the settlement as to the name of the hall a pro- longed discussion took place as to whether it should be called the " Brunner Guild Hall " or the " Trade and Friendly Societies Hall." Ultimately it was decided to leave the naming of the building to the donor. It was resolved that the trusteeship be conveyed to the Urban District Council,and that the hall should be managed by a committee selected by the various societies tenanting the ball for 12 months, and that the representation of the Council should nonsist of three entlemeo, oriP of whom should he a member of the authority. Toe trust deed was read thr,msli, and tint onthnos .4 the terms are to he printed and circulated ittnengst the I raties and friendly suciwies, Bz., in the town. The hall is to he open to arty religious, political, or social holy who msy desire to utilise it. Votes of thanks wer,:t passel to Mr. Brunner and the Chairman,on the motion of Mr.Gstley, 1'; M. S. rr.t)lJr.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>EXAMINER. SA.TURIIAY, APRIL</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER. SA.TURIIAY, APRIL</p>
        <p>BRUNSWICK MUTUAL IMPROVEMENT SOCIETY, RUNCORN. The last meeting of the session was held on Wednes- day evening. A tea was provided, the tables being pre- sided over by Mr. J. D. Jones, Misses A. Jones, Littler, Crosby and Simkin. Mr. Simister presided over the after-proceedings. He delivered a short address as did also the Rev. W. George. Songs were given by Misses K. Carter, and A. M. Taylor, and Messrs. Evans, F. Ellison, Cooke (Chester), A. Povali and Robinson. The room had been tastefully decorated by Mr. G. Hayes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>BURNING ACCIDENT TO A VY ATCFMAN AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>BURNING ACCIDENT TO A VY ATCFMAN AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>On Monday night, about 10.30, when Mr. Langford, manager at Messrs. W. H. Smith and Sons', railway bookstall, was proceeding home along the finlloway,• in which a sewer is being laid, he heard peculiar sounds issuing from a watchman's cabin. He went there and found an old man named George Cowman,of 2, Surrey- st., enveloped in smoke. With assistance the man was got out, and it was found that his clothing was on fire. The man had almost become suffocated with the flames. On being brought out in the open air he revived some- what. He was taken home, and his injuries were at- tended to. Had it not been for the timely assistance of Mr. Langford the man 'would most probably have been burnt to death. A RUNCORN MAN ASSAULTED AT FRODSRAM.</p>
        <p>Yesterday (Wednesday) afternoon, before Mr. A. Thomas and Dr. Steele at Frodsham Police Court, Henry Booth, Newtown, FrAsilam, was charged with assault- ing Thomas Ball, 11 E2erton-street, Runcorn, on Sunday the 7th inst. Defendant pleaded guilty. The evidence of the complainant was to the effect that on the data named he was in company with the defendant at Frod- sham Bridge. They had a quarrel and the defendant struck him. He was also bitten on the leg and thumb by a dog which was in company with the defendant.— Thomas Williams, Runcorn, deposed that he saw Booth assault the complainant who was also bitters by Booth's dog.—P.C. Price stated that he was in the Police office on the date named when the complainant came in. He was bleeding from wounds on the leg and thumb. Wit- ness advised him to go to a doctor. The magistrates imposed a fine of £2 including costs, or in default one montles imprisnnment.. The money was paid. _ _ _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>– THE REV. J. HOLDEN'S DEPARTORE.-PRESENTATION.</head>
        <p>- THE REV. J. HOLDEN'S DEPARTORE.-PRESENTATION.</p>
        <p>An interesting gathering was held in the National schoolroom on Wednesday evening, when the Rev. J. Holden, who has been preferred to the living at Antro- bus, was presented with an illuminated address, and a purse containing £3O. The Vicar (Rev. Canon Maitland Wood) occupied the chair, and there were also present, Revs. J. Holden and H. B. Firth, Messrs. E. liaison, J.P., James Littler, John Littler. W. Mellor, W. E. Dudle7. W. Timmins, J,ll,imilton, J. Yates, A. Jordan, U. Hewitt,</p>
        <p>_ • D. Lea,, E. KsV. a.- K. Digges, J. E. Meredith and E. Gregory. Mr. John Littler at the request of the Chairman read the address. The following was the text of the address :—" Rev. and Dear Sir. You have laboured among us in the great parish of Runcorn for nearly seven years,and dur- ing that time have won the sincere respect and regard of all who have come to know you. You will be very much missed when you leave us, and by none more than by the poorest, whom you have so diligently visited, comforting them and lifting them up to God in sickness and death, and not fearing to rebuke and exhort in times of health and prosperity. We ask you to accept this address and the purse which accompanies it as a small token of the cordial feelings of the parishioners of Runcorn towards you, and we pray that in your new field of labour there may ever rest upon you the blessing of Almighty God. We are your affectionate friends." About ninety signatures followed, of the subscribers.— The Chairman in making the presentation said it was a witness in Mr. Holden's favour. He could assure Mr. Holden personally, and he believed he could for those present, of their esteem for the way in which he had worked amongst them. He had bters of very great help to the parish. He had been active in promoting foreign mis‘ions, and had also taken up the work of the Church of England Tem- perance Society; the Women's Guild, and the Women's Bible Class. Re had had the work of the largest district in the parish, and had, in addition, given instruction to the pupil teachers in religious knowledge.—Mr. Imison alluded to Mr. Holden's services, and made special men- tion of his work on the relief committee during the re- cent distress.—Mr. Meredith and Mr. James Littler also sooke.—The Rev. J. Holden in his response, stated that he was deeply grateful for their kindness, and sensible of the kindly feeling shown towards him. He deeply re- gretted to leave the parish of Runcorn. The proceedings then terminatod. ST. JOHN'S AMBULANCE ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday evening the first meeting of the recently appointed committee of the Runcorn branch of this association was held. Mr. E. Newton was appointed chairman of the committee for the year,Mr. J. D. Smith being selected as vice-chairman. Messrs. W. Manning and J. Bosworth were appointed auditors. It Wati sug- gested that an ambulance station was desirable if rv-,t absolutely necessary for the efficient carrying out of the work of the association,and the secretary (Mr. El. Clare) was authorised to secure suitable premises for the purpose. . -</p>
        <p>. . A QUICB DESPATCH. The " Foynland " of Tonsherg, registered tonnage 683 tons ; burden, 1,100 tons ; has been !aided in Weston Point Docks with 478 tons of bag salt (filled alongside), and 21 tons bag salt (ready filled), in 401 working hours. The despatch in this case has given great satisfaction to the Liverpool shippers. The above is the largest vessel which has entered any of the docks on the Upper WI( r- sey. The broker for the vessel was Mr. F. Smith, Cus- tom House Buildings, Rancorn. ST. PAUL'S WESLEYAN CHURCU. RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>A Workers' Association h3s been formed in connection with this church, with the object of increasing the at- tendance at divine worship and of improvin,e the ser- vices of thP church so as to make them more attractive and beneficial. Mr. J.Povah has been appointed organi- sine secretary, and the committee,with power to add to their number, consists of Messrs. F. J. Norman, J.P., A. R. Norman, G. Bellamy, J. D. Jones, T. Leach, jun., J. Douglas, D. Bisbrown, R. Rtwlinson, and Greeoway Millington. A sub•committee has been formed for the purpose of receiving strangers to the Church and attend- ing to the congregation generally,and the formation of a Girls' Guild is being considered.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>CHEMICAL TRADE AT RUNCORN. A DINUIETING RUMOUR.</head>
        <p>THE CHEMICAL TRADE AT RUNCORN. A DINUIETING RUMOUR.</p>
        <p>Avery disquieting rumour to the effect that the officials at Wiggs' Chemical works at Runcorn had receive I instructions for a complete stoppage of operations at an early date was freely circulated in the town on Wednesday. On inquiry in official quarters, whilst the statement was not denied, it was thought that even yet the decision of the United Alkali Company might be modified. No official notices have been served upon the workmen engaged, up to the present. The statement caused much indignation against the United Alkali Com- pany amongst -Runcorn people. ever since the for- mation of the chemical syndicate some few years ago, the trade in Runcorn has been waning, and at the present time only about one-third of the work- people who were previously engaged in Runcorn are now found employment. If the Com- pany do decide to close their works they may expect opposition from Runcorn. Already, we can confidently state there is a deter- mination in certain cirles that the chemical indus- try in the neighbourhood shall not thus be destroyed. The erection of entirely new chemical works is talked about, and Weston Point has been referred to as a place possessing natural advan- tages for such an industry. It is quite possible that should the United Alkali Company carry out their intention to close Wigire' works Some definite action in the way indicated will be taken. The rumour which has been current for some mouths past, as to the closing of Cie Runcorn Alkali Works by the United Alkali C•nnpany, may also shortly prove to be true if Wiggs' works, or what are now known as the Old Quay Chemical and Copper Works, are closed. It will be remembered that the Manchester Ship Canal Company offered to buy these works during the construction of the canal. This was resolutely denied them, and they were then compelled to spend a large sum of money in providing the works with a water supply ; con- structing a swing bridge near the works, and also in carrying their material during the construction of the waterway. In the light of affairs at the present moment this seems to have been money needlessly thrown away.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>FRODT.iHAM.</head>
        <p>FRODT.iHAM.</p>
        <p>WESLEYAN MEN'S BIBLE CLASS.—Mr. Henry Lewis pre- sided at the open meal mg in connection with this clasA, held in the lecture room on Sunday afternoon. There was a large number of members and friends present. The address was delivered by Mr. E. J. Guest, J.P., of Helsby, the subject being "Character." Sacred quartets and solos were rendered by Mrs, W. Youd, Miss E. Kent, and Messrs. W. Youd, T. Pover, H. Bottomley, G. Chat- wend, H. Fogg, and J. Jones. TRINITY (WESLEYAN) MUTUAL IMPROVEMENT SOCIETY. —The closing meeting for the session of this society was hell last (Wednesday) evening when a " Ladies' Even- ing " was held. An interesing miscel,laneous programme of vocal and instrumental music was rendered. The soloists were Mrs. itekton, Mrs. Cutting ; Misses Gornall and Bennett (Warrington), and Linaker (Frodsham) : Messrs. Bennett (Warrington), W. Linaker and Fogy. Miss 13,.,ssie Dutton, of Moore, recited in an ex,ellent manner. The instrumentalists were Miss Guest (lielsby) and Miss Lewis (Overton). A most pleasant evening was spent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>LYM M.</head>
        <p>LYM M.</p>
        <p>natt Lark Me. CHARLES LISTER.—At the Ch-shire Quarter Sessions, Kt. Knutsifora, on Monday, Mr. liolke• Icy Allen, of Altrinehdin, was appointed to take the ()lace of the late Mr. Cnarles Lister on the Stlnthog Joint</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>A. FRODSIIAM MAN'S VISIT TO WIDNES.</head>
        <p>A. FRODSIIAM MAN'S VISIT TO WIDNES.</p>
        <p>MEN CHARGED WITH RIFLING HIS POCKETS.</p>
        <p>At Widnes Police Court, on Monday morning, before Mr. S. Sadler (chairman) and Mr. W. W. Gossage, Walton Bushell, flatrnan, Edmund-street, Widnes, John Connor, no fixed abode, and Patrick Keeghan, nc fixed abode, were brought up on remand, charged with having stolen a purse containing money from John Harvey, Church-street, Frodsham at the same time using personal violence. It appears that Harvey went to Widnes on the previous Monday to draw his army pension, and remained in the town until Tuesday. While he was under the influence of drink he met Bushell who took him to Fitzgerald's lodging-house in Lugsdale-road, Widnes. When there Harvey paid for the prisoners. Keeghan took Harvey into the back yard, and Connor under the pretence of wanting to fight, got Harvey to take his coat off, and Bushell then put his hand in his (Harvey's) pocket and extracted a purse. Keeghan knocked Harvey down, and prisoners rifled his pockets.—Thomas Harvey, Frodsharn, spoke as to hav- ing about 15s. in his pockets, and part of that money he received from the pension office.—Mary Ann Conlpn, 22, Nelson street, Widnes, stated that the prisoners were in the lodging house on the forenoon of the day in question ' drinking beer. She saw Connor and Keeghan among a number of others. About 'one o'clock in the afternoon, Thomas Harvey came in. He lay down about a quarter of an hour, at the expiration of which time he said " I am for home." —Bushell replied "you have time enough for home." She saw nothing more until about 330 when the persons went into the yard. She head Connor challenging the complainant to fight. When Harvey was in the act of taking off his coat. Bushell whipped a purse out, and placed it in his (prisoner's) pocket. Keeghan and Bushell knocked the complainant against the wall and afterwards to the ground. Whilst on the ground the prisoners rifled Harvey's pockets but witness did not know whether anything was taken out of them. Bushell and Keeghan then lifted complainant up. Witness told prisoners that that was " nice carry- ing's on to an old man." Keeghan made use of some foul language. She informed Mr. Fitzgerald, the land- lord of the lodging-house, what had occurred, and he communicated with the polic*.—P.S. McGregor gave evidence to apprehending prisoners who were ordered to take their trial at the next Liverpool Assizes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>RUNCORN BURIAL BOARD.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BURIAL BOARD.</p>
        <p>THE LAST MEETING.</p>
        <p>- _ A meeting of the Runcorn Burial Board—which will be the last under the present management—was held at the Board offices on Wednesday afternoon. Mr. E. Imison occupied the chair, and there were also present Rev. Dr. Gastaldi, Mr. R. Morris and Mr. W. F. Silkeld (see,retary). The Secretary's report showed that the interments for the year ending March 25th, numbered 305. as compared with 503 during the corresponding period last year; thus giving a decrease of 198. The fees received during the Past twelve months amounted to £2BO Bs.; as against £467 17s. 83. last year. The report added that the cemetery was opened on March Ist., 1860. and the inter- ments since that date were :—Church 4.434. Noncon- formist, 5.542, Roman Catholic 876, total to March 15th, 1895. 10,852.—The report was adopted on the motion of Dr. Gastaldi, seconded by Mr. Morris. Mr. Imison said he did not think the last meeting of the Board should be allowed to pass without some for- mal statement from the chairman. He would like to say as chairman that he thanked the members of the Board for the courtesy which had always been extended to him in the position he had held. He did not know what might be the result of the work of the new council hut he was perfectly certain that whatever it might be, it would not excel that authority in the goodwill and kindness which had existed amongst the members. Be would like to go a little further to say to Mr. Salkeld how greatly indebted they were to him for his services. ; Ever since the inception of that authority he had acted in the capacity in which be was acting that day. He thought it was aom,thing extraordin- ary that a gentleman of his years should be able to set before them in the straightforward and clear manner with which Mr. Salkeld presented his balance sheets and minutes to that Board. Be thought he should only be expressing the thoughts of those pre- sent when he said that they sincerely hoped that Mr. Salkeld might be spared for many years to come, He did not know whether he would be a servant to the new authority, but if not he hoped that he might live many years to enjoy his well-earned retirement. If he did continue his services he was perfectly certain that the new authority would extend to him that same kindness which had been extended to him by that Board. (Applause.) ' Mr. Salkeld said he was very much obliged for the many kind remarks made by the chairman. He had been very much pleased with the courtesy and kindness he had received at the hands of all the members of the Burial Board. It had been his duty to keel% the a crounta and books in a straightforward mannerorid he had done so for a number of years. It had Also been his till*y to keep the Cemetery in good order. During the 37 years he had held the position they had extended to him great kindness for which lie Was very much obliged, and he begged to thank them. In parting he wished them all health and happiness. (Applause.) The meeting then terminited.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>RUNCORN SAVINGS BANK.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN SAVINGS BANK.</p>
        <p>A special meeting of the trustees and managers of this bank Watt hold on Wednesday afternoon, for the purpDse ot considering a letter of resignation as treasurer frcrn Mr. J. Simpson. Mr.J. Brundrit occupied the chair,and there were else present Messrs.. Thomas Handley. J.P. James Handley, J.P.,W. Fisher,W.llankinson.F.A.Lake. and W.Hendley (actuary). A letter was read from Mr. F. Salkeld, expressing regret at his inability to be present through the last meeting of the Burial Beard be- ing held at the same time ; and also his sorrow on hear- ing of Mr. Simpson's resignation, remarking that it would he difficult to find as good a successor. Mr. W. Handley read the letter received from Mr. Simpson, stating that in consequence of ill-health he had determined to resign his position as treasurer to the Savings Bank. Re regretted severing his connection after so long a period of service. Mr. Handley also ex- plainrd that he had with Mr. Brundrjes consent. ap- proached Mr. Holland who had agreed to Perform the duties without commission or interest, and this With the authority of the directors of Peres Banking Company. He also explained what provisional arraneement had been made, and intimated that Mr. Simpson had ex- nressed his willingness to retain the position until May 20, the date of the half-yearly meeting. The Chairman stated that was by far the least pleasant gathering at which he had been present. They had to accept the resignation of a gentleman who for 40 years had been treasurer of the bank and had conducted the affairs in a manner that had cow:limed to its prosperity. 'Trustees. managers and depositors had felt that they had been in the hands of a good, straightforward, honest gentleman, who would take care that everything was done to secure their interests. Mr. Simpson had performed his duties faithfully and well Several times he had expressed his desire to retire hut in consequence of earnest solicitations of the trustees and managers he had been prevailed upon to continue in the position. Under the conditions it would _be un- kind to urge Mr. Simpson to withdraw his resignation. He was sure all concerned would hear with very great regret that he was leaving them. He did not wish to debate on his qualities as a private gentleman, he was so well-known, and his conduct was such that it spoke for itself. With regard to the bank a great deal of the prosperity had been due to the way in which he had fulfilled his position. He had by arduous manipulation, and he used the word in its highest sense, eAveqj the bank from interest and commission which had materi- silly assisted them to keep the balance on the right side. Mr. Simpson had nursed it threileh childhood and boa hood into manhood and had fairly launched it on the sea of prosperity which he (the chairman) honed would continue for many years. They would he failing in their duty if they did not record a hearty vote of tnanks to Mr. Simpson for the admirable way in which he had cDnducted the Bank, and whilst: regretting that he had resigned through ill-health, they hoped that he might be sparea for many years to faithfully discharge his duties with the institutions of the town, with which he was so closely identified. (Applause.) The Rev. Dr. Preston expressed himself as mach pleased with the chairman's observations. An epoch or crisis had arrived in the history of their Savings Bank. It was his strong opinion that there was no other Sav- ings Bank so -table or firm as that of Runcorn—a cir- cumstance dua to the effictient manner in which the treasurer, who was a tower of strength, had performed his duties. Mr. Simpson was well-known for his in- tegrity and uprightness of character, and it was un- doubtedly to these virtues that the public eame and invested their sayings in the ;bank. The sneaker then referred in complimentary terms to the chairman, and said that that gentl.snan's name gave the institution additional stability in the nubile. mind. He hoped that for many years he ('he Chairman) might he spared to occupy his present posit inn. He took the liberty of proposing a resolution to the effect that the managers and eith.manauers were deenly erieeed to learn the state of Mr. Simpson's health, which had caused him to sever his connection with the hank, and that the thanks of ell associated with that institution be given to Mr. Simpson, for the earnest ii:terest he had evinced for 40 years in the interests of the institu- tion, Mr. James Handley hoped that Mr. Simpson might he long spared to continue his everyday avocation. Mr. Thomas Handley also spoke in sympathetic terms, and eulogised Mr. Simpson's past services. Mr. Hankineon endorsed all that had teen said. The proposition on being put was carried unani- m au sly. The Actuary was requested to convey to Mr. Simpson the sense of the meeting, with a desire that he would retnain treasurer till the 20th of May. With regard, the bond into which the new treeserer should enter i'et was decided to pet it down at £l,OOO, and a committee consisting of Messrs. Lake, James Handley, and W. Fiche, ennnint-d to PPP the mae'er through. The pxoL;etA.:lcgo LIL:u tem:Lated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>13 1895</head>
        <p>13 1895</p>
        <p>RIVER WEAVER NAVIGATION TRUST.</p>
        <p>THE DECISION OF THE SELECT COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>A deputation from Runcorn Urban Council consisting of Messrs. P. Speakman, S. Taylor, J. Pritchard and G. Hewitt, met Mr. Brunner, M.P., at the Town Hall, Run- corn, on Saturday, with reference to Runcorn having a representative on the re-constructed Weaver Trust, as in years past. Seeing that the town is closely allied with the interests of the Weaver,it was thought that Runcorn had a greater right to be represented than Birkenhead, Stockport, and other districts which were not so inti- mately bound up in the river. Mr. Brunner thought that the traders and others would be glad to assist Run- corn to accomplish their desire, the urgency ot the matter was considered great, and it was decided to approach the parliamentary agents to see what could be done, and Messrs. Speakman and Pritchard were authorised to go to London to be prestuat at the proceed- ings of the committee of the House of Commons on Monday. ' The competing Bills promoted by the existing trustees at the River Weaver Navigation and the Sal: Union, for the rearrangement of the present constitution and , management of the Trust were again considered on Monday by the Select Committee of the House of Com- mons, presided over by Mr. Stuart:—The Chairman, in giving the decision, said they had decided that the num- ber of members on the Board should be 38. In the first constitution of that Board there should be ten repre- sentatives of the old Trustees, 12 of the Cheshire County Council, 14 of the tollpayers one of Northwich, and one of Winsford. They were also of opinion that as the representatives of tne old trustees died their places should be filled by members chosen by the County Council, with this limitation, that at least five were always to be residents in the county or within seven miles (not necessarily members of the County Council), and with the same qualifications as to property as thsse required for the present Trustees. As to the 14 repre- sentatives of the tollpayers, they would have when they came to clauses to de.lide the method of their election, all they now suggested for the consideration of parties was that no larger number than six should go to any single interest, such as the Salt Union, and that the remainder should be apportioned as far as possible in proportion to contribution to the tolls. The Committee were agreed that in the application of the reserve fend there should be permission to the Trustees to apply it to improvements in the navigation. Subject to these points of principle, the Committee proposed to pass the preamble of both Bills, and to apply to the Rouse for leave to consolidate them. It was then arranged that the Committee should adjourn until Monday, the 29th April, for the decision of the Committee to to given effect to in the clauses, and the promoters also undertook to supply the Committee with information with respect to the benefices now deriving support from the canal,the whereabouts of the churches, whether they kept up any schools, and how far the population they served was due to the canal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN AT</head>
        <p>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN AT</p>
        <p>RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>BY OUR RUNCORN CORRESPONDENT:</p>
        <p>Runcorn ferry has now been opened for a short time, but the traffic, so far, has been very meagre. This may he accounted for in several ways. One reason is that people have not been aware that the ferry was re-opened ; and then again, there is greater difficulty in crossing from the two counties than was previously the case. The Ship Canal has now to be crossed, and then afterwards the river, necessitating of course,a change of boats,which not only means a loss of time but a source of great in- convenience and danger. It is not likely that the railway company will look upon this with any- thing approaching regret, because having the monopoly of Runcorn Bridge, they have had un- limited freeddm in charging the people who have used it, either as railway or foot pass3ngers. very high prices. Any competition in the way of a ferry would be distasteful to them.</p>
        <p>• People do sometimes get possessed with strange fancies, but one which took possession of the ped- lar named Waters, at Runcorn, on Saturday. whilst under the influence of drink was a source of humi- liation and chagrin to him afterwards. lie had a desire, according to the statement he made to the police officer, of seeing inside Knursford gaol, and in order to gratify hi wish, he ivushed his crutch through the police office window. Of course this soon brought; out a representative of law and order, who immediately took him into custody,and placed him in a fair way of reaching his desired haven.ilis incarceration in the Police cell during Sunday seems to have smnewnat chilled his desire for a vicit to linutsford, for on Monday when before the MRlZiS- trates he pleaded hard to be treated leniently and to be let off on payment of a fine. For the two offences, of drunkenness, and damage to the police station, the fine amounted to about .£l. Perhaps if on another occasion his thoughts again run in the direction of Knutsford, he will not be as precipit- ate in endeavouring to put them in practice.</p>
        <p>Of late there has been quite a large number of Runcorn people drowned. Paragraphs of drowning cases have been frequent in the local prints, and in the present issue of the "Examiner" no fewer than three Runcorn men are reported to have met. their death by drowning. Of course when a great num- ber of people are engaged in the shipring industry it is almost impossible for all to escape from be- coming victims to the manifold risks which are on every hand. But most of the recent drowning cases have been of persons,not particularly engaged in shipping, but in other occupations, and seems to suggest that more precautions are necessary. In connection with the dragaing operations that have been necessary, Mr. W. Shaw, missionary of the Seamen's Institute, Runcorn, has rendered valuable assistance by means of his dragging apparatus which he has made for the purpose. It consists of. a large number of hooks, something like fish hooks but much larger, attached to rope or netting It is so weighted as to reach the bottom of the water- way, and is then pulled along by a number of men. It has the advantage of covering an area of several yards at one time, and is easily manipulated. This week two bodies have been recovered by means of this apparatus, and at the inquest afterwards held, the Coroner complimented Mr. Shaw upon his invention.</p>
        <p>What has been more than once stigmatised as a "death trap" by Mr. Worsley. the coroner for Run- corn, is the towing path along the canal bank lead-. ing from Runcorn to Weston Point. It is used by the public through lack of a more suitable road between the two places, and it was in order that negotiations might be made with the owners of land for the provision of a good highway, that Mr. Bislrown moved his motion at the last meeting of the Runcorn Urban Council. The question is not a new one to the local authority, for it was under the consideration of the late Board of Commissioners for a number of years, but no progress was made. This was in consequence of the members not seeing their way clear to provide a road at the ratepayers' expense. The owners of the land, it is understood are willing to give the land for the road,but are not disposed to contribute towards the cost of con- structing it. This proved the stumbling block,and it is one that has ve's to be overcome. For the Council to construct the road would supply a long felt want, but the expense would be considerable. As the owners of the land abutting the road will bene- fit in the future by its becoming more valuable for building purposes, &amp;c., they should not demur to paying at least some portion of the expense. Itsjor Orred set a good example when the Victoria-road improvement was made, for in addition to giving the land, be contributed £,1,000 towards the ex- pense.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>RF.PORTED DISCOVERY OF DARWIN'S MISSING LINK.</head>
        <p>RF.PORTED DISCOVERY OF DARWIN'S MISSING LINK.</p>
        <p>It is generally known that the late Professor Sir Richard Owen, the eminent geologist, discovered the ancient existence of th. Dodo, an extinct bird, consider- ably larger than the ostrich, through somebody sendinc a rase of old bones from the Antfpodes. News now comes from Java that a Dutch gentleman, a Dr. Dubois, has unearthed the remains of an animal which he has named the Pithecanthropus erectus. It is of the tertiary period. As yet only the skull, thigh hone, and one ROll- - tooth have been found, hut this Dr. Dubois consi- ders quite sufficient to justify his claim to having die- covvred Darwin's missing link, and that these " finds " enable him to certify tha'; the animal walked erect like mankind; that it was the height of a man, and from the formation of the jaw it could articulate without difficulty. Of course the 10a rm.(' doctor will pursue his researches for the missing bones of this missing link before he ran prpsOt•t,e sk.te,nn to puhiic view. just as Professor Oren 'rte out for more bones before he could cow- p'ete the skeleton of the Dodo.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
