      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>IMPERLAI PARLIAMENT</head>
        <p>IMPERLAI PARLIAMENT</p>
        <p>CONTINUED FROM PAGE 6.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.-ELECTION OF A NEW SPEAKER.</p>
        <p>The House of Commons met on Wednesday for thesole purpose of electing a Speaker in successbn to Mr. A. W. PEEL. The name of Mr. W. C. GULLY, Q.C., was pro- posed by Mr. WHITBREAD, who said he regretted to learn that upon the present occasion the practice which had happily endured for more than half a century was likely to be broken, and that the selection of those who formed the majority of the House was to be questioned. Mr. A. BIRRELL, who seconded the motion, expressed his con- viction that if the choice of the House fell upon Mr. GULLY, time—the sole test of a man's character and actions—would very soon reveal the fact that they had for their new speaker a man qualified both by nature and education, by mind and by will, to be the spokes- man of their rights, the guardian of their liherties. and the preserver of what sli,uld be dearer to them than their rights, more precious by far than any of their pri- , vileges—their order, their honour, and their dignity.. , Sir JOHN MOWBRAY. while agreeing on the impreance which attached to the unanimous choice of the Speaker, totally demurred to the proposition that the selection of the fittest man rested with the House at large. Pe claimed that they should choose as their Speaker a mein- mber who was essentially one of themselves, trainsd within those walls,not within the courts of law,one who was conversant with their Standing Ordeis, who was imbued with their t,aditions, and who was known and recommended to them by personal antecedents. Sir M. WHITE RIDLEY possessed all these qualifications, and he had therefore great pleasure in proposing him for the position of Speaker. Mr. WHARTON seconded the amend- ment. Mr. GULLY, in a short speech, said that in his humble opinion to on chosen Speaker of that ancient and famous Assembly was the greatest honour that could be conferred upon any subject of the QUEEN by his fellow-subjects. Sir M. W. IltyLsr, in humbly placing his services at the disposal of the House, said if it should be the pleasure of the House to elect him to the chair he would to the best of his abilities endeavour to attain to that splendid im- partiality which they had recently witnessed, and to win from every quarter of the House that confidence which was the main strength of its Speaker. Mr. BALEouri said it was not too much to say that Mr. Outur was absolutely on- known to them in his Pailiamentary capacity. In pro- posing Mr. GULLY, who was so little identified with their Parliamentary life, the Government had deliberately elected to break with all Pailiamentary traditions, and had adopted a course a hsolutely without precedent. Sir W. HARCOURT said the first object of the Government and himself had been to secure, if possible, a unanimous election. It was perfectly well known that the object of the Government was to secure in the Chair a man of all others who had had experience, and Mr. BAL- FOUR alone had defeated that. The objections taken to Mr. GULLY were identical with those urged against Mr. PEEL. There was absolutely no justifica- tion and no precedent for a minority forcing their nominee on the House, and he shoule give his warm and cordial support to Mr. GULLY. Mr. BALFOUR said he must not be inisunderatend as admitting the accuracy of the statements of the CHANCELLOR of the EXCHEQUER. The House than divided. and \tr. GULLY was elected by 28b votes to 274. He took the chair and brief/ thanked the House, and was then congratulated by Sir W. HARCOURT and Mr. BALFOUR. Sir W. HARCOURT announced that the Speaker-elect will be presented for Her Majesty's approval in the House of Lords on ths 22nd inst., and the House then adjourned to that date.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>FRIENDS' SCHOOL ATHLETIC sronTs.</p>
        <p>These sports were held on Monday. The warm weather made them pleasant alike for competitors and spectators, and much interest was shown in the various races and contests. J. Wareing wen a large proportion of the senior events, his kick of 49 yards' throw of 80 yards, and quarter-mile in 59 15 seconds being very goodesperially the last item. Among the juniors Robert Lowe was canepieuaus by his succeass all through. In the heats of the 100 yards race the winners were only a faw inehes ahead, whilst in the final of the junior race opinions were divided as to whether the race was won by F. Hewitt or was a dead heat. In the quarter mile Wareinz went at a great pace. as ia shown by the time, but WAS not at:le to dyerli.tul R. Houghton, to whom he had coneeded 40 yards start. The mile race for those under 13 was won by El. Ward, a bey of 10 years old, in the excellent time of six minutes seven seconds. In I ; jumning the contests were specially keen, Wareing, ! Andrew and Marsden being only separated by one inch in the high jump, whilst in the long jump the first two jamped exactly the same distance. The parallel-bar contest and t'ae ' senior tinrell4 race was very clos by contested, Wareing winning the latter by only a few inches. , The programme was as follews:-Throwine cricket ball I(k:inlet) : 1. R. Lowe, 55yds. ;2. J. Marsden. Kicking football (senior) : I,J. Wareing,49cds. ; 2, R. Houghton. ' One hunared yatils race (junior): First heat. 1, R. Lowe; 2, A. Houghton ; 3, H. Nash ; second heat, I,F. Hewitt; 2, J. Marsden ; 3, J. Lunt. High jump (junior) : 1, IR. Lowe. 3ft. Bin. ; 2, F. Hewitt. Throwing cricket ball (senior): 1, J. Wareing, 80rds. ; 2, W. Char-1 neck. One hundred yards race (senior) : First heat I 1, J. Wareing, ; 2, P. Marrow ;3, G. Holcicroft and R. Davies (equal, ; secmd beat, 1, 8.. Houghton; 2, S. , Lloyd ; 3, S. Marsden. One hundred yards (junior), final : 1, F. Hewitt, 13see. ;2, IL Lowe ; 3, J. Mars- I den. One hundred yards (senior), final : 1,3. Ware- log, 112 hth sec.; 2, R. Houghton; 2, H. Lloyd. Quar- ter mile (jenior), 1, R. Lowe. 72 2-sth sec. '•E, J. Mars- den, 3. F. Hewitt. Quarter mile (senior), 1, R. Houghton; 2. J. iVareing, 59 1-ssec. ; 3, Si Marsden. Mile race (jonior) : 1, H. Ward. Broin. 7sec. ;2, B. Youde ; 3. H. Nash. Long jump (junior): 1, R. Lowe. 10ft. ; 2, A. Hartley. High jump (senior): 1, J. Wareing, 4ft. Sin. ; 2. R. Andrew, 4ft. 4n., and S. Marsden (equal). Hurdle race (senior): First heat, 1, R. Davies; 2, J. Wareing; 3. S. Marsden •, second heat, 1, R. Andrew; 2, H. Lloyd; 3, C. Harlow. Parallel bar contest (senior): 1, R. Davies; 2, G. Cooke; 3, J. Kelsall. Long jump (senior): 1, J. Wareing ; 2, R. Andrew, lat. 10s. ; 3,R. Davies. Hurdle race (junior): First heat, 1, A. Hnughten ; 2, A. Hartley; 3, J. hunt; second heat, I,R. Lowe; 2. F. Hewitt; 3, J. Marsden. Hurdle race (senior), final: 1,3. Wareing ; 2, R. Davies; 3, R. Andrew. Hurdle race (junior), final : I, R. Lowe; I 2, F. Hewitt ; 3, J. Marsden. ' CONSw.RVATIOE MEETING.-On Tuesday night a meet- ing of Conservatives residing in the townships of Penketh !Sankey, Bold and Cuerclley, was held in the Conservative clubroom, Penketh. There was a moderate attendance, among these nresent being J. Woodward, W. Denton, P. Pownall, C. Humphries. J. Eden, J. Hayes, Halton, and I others. Mr. W. P. Skelton occupied the chair, and was supported by Mr. Owen. Mr. Skelton in opening the meeting said they had met together to elect officers for I the ensuing year,and they required to be good workers, as there was likely to he a general election this year. Mr. Jonas Foster then read a letter from Mr. J. C. Ridgway, reeretting his inability to attend and his wish to r,tino frem the chairmanship. Mr. Owen said they could ill 'I afford to lose 'he services of such a man as Colonel Ridgway and proposed that Mr. Ridgway be asked to reconsider his decision. This Was carried. The vice- presidents for the different townships were Messrs. T. Raker, M. Taylor, M. D. Honghton, and W. P. Skelton. Mr. Jonas Foster Was unanimously re-elected speretary, I and thanked for the way he had looked after their in- terests in the past. Mr. James Davies (Bold) was re- I elected treasurer. The &amp;legs tes from the township,' I were elected Messrs. Brocklehurst (Hold). J. Wright ((uerdley), P. Pownall (Penketh). and Henry Rate (Sankey). The old Committee were re-elected with the exception of it. tioardman and A. T. JiMn- son (deceased) and the addition of Messrs. Habum, Flobson, and Boscoos. Mr. John Eden then moved a resolution "That this meeting considers that the domestic policy of the present Government is franght with danger tn the Empire. does not represent , the proper feelings of its constituents. and would gladly welcome a more suitable and patriotic party." Mr. W. Dentnn seconded this, and it was carried unanimously. Mr. Henry Fielding was then introduced, and gave an on political tonics. A vote of thanks WAS passed to Mr. Fielding. and the meeting terminated. PENEETH AND SANEST WINDOW AND COTTAGE GAR- DEN SOCIETY.-The general meetine of the above society was held on Friday evening in the Elementary Schools. Mrs. Garnett, the president, occupied the chair. The moeting was well attended by members. Mr. Clarke, the secretary, said the balance sheet showed a balance in favour of the society of £1 15-t. Id., and he com- mented upon the different items of expenditure. He said the thanks of the society were due to the managers of the infants' school for the free use of that room for the exhibition ; and to Mr. G. H. Bolton for the loan of decorative plants, and the assistance of the gardeners in arranging the room. Be also SOniTA of the kind way the managers of the Elementary Schools had treated the society by allowing the committee meetings to be held there at so small a cost. Resolutions in accordance with the above suggestions were unanimously passed by the meeting, and the Secretary was requested to communicate these expressions of thanks. The meeting then proceeded with the election of officers far the ensuing year. Mrs. Garnett was neanimeaisty elected president of the society,Messrs. Longshaw, Newcombe, Millington, Wigfield and Mt-wares, the committee, and Messrs. Jtckson and Clarke treasurer and secretary respectively. A suggestion on the advisability of haying classes for birds at the next show led to an animated discussion. and Mr. J. Samp- son was added to the committee to represent that sec- tion if such an addition could be Mad.. A new class for small gardens was etiggested by the Serretary.and being sTeneraiir approved was referred to the committee. A further class for a boutattet of wild flaw era to be eathered by children under thirteen years Was '4(1,0, aol rtrZ,,r3 were promised for Oda class indep.,rnient of the gei,eral fund.</p>
