        <p>WAR RINGTON GY 3INA SLUM.</p>
        <p>THE ANNUAL MEE lING.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the members of Warrington Gymnasium was held on Tuesday evening in the large room, the chair being occupied by the President, Mr. ['homes Morris, F.G.S. There was a:fair attendance of members.</p>
        <p>THE SECRETARY'S REPORT. The PRESIDENT said they were compelled by the law of Charity Commissioners to hold an annual meeting, although the business to be transacted was of a formal character. _</p>
        <p>Mr. W. W. DIMMELOW (hon. secretary) made a state- ment with reference to the number of members and the financial position of the Gymnasium, from which it appeared that the number of members attending the men's class is 65, boys' class 24 ladies class 51. There is a decrease in the men's class of seven, an increase in the boys' class.of six, and in the ladies' class of eight. The loss of revenue during the year had been principally due to the non-hiring of the roam for balls and meetings, including the football club, who had not joined this season. The total income of the year had been £167 13s 9d., and the total expendi- ture £169 94. 11d., leaving a debit balance of £1 163.2 d. He considered that the balance-sheet was very favour- able compared with the one presented last year, being only is. 7d. in excess. (Applause.) The cotntnittee were sorry that the Saturday evening class had to be discontinued. It was arranged owing to an app!ication signed by various members of the adult class, but in consequence of the small attendance and want of enthusiasm the Committee were compelled to discontinue it. There was another matter to which he wished to refer, and that was the Physical Recreation Society. No doubt the members felt disap- pointed there had been no competition this year. That matter was left in the hands of a sub-committeer,t,o,)odseeadl with, but it was evidently decided to allow the matter to elide. He hoped that next year, as it was p to amend the rules. they would have an opportunity of joining again. (Hear, hear.) Mr. FERGUSON moved, and Mr. W. BAMBEIZ seconded the adoption of the balance sheet.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN said the report was most satisfactory. Last year one of the members had to find £25 to keep the current expenses going, but this year he had not to find twentv-fiva pence. (Applause.) They had a fair - number of members—more than they had this time last year. That was a great thing to say,and there was also more enthusiasm than had been known since the . Gymnasium was started. B)me people said the institu- tion was dying, but he could tell them to the contrary. (Hoar, hear, and applause.) The motion was agreed to. THE PRESIDENT. On the motion nt Mr. Austin Vernon, seconded l)y Mr. McGregor, Mr. Thomas Morris was unanimoasly and - heartily reelected President for the ensuing year.—The President briefly returned thanks. He said it. had been suggested to him that he would no doubt be glad tor the institution to he handed layer to the Corporation, as it had been such a great tax upon his time awl pocket. His reply was, " him: of the sort. Our committee is a good working committee." Ho believed, in fact, that , the committee worked better than any he was connected with, and he was on about a dozen. tighter.)</p>
        <p>OTHER OFFICERS AND COMMITTEE. The following, appointments were then made :—Com- mittee, Messrs. D snip! Jones, John Evans, U. Ferguson, David Tinnion, J. E. McGregor, John Hesketh, James Lyon Whittle, W. W. Dimmelow, and James Richard- son ; hon. seCretary,Mr. George Vernon, and hon. treas- urer Mr. Austin Vernon, and assistant treasurer • Mr. Mottrsm. It was explained that Mr. Dimmelow was unable longer to bold the office of lion. secretary owing to the increasing business engagements.</p>
        <p>PROPOSED TESTIMONIAL. The PRESIDENT said they were parting with a gentle- man who had been secretary of the institution nearly since its formation. At any rate he had occupied that post for five years, and he hoped that at some future time his services to the Gymnasium wsuld be rewarded in a substantial manner. (Hear, hear, and applause.) He had worked very hard for it. (Hear, hear). —Mr. Trxxims, in proposing a vote of thanks to Mr. Dimmelow, said he heartily concurred in the suggestion which had been made by Mr. Morrie with reference to the proposed testimonial to Mr Dimmelow. It had been well earned. kApplause.)—Mr. Morr RAM seconded, ancl Mr. McGaeoon supported the m4tion of a vote of thanks, which was put tu the meeting and carried with great unanimity. Mr. DIMMELOW returned thanks, and a vote of thanks having been accorded to Mr. Riusley the instructor and Mr. Morris for, presiding, the meeting terminated. WARRINGTON HIGHWAY BOARD.</p>
        <p>L meeting of the above Board was held on Wednesday in the Bewsey Chambers, Bewsey-st. Captain T. EllameS Withingtm presided,and there were also present Messrs. J. Houghton, Millington, Royston, P. J. Bate, Clare, Grounds, Ormrod, H. Bate ; E. A. White (assis- tant clerk), and J. Thompson (surveyor).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>THIt RECENT LOCAL GOVERNMENT- BOARD INQUIRY. _ – .</head>
        <p>THIt RECENT LOCAL GOVERNMENT- BOARD INQUIRY. _ - .</p>
        <p>Mr. Ormrod asked the assistant clerk how the -cTs.e.f or the Highway Board was received at the recent inquiry at the Town Hall. lute the application for the extension of the Borough of Warrington.—The Assistant Clerk said that of course the decision would not be known for some time yet. The Highway Board was not opposed to the extension so long as they could get some arrangement assuming that a provisional order were made, whereby the Board's interests should be guarded—Mr. Ormrod said that he thought that was what the Board really- wanted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>BALANCE IN BAND.</head>
        <p>BALANCE IN BAND.</p>
        <p>The Clerk reported that the balance in the hands of the treaeurer was -2428 184. 51. on March 25. All the calla had been paid, buL there was something due from the county hal,,nce. ATTENDANCE or MEMBERS FOR THE PAST YEAR.</p>
        <p>The Asstatant Clerk said that the Fsnance Committee had met 12 times during the year, and the following wait the attendJsme of members :—Mr. Royston, 9 • Mi.Floughtm, 9 ; Mr. Gough. 3 ; Mr. Clare, 8 ; Mr.d.Bate, 4 ; Mr. P. Bate. 9; Mr. R. Lowe, 5 ; Mr. It Owen, 2 Mr. Jsmes Kerfont, 9. There had been eight ordinary general moetinga of the Board, of which Mr. Gough had attended, 1 ; Mr. Wright, 2 ; Mr. Clare. 6 ; Mr.H.Bate, 5 ; Mr. Stonier, 1; Mr. P. Bate, 8; Mr. Ormrod, 7 ; Mr. Millino•ton, 7; Mr. Royston, 5 ; Mr. E. Owen, 3 ; Mr. Kerfoot, 6 •, Mr. Houghton, 6 ; klr.GroundS, 8 ; and the ChairmaL, 6.</p>
        <p>FOTFS OF TIIANKS.</p>
        <p>Oa the motion of Mr. P. J. rate, seconded by Mr. Grounds a vote of thanks was accorded the chairman for his services during the past year. A similar compli- ment was accorded the senior and junior vicr-chairmen on the motion of Mr. Millington. seconded by the Chair- man.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>JABEZ BALFOTTR'S RETURN.</head>
        <p>JABEZ BALFOTTR'S RETURN.</p>
        <p>ATTEMPT TO RE-LAND ruE FUGITIVE.</p>
        <p>• BUENOS AYRES, TUESDAT.—Aithough the Tartar Prince, with Jabez Balfour on board, was to have left on Sunday, and did actually leave her berth, she found she could not get out to sea owing to the prevailing low- ness of the tides. There was not sufficient water in the channel for a vessel of her draught, and she is still de- tained owing to this cause. The criminal judge at Salta took advantage of this fact to make a final attempt to keep Jabez in this country. Two of his agents Testerday went on hoard the Tartar Prince with a written order to take Balfour back with them. The captain, however, refused to surrender and at once communicated with the Hon. F. Packen- ham, the British Minister. Th att-•r informed the Federal authorities of what had taken place, whereupon the Minister for Fereign Affairs gave order that a de- tachment of soldiers should be sent to lender, if neces- sary, any assistance the captain of the Tartar Prince might re quire to prevent Balfour being landed again. Blackpool is priding itself somewhat on the fart thrd-, one of its townsmen, Captain flesketh, is bringing Jab, z Balfour back again. It is the hrst voyage of the Tartar Prince, and singularly nouch before he sailed he was jocularly asked to bring the wanderer home. The steamer is the !atevt addition to the Prince line of steamers, and is 500 tons burden.</p>
