      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>A MIDNIGHT MARATIDER.—PENSION DAY INCIDENT. Oa Saturday morning, at the Warrington Borough Police Court, before Mr. J. Burgess (chairman), Mr. S. Chandley, and Mr. J. Geddes,Martin Gillighan,labourer, was charged with stealing from the model lodging-house Friars'-green, on the 3rd inst., a neck tie, value is., the property of John Jefferson ; prisoner was also charged with stealing two keys, the property of Alfred Brouche- mite.—Mr.S. Hunt (chief constable) stated that the first complainant, John Jefferson, army pensioner, lodged at the Model Lodging-house, while the defendant was a farm labourer, who "worked " Runcorn, Warrington, and Widnes. Tuesday, the Znd inst., was pension day and Jefferson drew his pension £3 16s. id., and bought some wearing apparel,afterwards depositing some of the money with a friend, reserving to himself 7s. About 11 o'clock on Tuesday night, the prisoner, who lodged at the same place as complainant, saw the latter count his money before going to bed. The complainant on reaching his room locked the door and placed the 7s. in his cap, which he put under the bed. In the morning he awoke and found his clothes disarranged and net long after prisoner walked into the room with some whisky and gave complainant a drink. Complainant rose and dressed himself, but missed his scarf and the 75., and it was not until Thursday that complainant made known to Detective-Sergeant Monaghan his loss. On Friday,Monaghan met prisoner inßridge-st.and after a moment's conversation noticed that he was a bit bulky on one side, and underneath his shirt, Monaghan found the scarf. Be then conducted prisoner to the police station. In Mersey-street the prisoner adroitly dropped the keys which Monaghan picked up. One of the keys in question will unlock all the bedroom doors in the lodging house and the other store cupboards,! —John Jefferson the complainant and Detective Sergeant Monaghan gave corroborative evidence.—Aifred Brou- cbcmie, teeper of the Model Lodging House, deposed that the keys had been lost for about two months and since their loss there had been numerous petty robberies and the prisoner had been known to come down from the rooms about 3 o'clock in the morning. It was witness's opinion that defendant was in the habit of going to the rooms of lodgers for the purpose of stealing and if dis- turbed offering them sedrink of whiskey which he would have with him for the purpose.—The prisoner was sent to gaol for one month in each charge, with hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGH COURT.</p>
        <p>FRlDAY.—Before Alderman Harrison (chairman), Coun- cillor Roberts, Dr. Gornall, and Mr. S. Wright. MISAPPROPRIATION OF ANOTHER MAN'S GOODS.— HEAVY PENAL Ty..—George Peddar was charged with stealing from No. 1, Railway Cottages, on Thursday afternoon, a man's overcoat, value 10s., the property of Edward Jordan ; also charged with stealing a jacket from the same place, on the same date, the property of John Greenade.—Mr. Huht (chief constable) stated that the complainant lived at Railway Cottages, Liverpool- road, and that the defendant was a blacksmith, and na- tive of Carlisle, and lodged with the complainants. On Thursday afternoon the defendant was seen by John Greenade to come out of the house wearing the evercoat, the property of Jordan, and he also noticed that the de- fendant had something bulky under his arm, An hour later Greenade missed his jacket, and immediately sur- mised that the parcel referred to was his jacket. Jordan and himself then set out and found the defendant in the Higher, Angel" wearing the overcoat in question Defendant was given in charge to P.C.Woods.The jacket was afterwards found sold at a shop in Mersey-street.— Ed ward Jordan and John Grevnade both gave corrohn rative evidence, and the defendant was sent to gaol for two months' hard labour on each charge (four mouths in all) ILLTREATMENT OF A Hossg.—Thomas Turner, Dallatn- lane, was charged with illtreating a horse on the 23rd March by causing it to be worked while in an unfit state. , —P.C. Bell deposed to seeing the horse being worked in !Froghall-lane. It wa, lou H the near foreleg.—lnspec- tor Luff, R.S.P.C.A., also proved the horse's lameness. As the animal was to be sold for lighter work, the case was adjourned for a fortnight. Morta SQUABBLING NEIGHBOURS.—EIiZabeth VVOndi.33, Morris's Court, was charged with using threats to Eliza- beth Cummings on the23rd M,lrch.—Complainant stated that defendant had threatened to blind her and 4lsn used foul language. After evidence had been given, the Bench thought there was no case and dismissed the charge. MONDAT.—B4nre the Mayor (Councillor James Psi r dough), in the chair, Alderman Harrison and Mr. F.</p>
        <p>„ Monks. A VIOLENT PRISONER.—Jr.spph Warburton, Tanners'- lane, was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Winwick-street, and also with wilfully breaking a window, valued 7s. 6d., at the Golden Ball Inn, on Sat- urday night. Pg. Turner stated that he was on duty at Town's End,and saw the prisoner drunk. Prisoner picked up a brick and threw it through the window of the Golden Ball and a man who was inside the public-hou,O. narrowly escaped being hit by it. Witness had ,reat diffic:llty in bringing the prisoner to the police station,as he lay down on the floor and had to be dragged part of the way.—The Chief Constable said that the prisoner frequently got drunk at Town's End, and seemed to think that he would get plenty of assistance.—Prisoner was fined 20s. and costs or one month. ORDERED TO LEAVE THE TOWN.—John Barratt and James Wilson were each charged with being drunk and disorderly in llorsemarket-street, on Saturday night. D.S. Monaghan and P.C. Barlow said that the two inen were near Market Gate drunk and begging from every- one they met. They were taken to the police station.— The prisoners said that they came from Stockton on Tees, and that the drink they had, had been given them by people they had met. They promised to leave the town if the magistrates would let them off.—The bench agreed to this course. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.--Mired Woods, 6„Toll.y's- yard, for being drunk and disorderly in S,hnol Brow, on Sunday, was fined ss. and costs or seven days; for the same offence committed in Mersey-street, on S,7nday night, James Morgan, Friars' Green ,was discharged with a caution. TIIRSDAY. —Before Mr. B. J. Guest (chairman) and Dr. T. S. Smith.</p>
        <p>.• • SCHOOL ATTENDLNCH CASES.—The following persons were fined at the instigation of Mr. G. Riley, for not sending their children regularly to school .m Evans, Latchford, 10s.; Walter Lunn, Dallam-lane, 55.; Henry Carter, Latchford. 25.6. L; Thomas Fearnley, liowpev-road, 61. ; James Taylor. Wakeßeld•street, 24. 61. ; Patrick Brady, lodeing house-keeper, Latch ford aq• 61.: J.m.s Sktilly, Crossley-street, 5. ; John Small- man, Bewsey-road, child ordered to attend school and defendant fined ss. and costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <p>BE WISE IN 'LIME t ' Sufferers from Or 'vet, Lumbago. Piles, Pains in the Beek, Dropsy, Wind and Water Complaint. Diseases of Kidneys, Bladder Stone. Sciatica, Rheumatism an,il Gout will find a vos.tiye C..re in Holrovi's Gravel Pills. Try a small box and if not satisfied your money will be returned. Prire is. of all Chemists. or post free for 12 stamps, from HOLROTT'S Medical Hall. Checkbeatnn, Yorks. Don't be put off. If you cannot get them write to the Proprietor and a bqx mill be nt rext post.—Adv.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 13, 1895 FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, APRIL 13, 1895 FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>RUNCDRN kEITY SEBSIONB. Monnair.—Before Sir Richard M. Brooke, and Dr. , Steele.</p>
        <p>DRUNKENNESS.—Joho Critchley, Mersev-5t., drunk and disorderly in Church-at, on the 30th Mirch, ss. and costs ; Annie Shaw, 97, Percival-lane. drunk and dis- orderly in High-et., on the 30th ult, 2s. 61. and costs ; Ann Corcoran, 11th appearance, drunk and disorderly in Mersey-st., on the Ist April, ss. and costs. DAMAGING THE POLICE STATION.—TComas Waters, who said he was a pedlar, was charged with being drunk in Bridge-st., on the 6th inst., and further charged with breaking a pane of glass at the Police Station, .value is. at the same time.—P.C. Richardson stated that at eight o'clock on Saturday night he was on duty at the Police Station when he heard a smashing of glass. On Woking out he saw the prisoner with his crutch through the Police office window. He was going to break another pane when witness stopped him. He said he had. been in all the gaols in ogland,with the exception of Knots- ford, and he wanted to be locked up in order to see what sort of a place it was.—Prisoner admitted the offence, but said the latter statement was an exaggera- ti in on his part. He asked for the fine to ne as light as possible, and he would pay it from the' money he had in the bank. The magistrates fined him is. in each case, and the costs 12s. 4d. *r^ in default 14 days' imprison- ment.</p>
        <p>BLIND CHILDREN'S EDUCATION.—An application was made by Mr. Lake on'behalf of the Runcorn School Board under the Elementary Education (Blind and Deaf Children) Act, for a payment to be levied against James Rowbottorn,towards the maintenance of a child removed from his care to the School for the Blind, Hardman-st., Liverpool. He said the man Rowbottom married a woman for his second wife, who had the child in ques- tion before he married her. They lived together until towards the end of last year when Mrs. Rowbottom died. The child Elizabeth Whittaker was in kis custody and formed part of the family at the time of the wife's death. On January 3rd laat, she was removed to the Blind Institution in Ltverpool, with Rowbottom's con- sent. He was asked to contribute towards her mainten- ance. A clause in the Act set forth that the sum to be paid should be such as agreed upon by the School Board and the parents,or if it was not possible to come to an amicable arrangement,the amount should be decided by a court of summary jurisdiction. The case had there- fore been brought for their worships to settle. Be un- derstood that the only objection raised by Rowbottom was that he was not the father of the child, and therefore not liable for its maintenance. But under the act of 1893 it pro- vided that the word "parent "included guardians and every person who was liable to maintain or had any custody of the child. The School Board had to pay £2l a year for the child. There were other parents in Run- corn who contributed from 23. to 2. 6d. per week.— James Rowbottom said he was unable to pay anything. He had Ave children to keep.—Thomas Bowyer, school attendance officer, gave evidence as to the position of the man.—G. Moss, clerk of tbe School Board, spoke as to the removal of the child to Liverpool. The Board paid £2l per year for the maintenance of the child in the Institution.—The Bench made an order for the payment of 23. a week.—Rowbottom said he would not pay it.</p>
        <p>(IEO. G. BUSSBY AND CO., the leading Sports Mannino- .X Wrens, of London, S.B. have published a most com- plete estalogue. It is bounSto interest Cricketers, Go,fers, Tmnisers, Croquet Players and ths like. If you cannot obtain a copy front the Local Dealer, send a post card to G. G. B and Co., as st-)ove. Cricketers should get the G. G. B. Diary, price 60. post free.</p>
        <p>RUGBY. WARRINGTON v. SALFORD.</p>
        <p>The return fixture between these two clubs was played on the Wilderspool Ciiround, Warrington, on Saturday, before a fair number of spectators. Jolley and Barber were absent from the Warrington side and when lined on the field Ma teams were strongly represented Warrington: Boscow, full-back; Burton, Heescm„ Cross and Moores, three-quarters; Houghton and J. T. Bate, half beaks'; Turner, D lkin, Thorniley, Saunders, Hill, Burrows, Taylor and Evans, forwards. Owing to a strong transverde wind four twenties were played and the game was commenced with Warrington defending the railway goal. Turner kicked off and a scrimmage almost immediately resulted, from which Bate got the ball but was immediately collared. After a nu sTher of other scrimmages on the 25 flag Burton broke away and raced off towards the visitors' goal.only to be knocked into touch over the half-way line. A bad throw- out from - Salford put the ball into touch and fr...m off-side play in this, Warrington were granted a free kick, which Turner availed him- self of, but the wind allowed of but little headway heing made. Salford with the wind in their favour were now pressing strongly and rushing up, made things look dangerous for the Warrington go I, but Boscow was equal to the emergency and saved foully. A. kick- dead by a red-coat tor ward gee Tamer a cnance of re- lieving and a moment later Houghton made his mark on the halt-way line. Boscow again came to the fore very shortly, and by one of his tricky dribbles managed to cross the fiat 1 with the ball, before being grassed. War- rington were next penalised and Mainwaring took the kick which Burton received and returned the ball to the 25 flag; Qaarter time then came after an interesting but unexciting 20 minutes. half ord re-started, and Barratt seizing a good oppor- tunity and the ball at the same time,dropned a neat goal, backed uo with vociferous cheers from the visiting con- tingent. Turner kicked off, and Warrington with the wind at their hicks drove ali befcire them, kicking the bail out of b-iiindary behind the visitors' goal. Near the touch line Burton macle.off with the ball and looked promising, hut he was again knocked into touch. Bos- cow followed up by making his mark, and the kick car- ried the ball well in front of the Salford goal. Nothing resulted, however;but a scrimmage saw Moores running freely and passing to Burrows that player failed to catch the ball and the chalice was lost. The oval coming with- in his reach Boscow made an attempt at goal which proved abortive. This concluded the first half of the match with Salford leading by one goal to nil. Turner re-cOmmenced against the breezb, and Barratt for Salford getting the ball, made a shot at goal which created much apprehension as the ball just caught the posts and rebounded. The same player snapped up a bad pass from Moores and ran quarter-field before being tackled. A quantity of line-outs and scrimmages took place in succession, and Stevens getting possession raced down the field, only to meet his downfall by Boscow's sure tackling. Another free kick was granted Salford which Miinwaring took, but the attempt was futile. This terminated the third quarter with nothing further scored. Salford put the ball in motion again, and Warrington were soon forcing the play. However, Stevens unex- pectedly got away from Rangley's pass, and passing all the Warrington men, went romping towards the home goal which was in imminent danger until Burton,loudly cheered, was seen overhauling the impending scorer, whom hepanaged to floor less than a dozen yards from the line. the game rolled back to the visitors' goal and loose play amour the S dford threequarters put the ball into the hands of Burton who in a brief run scored a try. Turner failed to convert. Play was of an even nature after this, and Warrington had hard lines in not scoring when near the visitors' goal. Barratt on one occasion made his mark, hut the kick was useless,and the game ended with another defeat for Warrington by, Salford 1 dropped goal, Warrington 1 try.</p>
        <p>kick went wide. At half-time Stockton were two tries against Altrincham one try. The second half was stub- bornly contested, and Barber was well attended to by Smith. Try as they would the " locals" could not score, although J. Elamnett was nearly in on one occa- sion. Towards the end Altrincham played desperately ' to pull the match out of the fire, hut this they failed to accomplish, and tasted defeat by two tries to one. Mr. ' Browning, of Sale, was the referee. WARRINGTON RANGERS T. Sits" A."—The homesters won the toss and Caldwell kicked off for the Rangers. Savage put in a good run to the line, but his final pass was not taken,a scrimmage taking place on the home line. The home forwards getting the hall away took play to the other end, and from a line Kilian scored a try for them, which was not converted. From the kick-off the visitors' half-hacks started some paszing which ended in Plinston scoring a try far out. Price made a good attempt at the difficult angle. From the drop-out the visitors' for- wards dribbled to the home lines and from a forward ru;h J. Monks scored a try, Price making a good attempt at goal. Just on the call of half-time Foden got the hall from the line-out, and eluding three of his opponents snored a grand try, which remained uncon- verted. Half-time was soon called, with the score— Warrington Rangers,three tries (nine points) ; Sale "A," one try (three points). The homesters started the game, and it was soon seen that the visitors with the wind would carry all before them. From a scrim- mage Foden kicked across to Bailey, who had no diffi- culty in walking in under the posts, Foden doing the needful. From the kick off some exciting play followed, in which Thompson charged the full backs' kick down and scored under the posts, Foden again doing the need- ful. A passing movement,started by Foden and Clarke, and taken part in by Siddall, Thompson, Bailey, and Plinston enabled the latter to score his second try, Sid- dall failhl7 at the liice. Time was soon called ,with the score—Warrington R,ters 2 goals four tries (22 points), Sale " A," 1 try (3 points).</p>
        <p>• - RUNCORN RECREATION V. SALE A SHFIELD.—PIayed at Runcorn on Saturday. A game of four quarters was played. The early part of the game was evenly con- tested, the Sale men having, slightly the best of matters, but no scoring occurred. In the second quarter, Wil- kinson scored for the Recreation. At half-time the score stood : Recreation, one try ; Sale, nil. On re- starting the visitors played up with vigour, but they were soon outplayed, and at the close of the game the home team had won bg the handsome margin of four tries, four minors, to nil.</p>
        <p>Rmacornsi v. Sr. HELENS RECS.—The St. Hel ens men visited the 1rw.,11-lane enc!osure, Runcorn, on Saturday afternoon. There were a good number of spectators. When the teams lined out they were as follows :—Ron- corn : Jolley, back ; Myers, W. Faulkner, Gregory, and Dunbavin, three-quarters ; J. Faulkner and S. Abrams, half hacks ; Lightfoot, Walker,Gayter, Langley,Farmer, Williams, Johnson, and Taylor. St. Helens Rees : Trow, hack ; Traynor Seddon, Crossley, Pilkingtou, three- quarters ; Molyneux and Orford, half backs ; Mousdale Fildes, Reynolda, Wilson, Briers, Caveney, Chapman, and Dolan, forwards. Owing to the late arrival of the visitors,a start was not made un- til 4.20. Taylor started on behalf of Runcorn, Seddon ,making a poor return. After a scrimmage at the centre Taylor got hold and got away with a run,but was soon brought down. A free kick to Runcorn took matters in the Recs. half. Some passing between Langley, Light- foot, and Farmer gained ground for Runcorn. A free kick was awarded to the visitors,play being transferred to the Runcorn end, but with short rushes the homes- ters soon got back again. Molyneux, with a free kick from the centre, kicked into touch at the Runcorn three quarter flag. From the throw out the Recs. worked up the Runcorn line,but Abrams and Johnson reliaved with ' a dribble.. Passing between W. Faulkner, Gregory and Gavter took place to the centre, where J. Faulkner broke away with a good run into the Recs.'half. Traynor got hold, and returned with a good kick,Jolley replying by a kick into touch. The Recs. now played up, and got to the Runcorn end, where passing between Moly- neux and Traynor ended in the latter scoring from the quarter flag' the same player making a good attempt at goal, the ball going wide. From the restart the Recs. got in the Runcorn half, but by good forward play the homesters got out again. Orford kicked to Jolley. the latter making his mark. Soon after the Recs. dribhled over, and Pilkington rushing up scored for the visi- tors, Molyneux failing at goal. Half time score—St. Helens, 2 tries, Runcorn nil. After the interval, some loose play followed, neither side having the advantage till W. Faulkner, with a free kick, took the ball into the R.cs. half. After some dribbling J. Faulkner got hold, and passing to W. Faulkner, the latter got over, and Myers made a good bid for goal,but the ball went short. Runcorn now had the best of the game,pressing hard on the Recs.' goal line. W. Faulkner obtained the ball,and dropped a magnificent goal, placing Runcorn ahead. Final result ; Runcor 2 goals, 1 try, St. Helens Recs.-, 2 tries.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>HOW WE SPENT GOOD FRIDAY.</head>
        <p>HOW WE SPENT GOOD FRIDAY.</p>
        <p>Easter was apppqaching, and the magic word holidays imbued us with a transient amiability. We made ar- rangements for tea parties and even contemplated a day at the seaside or in the country. We meant our break- ing up tea to be a thing of beauty, if not a joy for ever. So many suggestions were proposed, that a special rule was made. Each girl was to speak in her turn, and at all other times to hold her peace. This rule suffered a similar fate to Miss Pickmiff's ourtsey. The be3t laid plans—the trite well worn saying still holds good. On Thursday morning the following notice stared offensively at us, from the workroom door,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>EASTER HOLIDAYS.</head>
        <p>EASTER HOLIDAYS.</p>
        <p>"The warehouse and factory will be closed on Mon- day, Tuesday. and Wednesday next. No holiday on Good Friday." We stared at one another in stupefaction and anger. Such a thing had never occurred before. We must have a meeting, said Pierre breaking the awful silence. We placed a picket at the door for it was a strictly private meeting, and fell to discuss- ing this unwarrantable infringement of our rights and privileges " Cussing " it would be nearer the truth. Our indignation worked itself out, without hitting upon a practical scheme, by which we might gain our end. A dirk girl, who answered to the name of Sweep, was the first to make a sensible sugges- tion. Let us go downstairs in a body, and ask the man- ager to give us the holiday. " All very well," I said cautiously, " but who is to bell the cat. Who will be spokeswoman." There was a long piuse. Nabody cared to volunteer for a post which might entail her dismissal. Then Pierre said," You, Freda." Some have greatness thrust upon them. "Do you think I'm a fool to rush in where an angel fears to tread," I a-ked her. " Tell you what," said Kitturns briskly, " We'll all speak together ; they can't sack the lot of us." This scheme so touching in its simplicity met with the applause it deserved. We sallied downstairs arm in arm. Kittums a beink the most imposing in appearance led the way. They forced me to accompany her as being what they were pleased to term bumptious. Molly hung modestly in the rear. We filed silently into the mana- ger's roam, and approached his desk. He looked round but his retreat was cut off—there was no back door. " Well, what is it,' he demanded with a piercing look which each girl felt was directed exclusively at her. We explained our wishes backing one another up. "You can't have it," he said briefly, " Now go away like good girls, and don't bother me again." A Mournful procession re-ascended the stairs. But when it was in its own den, a second time that morning we placed a guard at the door. "You Can bring a horse to the water but you can't make him drink," I guo•ed darkly. "Meaning," laughed Molly, "You can get Freda in the workroom, hut you can't make her work." " Much rood that would do," growled Kittums, "So much the worse for ourselves on Saturday." This was undeniable and wo settled down to work in a silence that was anything hut peace. That evening my brother took me to the theatre to see " Othello." I had the additional pleasure of seeing about half my workroom mates in the audience. Go where I would it was impossible to escape them. We assembled the next morning in a state ef fractious ill-temper, and began to work without exchanging a word. But %Mums could not remain silent for long to save her life.</p>
        <p>—.„ " Will you listen if I tell you something worth hear- ing," she a3ked. We assented but not very graciously. We would liaten, because we cculd'nt help oueselves, but we were not over sanguine as to it 9 he;rig worth our while. " Well then, I've reformed." "Kittums," snapped Peirre, who sat facing her, " you're like a sheep-head—all jaw." This uncalled for remark,led to an animated dialogue, sustained by both parties with considerable spirit. 'Me rest of us cheered up wonderfuliy,until the belligerents, envying us our entertainment relapse I into sulks, and refused to open their mouths. We had tea at three o'clock that afternoon, and made short work of ali basket of cross buns. Molly brought her crochet work out of her pocket and I leaned against her, watchirr, the quiok deft fingers. " I wish I had another bun." said Kittums greedily. "Got any m6mey, Freda." "On Friday, you're dream. me, Kitton,s." I held out my empty purse. lago's declaration on the previous, " Who steals my purse, steals trash," occurred to me, and suggested a compari- son. " Girls." I said, " here is a conumdrum for you. Why am I like lago ? " Wlt as I know them, I was scarcely prepared for their prompt replies. " Because you're so spiteful," said Sweep. " And so discontented," said Molly. " And so sly," said Pierre, lifting her handkerchief to her eyes. "I know, Freddie, said Kittums, in a confidential tone, "Its because you're such an awful ready made, you know what." This last was more than mortal girl could stand, and I went for</p>
        <p>KittumS. She wal,z.‘d round the table. "Now Freddie don't be spiteful; shake hands and be friends." For apswer, I pushed her into Pierre's skip, falling in, myself on top of, her. Ihe muffled yells of the unlucky Kittums, sent the others into shrieks of laughter. " Get out," said Pierre. overturning the skip and shaking us out, "I won't have you squabbling in my house. Why don't you stop at horns, where you had the drink." The door opened, and a little errand girl appeared. "Freda. Miss North wants you." "I can't go," I said desperately, "Just look at me." "I am looking," said Molly, gasping for breath. I dashed intn the lavatory, and hurriedly washed my face. Friendly hands came to my assistance, smoothed my dress, and tidied my hair. Kittums shared a minute piece of soap with me (we had . the 5. trongest objection to that provided for our use),and in three minutes, fairly neat and respectable, I pre- sented myself befare the forewoman, who fortunately, from our point of view, had three rooms to superintend with an expression of guileless enquiry. "Freda, will you tell the girls, they can leave at once, if they like. "If we liked." I flew back to the workroom with the good news. At the door I stopped abruptly. Sweep had called me spiteful. Very well. I took my hat and jacket from their peg on the landing, and donned them, leisurely, buttoned my gloves, and tucked my utnbrella under my arm. Then I opened the door. My appear- ance in walking costume evoked a storm of disapproval. " How did you get a pass ; take your things off ; it isn't fair," they shouted in angry chorus. " Miss North says. everybody can go home that lik-s," I replied. Then I fled. And it was time—an empty jam pot grazed my plume.</p>
        <p>FRED. GREY.</p>
        <p>Nothing greater can he said in Draiv, e Scott's Flninlsioo than to tell of Its succe,sfui results wherever it Is tried. Sc tt's Emulsion strengthens everybody who takes It. ft is a Quick cure for any wasting coneition, and is especially helpful to babies and children who are thin and suffering from wasting. 4)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>WARRINGTON v. BROUGHTON RANGERS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON v. BROUGHTON RANGERS.</p>
        <p>On Monday afternoon Warrington journeyed to Braue,hton to play off their return League engagement with the Rangers of that district. The Warrington team did not turn up as advertised, Cross taking the place of Barber at three-quarter, and Swift taking the place of Eakin in the forwards. The Rangers were without Chapman. "the ball was kicked off by the Warrington captain at the Weather Field Enclosure, Lower Broughton-road, at 6.15 before about '3,000 spectators. The Raneers first began to press and soon returned. Burton handed the leather and carried it half way, where a scrimmage took place. Warrington were penalized, but nothing came of it. Warrington were now very close to the homesters' goal line,when Berry received and passing to Whitehead he enabled the latter to tr-insfer play to Warrington's goal line where the ball was kicked dead. Free kicks were granted to both sides. Whitehead again took pas- sssion and passed to Rogers, who was finally floored just on the Warrington line. The visitors then forced play to half way whrre it remained for some time. Brilliant passing by the Rangers' forwards enatiled the home- eters to carry the ball away, but only for a short period. The homesters again broke away ' and hard lines were experienced on the visitors' goal track. Half-time arrived with no score. Resuming Squires kicked off, Barton returning with great force. Play remained in pie homesters quarters for over eight minutes, the Warrington being with great difficulty kept from scoring. Warrington now gained free kicks and Turner had a shie at goal, but his shot fell wide. Houghton now received the leather from a scrimmage but was unable to make any attempt at scoring. Hacking received but was floored by Cross. Before he got his kick in, play was taken to the visitors' territory where Boscow showed grand form, he not letting a single man pass. Ileesom showed equal form by charging flacking's kick, thus enabling himself to score. The referee's decision was against the try and a scrimmage took place. A series of scrimmages and line outs now took place until the Rangers forced the ball out of danger. Boscow was again called on and checked Auderton and Oscar in their turn. Warrington con- tinued V+ press, the ball being very seldom away from the Rangers track. Open play followed up to the finish but nothing was scored. The teams were composed as follows: Warrington: Boscow, full beck; Burton, Hsesom, Bate, Cross, threequarter backs; Houghton, Fodor], half backs; Thorniley, Turner, Burrows, Saunders,Swift, Taylor,Hill,Bailey,forwards. Broughton Rangers : Hacking, full back; Berry, Rogers. Anderson and Whitehead, three-quarter backs ; Garrity and Oscar, halr-backs ; Squires. Skidmore. Shepherd, Campbell, Fitton Medley, Jackson and Medley, forwards. Referee, Mr. W. W. Higgins. Neutral touch judges, Mr. A. C. Hide (Walkden), and Mr. T. Melledew (Rochdale Hornets) '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>WARRINGTON " A " v. 94LFORD "A."</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON " A " v. 94LFORD "A."</p>
        <p>The Warrington " A" team travelled to Salford on Saturday to play the "A " team there. On arriving they found that the ground was not in condition for good running and so slippery was it that several times during tile scrimmages the players fell down in a body, to the great amusement of the large concourse of specta- tors. A very strong wind was blowing across the field. Randalls kicked off for Warrington but Salford soon re- turned the ball to the visitors' quarter line where some good passing took place among the Salford forwards. Salford was a w;lrded a fr-e kick, but the Warriegton men got the ball and took it well down the field where Cheetham saved. fleffron then made a grand rush with the ball and was not checked until he got near the touch line where Carey obtained possession of the ball and it was rushed down the fidd and a try was almost scored, the Salford full back only checking the -rush within a couple of cards of his touch line. The ball was taken back to midfield and Carey made another grand rush, but the Salford men saved just in time, as in another two strides a try would have been scored. Good play by both sides followed and was kept up till half time. On the call of half time neither side had scored. On resuming play, Rawlinson got the ball and ran down the field with it but Mannion saved, and the ball was taken to the other end, where Salford pressed strongly and almost scored a try, the player being stopped a yard off the line. Lawless and Carey got the ball and took it down the. field with a grand run, and the Salford full back (Cheetham) went to stop them,but before he could do anything to save, Carey had scored a try' and even the Salford supporters could not help applauding the fine bit of play. The kick at goal failed, as the wind carried the ball to the side. The play after that was pretty even both sides making some good runs, but scoring nothing. Time Was called with the score, Warrington A one try, . Salford A nil. The gams was very well played throughout. The following constituted the teams : Warrington A : full hack, Mannion • three-quarters, Thernton, E lelsten, Lawless and Sankey ; half backs, Carey and Wood ; forwards, Randalls (captain), Swift, Andrews, Sudlow, Makin, Bayley, Hughes and Boyle. Salford A : full back, Cheetham ; three quarters. Eccles, Rawlinson, Ellison and Oates • half backs, Heffron and Bollix ; forwards,Wilkinson,ievin, Humphries, Lawton, Shaw, Shenton, Brundrett and RAMMage. STOCKTON HEATH V. ALTRINCHAAI.—ThiS fixture WAS played at ,Altrincham in a hurricane of wind, which rendered accurate play impossible. As the teams lined out it was seen that liainnett and Abrams were missing at half and Palmer arid Smith forward. Play for a time waa fairly equal, and the game was fast and chiefly for- ward owing to the gusts of wind. Stockton Heath worked to Altrincham lines,and fligginbottoin ran in at the c hcer., No goal resulted. Scarcely had the game restsr tel ,vhen Fry came away to the Altrincham end, giving the ball to Barber, who dodged in. No goal fol- lowed the kick. With the game six points against them the " locals" laid themselvei out to prevent a repeti- tion of this kind of thing, and at once took play to Stockton end, where they had several hard lines ere Hamnett crossed their lines after capital play. Gray's</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>NEWTON-LE-WILLOWS V. PENKETH. WARRINGTON SENIOR LEAGUE.—The above match was played on the Newtcm ground on Saturday last before a moderate at- tendance. Both teams were but poorly represented, the homesters being minus Laithwaite, R. Bartley and Limood. The visitors winning the toss elected to play with a strong wind behind them, but notwithstanding this the Newton lads were the first to score, Lennox steering the ball between the sticks, well out of the reach of Bowyer. Penketh were in this half mostly on the aggressive but what with poor shooting and good goalkeepinx were only able to beat Percival once,and half time arrived with Penketh 1, Newton 1. With the assist- ance of the wind in the second half the hotnesters had all the play, and two minutes from the restart Boardman put them ahead with a grand long shot amid loud ap- plause. Percival was nearly a spectator the whole of this half, but towards the finish went amongst the for- wards, and scored a goal. This is the last match New- ton have to play in the League and undoubtedly will finish third, although they have had two points deducted in the table for playing a supposed uneligible man.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON ST. ELPHIN'S V. BARNTON ROVERS.—The match between the above clubs was played on the Arpley Cricket Ground on Saturday, it being the semi- final for the cup competition. There was a strong wind blowing during the game. The Saints kicked off and Barnton returned,Forbes relieving the pressure by a good kick, but Garnett was safe, and after a few exchange kicks in which Barnton were the better exponents, IYA,onp:•,rt scored in the first five minutes' play. Aroused by this reverse the Saints tried hard to equalize,without success. The Rovers, however, played skilfully, and after some interesting ploy Tomkinson scored shortly before half-time, which arrived with Barnton leading by two goals to nil. On resuming, the Saints went away with rore dash, but Sumner was to the fore with a grand run, combination, however, lacking in their efforts. Harper was soon afterwards applauded for rood I play, Scoring the first goal of the second half,which Rob- son just failed to clear. Interesting and exciting play followed, each team playing remarkably well,Tomkinson scoring the fourth and last goal of the match with a clever shot. Soon afterwards the whistle sounded time with the scores :—Barnton Rovers, four goals; St. Elphins, nit.</p>
        <p>NEWTON RESERVE V. PENKETH RESERVE.--This Junior League fixture came off on the Penketh ground on Sat- urday last before a good few spectators. The Newton captain won the toss, and elected to play with the wind behind him,and taking advantage cf the same were soon swarmin round the Penketh custodian, Houghton de- feating him with a grand shot two minutes from the start. Still keeping up the pressure, the same player was not long in adding another from a corner well planed by Beresford. One of the backs conceded a corner and Berestorel placing the bill well in front of goal Limond with a swift shot notched a third point. Houghton scored the fourth goal for the visitors. The interval arrived with Newton Reserve leading by 4 eoals to 0. The game ended leaving the visitors winners by 4 goals to O.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT,</head>
        <p>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT,</p>
        <p>MONDAY. -THE LOCAL VETO BILL.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons a new writ was granted for Mid-Norfolk in the room of Mr. CLEMENT HIGGINS,WhO I has accepted the Chiltern Hundreds. The SPEAKER rose in a crowded House, and in a feel- ing and dignified speech announced his reluctant re- signation of his office on considerations of health which he could not overlook. Sir E. GREY informed Mr. DARLING that the Argen- tine G overnment had fulfilled its treaty obligations,and that a British police officer was starting on board the Tartar Prince in charge .of ABEL BALFOUR. Sir WlLLtem HARCOURT informed Sir J. LEKG that he had no power to demand from banks a return of un- claimed deposits. Sir WILLIAM HARCOURT, in asking leave to introduce a Bill to establish control over the traffic in intoxicating liquors, said it was with certain amendments substan- tially the same Bill which he previously brought before the House. The present drink traffic was the outcome of the present condition of our law, and if it was to be remedied, it must 13.3 by legislation. Sir W. HARCOURT afterwards outlined his measure which he said w ould properly be described as the Local Option Bill, be cause it offered other options besides that of veto. The requisi • tion would be one-tenth of the parish electors. The poll would be taken by the local authorities. The areas in the boroughs would be the wards, and in the rural dis- tricts the parishes or wards of parishes. A prohibitory resolution might be carried by two-thirds of the electors and it would come into force at the date of the annual licensing meeting which oc- curred next after the expiration of three years from the commencement of the Act, or tho expire- piration of one year from the date of the poll, whichever occurred first. In order that the community should not be constantly disturbed, no future poll would be taken for three years. The majority for the repeal of a prohi- bitory resolution would be a simple majority. Sir E. CLARKE did not think the curious and complicated</p>
        <p>E.)cine of the Bill would :receive anylarge support either I° the House or the country. Sir WILFRID LAWSON re- gretted the exemptions specified and the non-application to Ireland,: hut said the Bill would receive the Qtror.g support ot the United Kingdom Allianee. Mr.amoltOSE, Mr. EVERSHED, Mr. MONEY-WIGRAM, Mr. R. COOKE and the Marquis 4 CARMARTHEN,: oppo,,ed the Bill ; while Mr. BROADHURST, Mr. JOHN WILSON (Govan), and Mr. W. S. CAINE supported it. The Bill was read a first time at midnight without a division.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
