      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>TUESDAY</head>
        <p>TUESDAY</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Tuesday evening, Mr. ASQUITH informed Mr. FIELD that it had been decided to liberate an Irish political prisoner named WCANN, who had been twelve years in prison, and was now seventy years of age. Sir W. HARCOURT, in answer to J. H. ROBERTS, said he hoped the Light Railways Bill would be brought in on an early date after Easter. Mr. ASQUITH, replying to a question put by Mr. WOODS, said that so far as he was aware the" particulars dance" in the new Factory Bill covered all the grievances com- plained of, au] hs could not at Lids stage pledge either to propose or accept an enlargement of its scnp“. Sir W. HARCOURT moved the resolution thanking Mr. PEEL for his services as Speaker of the House. He aid an eloquent tribute to the high qualities of the right h,,n. gentleman, and said he added fame to a name amongst the most illustrious in the annals of the House of Com- mons. Mr. BALFOUR seconded the motion, also in terms of warm praise ; and Mr. Justin M'CARTHY supported it 1 in a speech in which he said his Irish Nationalist collea- gues associated themselves gladly with the testimonial which the House was about to offer to Mr. PEEL. Mr. CHAMBERLAIN and Mr. J. REDMOND expressed hearty oc- currence in the sentiments which had been uttered,and, the SPEAKER having made his acknowledgment the reso- lutinn was passed amid loud cheers. A further resolution was passed for an address to the QUEEN asking Her Majesty to confer some signal mark of her Royal favour on the retiring Speaker,and assuring her of the readiness of the House to meet any expense incurred thereby. The House adjourned shortly after midnight. Mr. PEEL was loudly cheered as he left the chair for the last time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AD</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AD</p>
        <p>ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>FOOTBALL,</p>
        <p>_ Warrington have during the week added one each to their losses and draws, and I am sure it would be diffi- cult to find another team who have made so many draws as they have. In fact the match on Saturday was almost a draw, for Salford only won by four points to three. Salford have now repaid Warrington for the beatings they received last season, for they then lo3t both matches, but now the boot is on the other lee. Warrington have, however, had hard lines fn each match with Salford this season, lor at Salford they were beaten by a place goal to a try, while at Warrington, they were beaten by a drop goal to a goal. There is always an element, of luck in obtaining a goal, and Salford had that luck on each time of asking. It thus COMPS about that. although Salford have won twice, they have only scored three points more than Warring- ton in the two games.</p>
        <p>The game with Broughton Rangers ended in a point- less draw, and Warrington thus proved that they can play a good game if they choose. The Rangers have been going so strong of late that another defeat for War- rington was confidently looked forward to. Broughton Rangers have during the past few weeks beaten Swinton and Wigan, and on Saturday they defeated Rochdale Hornets to the tune of two goals, five tries to a goal. Warrington have therefore cause to shake hands with themselves that they escaped defeat. This was the third meeting of the two clubs this season. At War- rington, the home team won by a try to nil, while the result of the two garr.es which have been played at Broughton were exactly alike.</p>
        <p>The game at Broughton on Monday was a very excit- ing and well contested one, and Warrington ought to have won, for Ffresom got over the line, but the referee would not allow the point. They also had the hest of the game all through. This match brings the fixtures of Warrington in the Lancashire Rugby Competition to a close and the following is the table up to Monday night : Agst.</p>
        <p>For.</p>
        <p>Pld. Won. Lost. Drn. G.T. G7.;F:13-ts. Tyldesley Oldham ll . 6 3 . 2 . 8 18... 2 11...14 St. Helens lO . 6 3 . 1 . 8 10... 3 6...13 Warrington Swinton Broughton Rangers Rochdale Hornets Leigh (on September 19), Salford (October 16), and Wigan (November 13),having been suspended for professionalism, will not take any part in the competition this season.</p>
        <p>It will be seen from the table that Warrington are at present fourth, and of course they can rise no higher. The only club, however, which can pass them is Brough- ton Rangers, who are two points behind, but have still three more fixtures. As these are with the three top clubs, it is not very probable that they will get many points out of them.</p>
        <p>The question of the championship is still unsettled, owing to the defeat of Tyldesley by Oldham on Saturday. Tyldeslov and St. Helens have the best chance of carry- ing it off, and of the two the former are strong' favour- ites. It would be a feather in the cap of St. Eielens to win the championship, for they would then have been the champions of both divisions of the league in succes- sive years. However, if Tvldesley do not loss their two succeeding matches, neither St. Helens nor Oldham can get to the top.</p>
        <p>The weather is now getting much too warm for foot- ball, but nevertheless, Warrington have a full list of fixtures for Eastertide. Thr e st'ff matches have to be gone through—Runeorn, St. Helens RCS., aad Lancas- ter, and if the players are not heartily tired after these, then they must he inade of iron.</p>
      </div>
