      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>tetters to the Obttoe.</head>
        <p>tetters to the Obttoe.</p>
        <p>FREE TRADE. Sir,—We have it on Colonel Howard Vincent's autho- rity, whoever that evidently great and learned gentle man may be, " that our former customers are doing their own work now,"and he seems to believe that if we stop them from sending some of it here, it will put mat- ters once more on the same footing as in years gone by, "when everything was in England's favour." But there is a great doubt about that. And if we succeeded not in stopping the foreign trade by putting on the tariff I should feel curious to know who is going to pay for the extra officials that would very prJbably be employed. Would it be advisable for us to petition Parliament to make U 3 all pay more for cur goods and do away mow and tor ever with the words " Made in Germany," and they may as well at the same time in- tercede with our masters for us to have more wages so as to meet the greatly increased expenses that we shall in every way incur. And then we might have the satis- faction of knowing that after some dealers had made a good profit out of the perishable goods already sold they might consign the rest under the soil of the nearest field, when it might have goneto the poor house with little or no harm to the feelings of anyone. Now, as the noble Colonel is rather fearful that the people will begin to, rouse themselves and show that they have yet a will 9f their own, let him mind how he tries to win their love. There is the railways question yet to face, and mining royalties,with better management of land generally and nationally, for I can well guess,although I do not know, that he is a Tory; and let him bear ever in mind and conscience that there is a medium in everything.— Yours, etc.,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>WORKING-MAN.</head>
        <p>A WORKING-MAN.</p>
        <p>A CRY FOR PROTECTION. Sir,—llaving read the article in your issue of last Saturday on Mr. Mather's scheme for the unemployed, will you permit me to state that I think it would have • been better for this country if the people and the govern- ment had fostered by every possible means our manu- factures and the produce of our colonies, instead of en- couraging or failing to discourage events that have re- sulted in other countries supplying our colonies with so many articles which we manufacture and formerly sup plied them with. Did not the coal strike cause us to loose a considerable amount of trade that we are not likely to recover ? Was not the cotton strike that pre- ceded it very injurious ? What will be the result ef the strike now existing in the boot trade? Calamitous as -such strikes may be,all suggestions of arbitration °eight to come from the disagreeing parties, and persons hold_ log or seeking public positions ought not to be the arbi 1 • trators, otherwise political capital or popularity may be unfairly and unscrupulously gained. I sincerely hope that those whom we call workingmen will not be misled by the ambitious seekers for public position or popular- ity. I hope they will not be by parasites upon the pub- lic weal betrayed.. In consequence of these events other countries have been and are supplying those who used to beour customers,and finding employment for their people whilst our workmen are uaemployed and in poverty. Our colonies—lndia,Australia, New Zealand and Canada —are splendid commercial hunting grounds for Germany and other countries, though we have recently placed an Indian tax upon certain qualities of cotton manufac- tured in Lancashire, in consequence of which,if a state- ment in the Liverpool "Echo " is correct, a mill has been stopped in Preston and about 400 persons are thrown ut of employment. We seem unable to understand the injury done to India by the demonetisation of silver and a falling rupee. If the countries I have named had been German Colonies a prohibitive tax would have been placed on British naanu- facture, for the Germans in self interest wisely protect their labour and encourage their manufactures, and by so doing lessen the number of their unemployed. If Germany had never protected her manufactures she would not now have been such a formidable competitor with us in neutral markets. She would not have been supplying to:our Colonies such large quantities of goods. If thirty years ago the wisest policy was to admit un- taxed and free nearly every article to this country and our colonies it does not follow that it is so now. Scientists tell us that those creatures that cannot ac- comodate themselves to altered conditions often perish, Mid the people of this country are suffering because we have not adapted ourselves to changing and existing con- ditions. If we had in time acknowledged, realized and acted wisely under the conditions affecting us, I think there would have been less need for Mr. Mather's scheme ~sf colonies for the unemployed, which if they come into existence will, I fear, in some degree lessen the income of some portion of our countrymen, and they will be monuments of the nation's misery and symptoms of its decay:—Yours, kc.,</p>
        <p>S. KENYON.</p>
        <p>Warrington, April 4th 1995. --- WANTED A PUBLIC CLOCK.—A UNIQUE ECBENIE. Sir,—lt has often struck me that there is perhaps no other town of the same importance in the Kingdom, so badly supplied in the matter of public clocks as War- rington. The clock in the church spire near Market Gate can, owing to its being choked up with other buildings, be seen only from an area of a few yards of the public streets ; so this is practically of no use as a public clock. The only one of any public utility then is the advertisement clock ;of Eustance's, in Sankey St. ; and this can he seen only from a limited portion of this one street. The fact is that at present one can walk through about ninety-nine-hundredths part of the town without seeing a public clock, or having any means of ascertaining the time. unless he happens to have a re- liable ticker himself. This is surely most deplorable,in a town of the size andimportance of Warrington. A very original and unique method of supplying this defect has been obtruding itself within my pericranium for the last month or so of week-days. It is, that a quadrupedal iron structure should be ejected at the junction of the four principal streets at Market-gate. The four legs could rest on the four adjacent corner- walls, and the structure could be brought together,over- head the centre of the cross-streets, like a small Eifel! tower. In fact the whole style and architecture could be, in a measure, after the model of the Eifell tower— but, of course, infinitely simpler. A clock-dial could he fixed on each of the tower sides facing Bridge-street, Sankey-street, Borsemarket-street, and Buttermarket, street, respectively ; and, with the tower made of a suitable height, and the dials of a sufficient diameter,the time could, I think, he read from most if not all parts of the town. A scheme of this sort, efficiently carried out. would,in my opinion,afford the maximum of usefulness, convenience, and ornamentation, with the minimum of expense. The structure, being of iron, could no doubt be erected by Warrington firms; and it Would thus also afford a magnificent advertisement for the iron works -of Warrington. Beaides all the advantages enumerated, it would con- stitute a permanent and unique attraction to the town, a commodity which ought certainly to be no unwelcome 'addendum ta our civic pessessions. One chief objection which may be urged against the -erection of such a structure at this place, is that in rase -these streets were ever widened ,the tower would be ren- dered useless. Well, in the first place, as against this objection, it is likely to be a long time before the money can be afforded to widen such a congested and import- ant part of the town, and in the next place, the expand- ing legs of the structure could be 83 formed that by ad- ditions thereto, they could be conveniently further expanded, to he still available for the streets in their widene I cindition. This is the only sort of structure that could be erected in any central and suitable part of Warrington, without blocking up the already congested traffic, dm. My pro- posed tower could not, of course, cause any impediment to traffic (or even to navigation in the Ship Canal), but would be a stately sight to he seen fram every part in, and far around, the town ; and, most important of all, the cost would not, I feel sure, be very considerable.— Yours sincerely,</p>
        <p>ADAM NEWSENSE.</p>
        <p>i, Ist April, 1895.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>rarrington,</head>
        <p>rarrington,</p>
        <p>SOCIALISM VERSUS INDIVIDUALISM TTIE JUiTICE OF RESTITUTION. Sir,—The first part nf our prozramme in the nationali- sation of the land,or the restitution to the people f that which was thPirs,which was takan from them by force,</p>
        <p>Weak mothers will never know what reel relief means until they t ike Scott's If-ulsion This wonderful remedy strengthens and restores a normal condition of lth quicker and more effectively than any other. It 13 three times as siteetive as plain Cod-liver 011. (3)</p>
        <p>backed by laws made by the robbers for their own pur- pose and benefit. If Tony wishes to call that robbery, on our part it won't make any difference, we will get it all the same, and will not feel any qualms of conscience. Whatever is necessary for the life of a nation must and will be done, and we cannot do any longer with the pre- sent system of land tenure. No longer can we allow the workers to be divorced from the land. Land is the I mother.and labour the father of all wealth.</p>
        <p>Tony makes statements at random, which I challenge him to prove. He asserts that socialistic colonies have been started and failed. That is false. Certainly some well sneaning comrades have attempted to rescue num- bers of starving gutter snipes from the slums of large cities, but failed in the attempt through want of proper means and the want of co-operation under proper direc- tion of experienced practical agricalturalists,and several other reasons. That is no argument against Socialism and nobody but a sophist would put it forward as such. Practical men on both sides of the House of Commons know very well how the unemployed question could be definitely settled in a profitable way to the na- tion, but they will not help to do it. Why ? Because when poverty is exterminated excessive indiv- dual wealth will die. Therefore, the wealthy knowing ones will obstruct the seAlement of the unemployed pro- blem with all their might. The only way to settle the question of poverty, misery and starvation, consequent on the existing system is to allow the workers free aeceis to the land. The rich know it and oppose it, still wish- ing to parade as benevolent Christians. The workers want no charity but justice. Almsgiving is degrading to the giver and to the reciever. Give the workers that which is their own by right, and there will be no need of charity. One item of the Social Democratic programme is, the production and distribution of wealth to be regulated by the Community in the common interest of all its mem- bers ; the administration of justice to he free to all ; the abolition of standing army and the est iblishment of a citizen force ; the people to decide on peace or war ; legislation by the people in such wise that no project of law shall become binding till accepted by the majority of the people ; annual Parliaments ; adult suffrage ; pro- portional representation ; second ballot ; abolition of the monarchy and the House of Lords ; the establishment of adequate pensions for the aged poor and infirm, any person attaining the ago of SO, to be kept by the Corn- munity,work being'optional after that age. Our programme is not the Newcastle programme, but it is a honest programme notwithstanding all that Tony and his confrerei can say to the contrary, and it will recommend itself to all clear, intelligent, and honest minds. As our Programme includes the repudiation of the National Debt, perhaps Tony will scream a little louder at that than at the nationalisation of the land. As we have ample justification for all we project to do, I will now leave Tony to his own resources and fertile imagination to point out where we are wrong.—Yours fraternally,</p>
        <p>A. BROUCHEMITE.</p>
        <p>ST. PET R'S SCHOOLS, JOHN-STIVET.</p>
        <p>Sir,—The managers of the above school have had for some considerable time the question of the requironaents of the Education Department, and recent developments are of such a serious nature that they think it advisable to bring the matter before the public, thereby giving them an opportunity of assisting the managers to do what is required._ . . _</p>
        <p>Two years ago Her Majesty's Inspector reported that the infants were "crowded into two little class-rooms which afford altogether insufficient accommodation for them, they were very well taught and disciplined con- sidering the difficulties," and the Education Department in their report called upon the managers to take "steps for the provision of better accommodation at an early date." An appeal was then made to the residents and .entry of the town for monetary assistance to (enable the managers to provide what was demanded. The re;ult of that appeal was £76 183. The sum actually required for the alterations was between £3OO and £4OO. Plans of the proposed structural alterations were at the time laid before • the authorities, and the same were approved. The school mana- gers were, however, unable to complete the work for want of funds, and they therefore expended what bad been collected in doing a proportion of the alteration demanded. The Education Department are urging the managers to complete the work as then submitted. The advisability of utilizing the building known as the " Workmen's Club " ia John-street has been under dis- cussion. Plans were submitted to the authorities in Lonclon,and they have after some correspondence stated their decision viz.:—" That the building is not suitable for permanent recognition inasmuch as it is simply a large room in a house in the same street as the mixed school about 50 yards distant ; there is no ground what- ever excepting a small yard belonging to the caretaker's house," but that they would "however consent to rocog- nise the ground floor room only for two years as provid- ing temporary accommodation for 94 infants, Previded the managers will undertake to replace it by permanent accommodation by the expiration of that period." Before replying we think it desirable to place the matter through the press before the public of Warrington. A further appeal will be made to the residents of the town for assistance, and the decision of the managers will depend upon the response to such appeal. We are anxious to complete the work as shown on the original plans. It may be pointed out that if the response does not come up to their expectations then the managers will be obliged to decline to accept the terms of the Department. The consequence of this would be that the school would have to revert back to an "infant school" pure and simple, and by this action 200 children would be thrown upon the town and accommodation would have to be found for these children in the present schools, otherwise the Corporation would no doubt he called upon to provide school buildings. A sum of not leas than £4OO will be required for structural alt.rationa and furniture. Help must be speedy, or it will come too late.—We are, on behalf of the managers, yours truly, B. PERCIVAL, JOFIN ROBERTS, P. EDGAR.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>VACCINATION QUESTION.</head>
        <p>THE VACCINATION QUESTION.</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln your report of the vaccination meeting at Penketh, I am described as "sitting on the fence." Well. I am still there, and I think the proper position for all who attended Lieut• Gen. Phelp's lecture and heard the promise Dr. J. Guest Gornall made to lecture in an- swer thereto. Now I, in common with many more in , Warrington, think it well to withhold judgment upon the Imatter until this counter-blast is given. Meanwhile I am ia full sympathy with those who regard compulsory vaccinatioa as compulsory defilement, and hold with ! them that the State has no right to compel anyone to thus defile the bodies of themselves and children with the accompanying risk of the invaccinalinn of such a loathsome disease as syphilis. Altogether the Anti- vac's have given the Pro-vae.'s some very hard nuts to crack, and in the interests of free enquiry, I hope Dr. J. G. Gornall will not lot judgment go by default In the case of such a reply I earnestly, deprecate the LISP of language on either side likelv Cr, arouse embittered feel- ing. For instance, let not the word "faddist "he heard for alas there are medical" fads" which are immoral alike in their inception and practice.:—TrulT, JOHN PEERS. " The flaws," Penketh, Lancashire, 13,..h April, 1895.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>ANTI-TOXIN AND ITS RISKS.</head>
        <p>ANTI-TOXIN AND ITS RISKS.</p>
        <p>Sir,—lt is constantly declared hv the inventors of the serum system ofltrpating diphtheria that if the anti-toxin does no good it will at least do no harm—that it is harm- less ; but Dr. Flannesmann, an assistant of the distin- guished Virchow, says" that the serum injections could by no means be considered harml s,as affections of the I kidneys had frequently followed, in one case more severe in tyne than had ever vet been observed in diphtheria ; and Virchow himself, at 3 meeting of the German Medi- cal Society held in Berlin, while urging physicisns to employ the remedy, added that there was the pocsihilitv of certain attendant effects of an injurious character. The anti-toxin may also contain the germs of glanders or other diseases, which hy inoculation may be given to the natient ; and the risk is not to be overlooked. The Royal Commission on vaccination, now sitting, is tning to recommend, among other recommendations, I under- stand, a pure lymph (that is, according to the ideas of the Commissioners, a lymph free from blond), as pro- vacci nal evidence on the subject goes to prove that it is owing to the had lymoh (by which term is meant a lymph not free from blood), that vaccination is attended with grave risks, and frequently followed by disease, in many cases with a direct fatal ending ; hut here in this preparation, anti-toxin, it is the blood that is taken. Supposinv that anti-toxin is al that its discoverers claim for it, is not the remedy likely to ho the greater evil of the two? Faithfully yeurs, M. It. COLLINSON. Hendon, Middlesex.</p>
        <p>SOCIALISTS AND LAND NA.TION ALI ZATION Sir,—To begin with a question. I would ask " When is the name Tong to be separated from misrepreserita- tion ? " I said the other week that I had never heard Of Socialists wanting to divide the land among families, and he. twists it round and makes me appear to say that I had never heard of the taking of another man's pro- perty socialistically described as robbery. Now I like a good argument, and am prepared in my humble way to thrash the question nut with Tony, but I must ask him to stop twisting and turning and give us some facts to prove that Socialism will not do what we say it will, and I will try to give some to show that it will. For my part I don't think that railing at Socialism, and blackballing Socialists will stop the cause from advanc- I ing, but some good solid facts might upset the move- ' ment and throw us back where we started from. Our friend grumbles at me in his last letter about taking my autb,rity all from one side as I will try to contradict one statement he makes with authority from a different standpeint. He says the country is over popu lated and that is ens cause of the poverty and•misery- armsnd us. Now in Floyle's " Sources of Wealth " it is stated that there are 50 million acres of average quality land in the British Isles available for agriculture. Lord Lauderdale estimates that one acre of land will feed four people which would make our islands feed 200 millions of people, so if we drop the estimate by one half we</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
