      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>Ta E EXAMINER, SATUR D Ann, 13, 1895.</head>
        <p>Ta E EXAMINER, SATUR D Ann, 13, 1895.</p>
        <p>hall still have a good margin to go on with. The above estimates are based on ordinary farming, but Prince Kropotkin states that with scientific farming the crops could be doubled or trebled in a few years time. Now, if Tony has any better authorities than these I hope he will bring them out and let us examine them, and let the readers of the " Examiner " judge for themselves which is right. Warrington, April 10th, 1895.</p>
        <p>HECTOR.</p>
        <p>WHERE—RATHER THAN "WHICH—IS THE TRUE CHURCH ?" Sir,—There appears to be Church trouble in the air ; the atmosphere is heavy with the portentious rumbling of ecclesiastic thunder. The sounds of strife come from church defenders and those against whom they are array- ed. Meanwhile we, who are lookers on, are asking our- selves whether, apart from the very earthly element of "livings"&amp;c.,any church is worth defending and whether there be any such an institution as a true church at all? In this use of the word Church let me be understood to mean only the concrete institutions so celled—as the Greek, Roman, Anglican and Non-conformist churches. To the first three only I wish to draw attention at pre- sent—the younger Non-conformist Church, not having as yet attained to the unenviable distinction of age and iniquity. Given time and opportunity and I have no doubt of its ability to follow the bad precedent of the other elder and more hardened sinners. All the three so-called Christian Churches claim for themselves Jesus of Nazareth as their founder and exemplar. Now it is nothing to our present purpose that there is no sufficient evidence to' be found of the actual existence of this ideal being ; let it be ours to answer the question " Does anyone of the three follow the ideal of the lowly Jesus?" In what way can the "pomp and circumstance," the annoyance and intolerance of say the Greek Church—the richest corporation in the world—be said to be a following of the ideal of Him who had not where to lay His head, whose whole life, as sketched in the Scriptures, inculcated poverty and humility as essential to the Christian ideal. "It is easier for a camel to go through the eye of a needle, than a rich man to enter into the Kingdom of God." As Corporations but intensify, sustain, and consolidate the evils of the individuals—the Greek Church at least may be said to be in a " parlous state." And does the black record of impartial history give a better show for the Roman Church ? Would this insti- tution, with its roots deep in Pao•animn, with a thin varnish of Christian gloss, with its florid ritual and pompous obsorvancies—would this have met the views of Him who had but the fields and the canopy of heaven the temple not made with hands "—for His minis- trations, His precept and example ? Would not utter repudiation be the mead of the self-styled vicars of Christ and Sovereign Pontiffs who thus make a parody of His teaching,if the actual Teacher were to re-visit the earth? _</p>
        <p>As to the Anglican Church. If its own doctrines be true they afford sufficient condemnation of the church on the grounds above mentioned. Hence, from their own showing,there must be a warm corner in store for plethpric dignitaries. Then where is the true church—or is there any such P And echo answers, in bitter mockery, " where 2" Cn.- tainly I know it not. For instance. lam quite uncon- vinced by the " arguments' advanced by the gentle- man styling himself Aug. J. Hilton, humble and defer- ential as they are. It is amusing and instructing to see the bland assumptions of this writer—by which he can appropriate the scriptural quotations he gives as prov- ing the Church of Rome to be the Church of Christ— quite ignoring the fact of their being just as applicable to the Greek Church as his, notwithstanding the equal pretention of the other inflated humbug to make this claim. So I conclude witti " lona " that if there be a Church of Christ it is an invisible,unritualised one with- out pope, presbyteryor priest, and consequently without priestcraft. But if Jesus himself was a myth- it can but he that His Church must be one also. Learned men seem to incline to this opinion. For myself,and for once, I am Agnostic, and do not know.—Yours truly, JOHN PEERS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>EA.HLESTO WIN IND lti E WTON.</head>
        <p>EA.HLESTO WIN IND lti E WTON.</p>
        <p>THE EASTER HOLIDAYS.</p>
        <p>For the Easter holidays the Viaduct Works will close on Thursday evening and re-open on Tuesday morning. The Vulcan will close at the same time and re-open on Wednesday morning, the same holiday being observed by Messrs. Evans and Co. at Haydock. The other works will close en Thurs- day evening and re-open on Tuesday morning. THE REV. F. E. MILLER.</p>
        <p>On Sunday the Rev. F. E.-Miller, of Earlestown, occupied the pulpit at Myrtle-street Church, during the whole of the day's services. THE LIBERAL CANDIDATE FOR NEWTON DIVISION.</p>
        <p>At a private ,meeting of the Executive of the Liberal 300 of the Newton Parliamentary Division, Mr. James Moon,lnspector of the Prudential Assur- ance Society, Liverpool, signified his acceptance of the incitation of the Executive to be their midi; date at the next general election. The acceptance is conditional upon the organisation of the Divi- sion being put into a perfect condition, and this is now being proceeded with. EARLESTOWN BAPTIST CRICKET CLUB.</p>
        <p>This club has secured the ground of the liarles- town Football Club, for this season. They have secured a good list of fixtures, and they exp Pot a successful season. At a meeting held in the li.ip- tist Schoolroom on Tuesday evening, the question of forming a tennis section connection with the club was discussed. There s a good number of young ladies present, who ri i lieu their intention of giving it their simport.</p>
        <p>..1111 VICAR Ot. &amp; VDOCK.</p>
        <p>We understand that arran;zements have been made for the Rev. C. E. Bowdon, M.A., vicar of Haydock, to exchange livings with the Rev. r. Lawson of Epping, Essex, about the end ot next month,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>PROMOTION FOR SERGEANT LONGWORTH.</head>
        <p>PROMOTION FOR SERGEANT LONGWORTH.</p>
        <p>Sergeant Longworth--,ie;s;-t-Z.-h has received a noti- fication from the Chief Constable of Lancashire, that he has been promoted to an In-pectorship, as from the 6th inst. At present, however, it is not known to what position of the division he will be transferred. Sergeant Longworth has been located at Newton something like seven years, being formerly sergeant at Bootle, until that place became a corporate borough, when he was tran— 'erred to Leigh, and thence to Newton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>BIIDDEN BEATH OF AN EARLESTOWN MAN.</head>
        <p>BIIDDEN BEATH OF AN EARLESTOWN MAN.</p>
        <p>On Monday night a man named William Schofield of Newton Common, Earlestown. died very sud- denly at St. Helens railway station, at the age of 40 years. The deceased had been to the St. Helens theatre, and hurried to catch the last train home. He entered a compartment, and after seating him- self, appeared to be in a fainting condition. A rail- way official was called, and had the man removed to a waiting room where he almost immediately expired.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>GOOD TEMPLARY.</head>
        <p>GOOD TEMPLARY.</p>
        <p>The usual weekly session of the Hope of Earles- town lodge of Good Templars, was held in the Co-operative Hall, Earlestown, on Monday evening. Bro. J. Miller, CT., presided, and there was a very large attendance of members, and a few visitors. One candidate was admitted, and one restored to membership. Several items of business were tran- sacted and others referred to the next session. Arrangements having been made for an open meet- ing, the lodge was formally closed at 8.30, when there was an influx of visitors, the room being crowded. Bro. Miller again presided, and was responsible for the programme. After a few open, ing remarks of an interesting character in which he referred to the principle of the Good Templars order, and the necessity for young people especially to unite with the body, the programme was proceeded with. Songs were given by Sisters A. IN ewman, H. Hill, L. Barcroft and Bro. John Huddle.ston, recita- tions by Bro. G. E. Warburton and J. Miller, selec- tions on the fairy bells by Bro. D. Patten, piano duet, by Sisters H. Hill and E. Blair, who also acted as accompanists. SALVATION ARMY, EARLBSTOWN CORPS. . .</p>
        <p>There has been great rejoicing in connection with the local corps of the Salvation Army, during the last week end. On Saturday there was "a great birthday ham tea," in the barracks, which was well attended, and alter an open air meeting on the market ground, there was a special affair in the barracks, led by Major Howe of the Liverpool dis- trict. On Sunday morning there was "knee drill," over 50 being present, and during the day there wer3 "Land of Canaan'. and "Red Sea" meetings. At night Major Howe gave an address on "Social sores; quacks and remedies." There were two con- verts. On Monday afternoon there was a holiness conference, and in the afternoon there was an open air meeting on the market place. An adjournment was made to the Wesleyan Chapel. where a dedica- tion service was held, the infant daughter of Cap- tain and Mrs. Horn being dedicated to the army. The chapel was crowded, and the service were con- ducted by Major Howe, assisted by Staff-Captain Hargreaves, Lieutenant Joy, and 32 other officers from corps in the Liverpool Division. The musical selections were given by the united bands of War- rington, St. Helens and Enrlestown. 1.0.G.T.</p>
        <p>The usual weekly session of the Earlestown Ebenezer Lodge No. 752 was held in the Co-opera- tive Hall on Tuesday evening and was presided over by Bro. J. Taylor E.T., there being a good attendance of members end visitors and also Bro. Frances C.E T. from the Great Heart Lodge St Helens. Minutes of last Session were read and adopt- ed. One candidate was admitted to membership. The programme of the evening was then proceeded with. Single members being responsible there was a number of songs, readings, and recitations given by various Bros. and Sisters, Bro Francis C.E. superintendent, then gave a very interesting and instructive address which was very attentively listened to. Bro. Francis also tendered to the Kar- lestown Ebenezer Lodge the Fraternal Greetings of the St. Helens Great Heart Lodge which was heartily accepted, and Bro. Francis was asked to c•Ynvey the same from the Earlestown Ebenezer Lodge to his Lodge. Bro. J. Taylor C.T., then closed the Lodge after which the married members and visitors sat down to a supper which was provided by the single members and was quite a surprise,and was very much enjoyed by all, a hearty vote of thanks was then tendered to the single members for their excellent programme.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES-WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES-WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>WYCLIFFE CONGREGATIONAL CHURCH.</p>
        <p>- - - - On Sunday, the pulpit of the above church, morning and evening, was occupied by the Rev. John Yonge. It being Palm Sunday, the sutject of the morning's dis- course was the follgwing text : " Behold thy King cometh unto thee," in which he referred to the coming of Jesus Christ to Jerusalem, and the strewing of the ground with polm branches. There were good congre- gations, and the service were much enjoyed. Special music was rendered in an admirable manner. BOLD-STREET PEOPLE'S SERVICES. - -</p>
        <p>There was a large attendance at the People's Service held in the Bold-street Wesleyan Church on Sunday afternoon. The meeting was conducted by the Rev. Owen Watkins. Alter the service had been opened in the usual manner and a portion of scripture had been read, a quartette was rendered by Miss Jean, Miss M. Belly, Mr. Wild and Mr. W hire. Mr. W. A. Sandford was then called upon and delivered an interesting and thoughtful address from the wor le "Am I my brother's keeper ?" After referring to the importance of the question to each one of them, he pointed out that having been endowed by God with certain qualities and abilities. they were very responsible beings, not only to themselves, but also to one another. Duty demanded of them that they should in a sense be their brother's keeper. In God's sight they were all one family knit together in sacred brotherhood. It was their duty to be, not a hindrance, but a help to their fellow creatures. There was no medium between the two. It was impossible for them to be neutral in this matter. They were by their words and by their actions either leading others nearer to Heaven and to God or further away. A selection of music was then rendered by the orchestral band in an excellent manner and a very enjoyable service was brought to a close.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>MR. GLADSTONE AND TEIE ARMENIAN</head>
        <p>MR. GLADSTONE AND TEIE ARMENIAN</p>
        <p>ATROCITIES.</p>
        <p>At the Chester Town Council, on Wednesday the Mayor (Mr. W. li. Churton) intimated that he had com- municated with Mr. Gladstone to ascertain whether, if a meeting were held is Chester for the purpose of protest- ing against the horrible outrages in Armenia, he would be prepared to attend it. b.e had received the following postcard from Mr. Gladstone in reply : Mv dear Sir,—l heartily wish well to the purpose of your meeting, and shall do all in my power to promote the cause of justice in Armenia. But I have been obliged with regret to make it my rule to decline all public meetings and celebrations whatever, for which! am no longer Wed —Believe me, yours faithfully, W. E. GLADSTON/.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>VACCINATION.</head>
        <p>VACCINATION.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l have read with much interest your very full report ef the recent debate at Penketh, and,as this ques don appears to he exciting very general attention in Four district. I trust you will permit me to make a few comments thereon. .</p>
        <p>Dr. Gornall is evidently a medical Rip,van-Winkle, or he would recognise that the time had gone by when opponents of vaccination could be stig,matised as "ignor- ant anti-vaccinators." Nor can they he curtly dismissed as" knowing nothing from practical experience," when such leaders of the medical profession as Dr. Creighton, Professor Crookshank, and many other eminent medical men, do not hesitate to cast in their lot lot with them, and to condemn vaccination as " a grotesque super- stition."</p>
        <p>Now as to Dr. Gornall's photographs. No one con- tends that the unvaccinated are proof against smallpox. We only say that they fare as well as the vaccinated, so that his photo of "a man who was unvaccinated and died from smallpox " rather misses the mark. It is for him to explain why such cases are found among the vaccinated and re-vaccinated.</p>
        <p>Dr. Gornall's 4th view " showed a boy suffering from smallpox and by the bedside a girl who, to the horror of the medical staff, was found to he free from smallpox, having been removed in error from the fever hospital. She was at once vaccinated and 'sat on the bed side to show that she was proof against contagion." Dr. Gornall wishes to draw a large conclusion from very small pre- mises, but page 46 of his own report records a case which completely nullifies the above. I refer to that of Sarah A. F—, Dallam-lane, who was vaccinated 24 hours after exposure to infection and who on the 12th day after" presented the eruption of a very mild attack of smallpox." Dr. Gornall's position appears to he this. A person is expos,d to infection, promptly vaccinated, and escapes enlsti-pOX--‘ • thanks to the vaccination." But another similarly exposed and as promptly vaccinated develops a mild attack of small-pox—" vaccination has modifind the disease." A third, similarly treated, dies of the dis.ase—" Alas! the vaccination was too late !". .</p>
        <p>The fallacy of Dr. Gornall's position appears to lie in the assumptian that everyone who is exposed to the in fection of small-pox is certain to develop the disease SuCh is not the rase. In a little work published last century by Dr. Baylies,entitled "Inoculation in Berlin," the author records ne less than nineteen abortive at- tempts to communicate small-pox to childrm by inocu- lation—they all failed to develep the disease notwith- standing that they were lir-Tulare,' with the virus in both arms, and shut up in a small bedroom, for some hours, with a had CASA of confluent small-pox. _ _ _</p>
        <p>But we need not go so far afield for an example of the newer of the healthy constitution to resist smallpox. In 1893 the township of Oldbury (which I have the honour to represent en the Urban District Council and Board of Guardians), was visited hy an epidemic of smallpox. The disease spread to a family of the name of Loach. rhe vaccinated members were the first to succumb, but eventually the unvaccinated infant was attacked, and as the mother was nursing the child at the breast, she had to accompany it to the hospital. She had never been vaccinated, and the medical oißeer urged her to at once undergo the operation. But she declined ; continued the nursing er her child till the fatal termination of its illness. aed came out of the hospital scathless. Unfortunately, I failed to obtain a photograph of this unvaccinated mother nursing her smallpox stricken child, or it would have afforded a far more impressive object lesson than Dr. Gornall's 4th picture.—Yours truly,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>A. TROBRIDGE. Langley, near Birminzham, 9th April, 1895.</head>
        <p>A. TROBRIDGE. Langley, near Birminzham, 9th April, 1895.</p>
        <p>A. SUDDEN CHANGE in the weather always brings disastrous results. It checks the circula,ion, closes the pores of the skin, and throws the .blood of the hod y hark upon those delicate organs, the kidneys. Then come headaches, colds, coughing, sneezing and the thousand troubles so annoy. inc and ofben dangerous. The kidneys are the most delicate organs of the body, and almost the first to suffer, whether in men or women. To keep them in order means perfect health mere than anything else conceivable; to allow them to become dis- ordered means certain disease or death. There is hut one great remedy for kidney troubles, and that is Warner's Safe Cure. This fact is admitted by the highest medical authorities throughout the world, and the great revolu- tion it has wrought in curing complicated kidney troubles, and even Bright's Disease, and in preventing the evil effects of the season upon the kidneys, has Justly mads it the mast popular of modern remedies.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH'.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH'.</p>
        <p>HILL CLIFFS AND STOCKTON HEAT UNITA]) BAND O' ROYAL—The first annual festival in coaneeition with this Band of Hope was held on Monday evon- ing. the chair being taken by the President, the Rev. J. S. thwhes, who congratulated the workers on the success of the session. There was a crowded attendance. The programme included recitations by Masters Tom Breretnn and Gcorge Moss,Mr. G.Whitfield, and Misses Annie Brereton,Flrlena Grounds and Edwards, gongs by Masters G. Taylor, J. Houghton, Mr.Fairhurst, Mr. Potts, Miss Eva Grounds,dialogues by Messrs. Brant- hall,Davenport,Grounds, Ashbrook, Kenworthv,Hughes, Houghton, and Jackson. gavotte, Miss Olive Potts. and Messrs.C. K. Potts and W. T. Davenpnrt, rand march, Mr. Potts and party. Mr. A. B. Kenworthy presided at the organ. A short but interesting report was given by the secretary, Mr. Hallows, and instructive and inspiring address by Miss BPSSiP Hutton, of Moore. Short addresses were also delivered by Miss Bradford (Reddish Hall), Mr. Henry Eves, Mr. J. Miller, and Mr. Brereton.</p>
        <p>T. MoTarits.—Mrs.Winslow's Soothing Syrup has been used over fifty years br millions of mothers for theirchildren while teehing with perfect SlleCeBB. It will relieve the poor sufferer immediately. It is pleasant to taste ; it preduces natural quiet &amp;leap, by relieving the child from pain. snd the little cherub awakes "as tu-ichti as a button." Of all Chemists, is. lii. per bottle.—Adv.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
