      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>GOOD -TEUPLAR CONVENTION AT</head>
        <p>GOOD -TEUPLAR CONVENTION AT</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>The monthly session of the No. 1 Convention of South West Lancashire (Good Templars), was held in the Co. operative Hall, Earlestown, on Saturday. The Conven- tion was opened at 4 o'clock, Bro. Roberts, C.C.T., pre- siding. The reports of the C.C.T. and C. Sec. were re- ceived, the latter baing read by Bro. J. Miller. The re- port of Bro. E. Middleton, C.S.J•T., showed good work done in the area. At Wigan, where an effort had been , made to restart the lodge, they had hopes of an early re- opening. It was decided to help the Wigan lodge by means of an entertainment to be given in the Wigan Public Hall, in order to raise funds to clear off the debt. Bro. A. E. Eccles had sent two parcels of leaflets and books for the purpose of helping forward Juvenile Tern- plary in the Convention area. He also reported that he had visited the Vulcan lodge, and in regard to the other Temples in the district he had found them in a flourishing condition. The-report of Bro. A. Francts,C.E. Supt., made allusions to the Council elections at Earles- . .</p>
        <p>town and Leigh, at the latter place a Temperance Coun- cillor having been elected by a majority of 400 votes He also said that the Local Veto Bill woulJ be brought •torward in the House of Commons on Monday, and ex- pressed the hope that it would be successfully carried</p>
        <p>through. The next place of meeting was fixed for Hind- Icy on May 11. The credential committee reported 60 present. A resolution for the Hope of Earlestown lodge that the time of opening the Convention in future be 3.30 was adopted. The programme for the next session was left in the hands of the Convention Executive. A paper on " Antiquity of Teetotalism "by Bro. Matins, G.c.T., was afterwards read by Bro. J. Miller, W. H. Dale, and Jelin Taylor. A short discussion ensued and votes of tip nks to the readers were adopted on the:motion of Bro. J. Lindon, seconded by itrosA. Francis. A resolution of thanks to Sir William Harcourt for his efforts in connection with the Local Veto Bill, with an ex pr:ssion of hope that pressure would be brought to bear, so that the bill should be passed during the next session of Parliament, was unanimously adopted on the motion of Bro. Clarke, secon,led by Bro. Vint. Votes of Vianks were tendered the Earlestown friends for the excellency of their arrangements. The Convention was afterwards closed and a public tea, at which there was a large attendance, was held. In the evening a public meeting and entertainment was held, the hall being crowded with an enthusiastic audience. Bro. J. Miller, CT., presided, and he Was accompanied on the platform by Bms.J. H. Musk D.E.S., J. W. It Merts, and the Convention officers.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said that was his first appearance as chairman of a public meeting, but he was proud that it was in such a good cause. He proceeded to speak of the success which had attended the Hope of harlestown Lodge, evidence of that being found in the block and gavel in front of him, which had been presented to the lodge for the largest increase in membership during the past year. The lodge was proud of it, and intended to do their utmost to keep it. He alluded to the objects of the temperance meetings, and strongly urged all young men to adopt their principles. He said there were many temptations surrounding young men in athletic circles, and said they needed all their firmness to withstand them. He urged them to stand true to the principle, system and method of Temperance Societies, as the prin- ciples of teetotalism were the best teaching for the young. Something, however, was required as a counter attrac- tion to the temptations he had referred to, and he was of opinion that the Temperance and Religious Organizations should aim at providing rooms, where social games, reading, &amp;c., could be indulged in. which would do much to help on to a more successful issue the efforts of Societies,which hitherto had comparatively been failures. (Applause.) Bro. J. H. Musk, D.C.S., of Manchester, also addressed the gathering of the subject of temperance. During the 1 evening a programme of songs, duets, quartettes, and instrumental selections, was pleasingly submitted by Misses Conway, Mee and Mrs. Latimer, Messrs. A. Canton, W. Limond, W. Crowther, Masters S. Ming and B. Latimer. Mrs. E. Latimer, A.R.C.0., accom- panied.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-04-13_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>SALE OF WORK AT NEWTON.</head>
        <p>THE SALE OF WORK AT NEWTON.</p>
        <p>The sale of work to which we referred last week, in connection with the Congregational Church, Newton, was continued on Friday and Saturday. On the former day it it was opened by Mr. B. B. Glover, at one time Superintendent of the Congregational Sunday School. A brisk sale was carried on during the day, and a satis- factory amount of business was done. On Saturday the opening ceremony was performed by Mr. A. Syme, of Newton, who in the cmurse of his observations, sail Mr. Macrae and Mr. Glover,had done their part so well, that they had left very little for him to do. except to say " Amen," to their addresses. He thought the church was very much indebted to them for their assistance and liberality on that occasion. Although they had left the district, and had other calls on their liberality, they were always ready, mindful of old associations to give them a helping hand in their time of need. Both of I them expressed a hope that when his (Mr. Syme's) turn came, he should find the stalls empty and nothing left for him to talk about. The stalls were not empty vet, he wished they were, but he hoped with their kind assistance that evening that their hopes would be realised: As the whole income of Crow-lane Church de- pended upon:the voluntary contributions of the mem- bers, adherents, circumstances would arise, such as the present, when measures must be taken to prevent the church being crippled by a load of debt. He took that opportunity, on behalf of the church, to thank the neighbours, friends and tradesmen in the district for the material support they had given to make the sale a success. Such kindness was very gratifying, as a recog- nition and approval of the good work done in the dis- trict by Crow-lane Church, under the guidance of Mr. Allatt. He thought they deserved success as a reward for the labour expended and the ingenuity displayed in bringing together such a splendid collection of useful and ornamentalsgoods. He then formally declared the sale open. Business was at once proceeded with, and as there was a large attendance, buying and selling was carried on briskly. In the evening the room was crowded, and the various concerts and entertainments were well patronised. It is specially to be noticed that throughout the whole affair, everything was conducted on perfectly fair and honest principle, full value being given for the money spent. The half-hour concerts were musical treats, and the Mar:lMM of curiosities was certainly worthy of a visit. Everybody worked with the utmost enthusiasm to ensure success, not the least energetic being Mrs. Allatt, to whrim a good share of the success is due, her task of organ'sing the ladies being an unenviable one. At the close of the sale the stalls bore a bare appearance, but there are still a few articles left. It is expected, however, that the object for which the sale was held, has been accomplished.</p>
        <p>NOTES BY THE WAY AT EARLESTOWN. (By IN OCCASIONAL CONTRIBUTOR.) The Salvation Army have had quite a lively time of it during the last, few days. They had a " ham and glory tea" on Saturday, and then on Sunday there was knee drill, and many other celebratisns described in terms quite foreign to my uninitiated mind. The great event however was on Monday when the Army assembled in great numbers in the Wesleyan Chapel, for the purpose of witnessing a dedication service, in connection with the infant daughter of the local captain and his wife. To those who are ignorant of the Salvation Army methods, I may say, that the dedication was for the purpose of handing over to the Army in the service of God, the infant in question. The services were exceedingly hearty and enthusiastic, but, oh ! that band!</p>
        <p>Sergeant Longworth after long years of waiting has at last received the reward of perseverance and industry, and in future will be known to all and sundry as Mr. Inspector Longworth. gverything comes to him who waits, they say, and Inspector Longworth has certainly waited quite long enough. I am not behind the scenes, but I know this much that if merit and a strict attention to duty go for anything he should have been an inspector five years ago. However, he at last ascends another rung of the ladder, and I know he will give the authorities no cause for regret.</p>
        <p>The schools are in a bad state about here just at pre;ent, and it is a most welcome thing that the Easter holidays are upon Us. Measles, whooping cough, and other juvenile ailments are rampant, and I am afraid unless things mend somewhat the schools will have to be closed. I don't know why, hut we seem to be rarely free in Earlestown from these tcoublesome diseases. Our sanitary arrange- ments are good, we are not crowded,and the schools are situated in healthy neighbourhoods. I think however that some parents are sadly to blame for these diseases. When they have one of the family laid aside say with measles, they do not trouble to keep the other children at home, but pack them off to school, and so the infection is spread.</p>
        <p>THE DARESBURY SCHOOL BOARD.</p>
        <p>The us-uil monthly meeting of the Daresbury School Board was held on Friday afternoon. Mr. Peter Tickle occupied the chair, and the other mem- bers present were Messrs. Alfred Broome, John Cooper, Peter Oleave,Henry MawdslPy,Rev. Thomas Whitley and Mr. S. Sharp (clerk for the time being) From the estimate for the ensuing half-year read by the Clerk it appeared that £297 was required in calls. It was resolved that calls at the rate of It in the be prepared tor signature at the next meet- ing. The accounts amounting to .E 163 2s. 9d. were examined and passed. The report of the School Attendance Officer was read and considered.</p>
        <p>EXCITING ACCIDENT ON THE SHIP</p>
        <p>CANAL.</p>
        <p>LATCHFORD LOCK GATE SMASHED.</p>
        <p>The first accident in connection with the Latch- ford Locks occurred under serious circumstances on Thursday afternoon about five o'clock. An eye-witness states that the steamship " Harold," a medium sized boat, and outward bound from Manchester was signalled to enter the 450 ft. lock—the size of the large lock when the inter-mediate gates are closed. Instead of slowing down, however. the Vessel apparently came full speed ahead, and passing ,through the open outer gates she crashed her bows on to the gates at the closed end. The impact was terrific,and the right hand gate ~was torn completely from its hinges and hurled-into the water, where it sank at the entrance to the lock from the Liverpool end The remaining gate was forced partly from its fas- tenings, and hue,,' half-submerged by the roller chains from the lock-wall. The vessel, of course, after passing the gates had a sudden drop of 16ft. to the lower level of water on the Warrington side of the locks. This, with the immense volume of water behind, caused her to plunge deeply into the seething turmoil below. The force of the blow made an enormous crash, while the water rose and half covered theiguivering "Harold," whose crew 90 suddenly awakened to their danger, stirieked aloud as the vessel went down. Fortu- nately, after the collision the boat righted herself, but was dashed against the wall before she could get free. Meanwhile,tbe officials seeing the impend- ing calamity and the impossibility of averting it, put on the engines to close the top gates and stop the rush of water. The result was that the gates instead of coming steadily and meeting at a leaf@ angle were separated, and one, the left band one, Went in front of the other, so that the latter came directly behind it and with a resounding crash deove it forward, so that the immense structure is bent into a curve while the water still rushes through the uneven joining and strained hinges. After leaving the lock,the "Harold," whose bow-plates near the water line were stove in, rounded the corner and was moored to the bank in the sluice-bay. Here the water in the ballast tank was released to lighten her, and says the on- looker, it poured in a steady stream through the fissures in the plate. No one on board was injured, but a small flat working at the bank close by was so violently upheaved by the sudden rush of water that a workman was thrown off into the canal. He was immediately recovered, however. Later on in the evening the position of affairs was critical from several reasons. At the top of the lock the gates were so jammed that a body of water about 10ft, long was pouring through zhe fissure with threatening violence. Inside the lock the water was greatly agitated and appeared likely to displace the remaining intermediate gate from its broken hinges. Each gate is or ponderous green heart, weighing 300 tons, and the one sunk prevented the outer gates being closed to relieve the pressure. Looked at calmly the gate presented, a very wrecked appearance. Its top platform of 3m. planks was split across, while the sheets of iron at the butt were twisted and broken in all directions. On the opposite wall where only the socket of the other gate was left,there was not much to see but what there was, was very signific- ant. The bolts with which the structure was originally supported, though inches thick Were snapped asunder like the proverbial carrot. Accord- ingly, there was nothing between the higher and lower reaches Of the canal but the damaged lock gates. To all appearance the gate was gradually giving way to the pressure, and little wonder the officials worked with might and main to stave off the impending disaster. Needless to say, if the gate gave way before the Ipwer gate was closed, the re- sult would be disastrous, and it would practically have involved the draining of the upper waters of the canal from Warn locks, and in sueh a manner that the immense rush would certainly very greatly damage the canal as well as any vessel which might be between 'dam and Latchford. As it ie,until the lock gates have been repaired, larger vessels will be prevented from using the canal, as they have to make use of the large lock, and it is of course use- less in its present state. Large numbers of people visited the lock as soon as the nature of the acci- dent became known.</p>
        <p>Mr. Hunter, the chief engineer of the canal, was soon on the spot, and arrangements made for rais- ing the sunken gate. A diver was sent down and the position of the impediment examined. Consequent on the large lock being blocked by the gate,all the vessels small enough were compelled to use the lesser lock. Some apprehension was felt as to the larger vessels which were expected. It wrould be impossible to let them through and the conse- quent delay would entail great expense. Strenu- ous endeavours were made to effect a clearance,but up to Wednesday night nothing material bad been effected.</p>
        <p>GILBERT GREEN—ALL LODGE OF FREEMASONS. GRAND CONCERT. It should be a matter of gratification to the officers of the above lodge that they so successfully organised and brought to such a successful issue, the concert held in the Lion Hotel Assembly Room, on Monday evening. There was a large attendance, and the concert com- menced in good time with "The Shatnrock " (Batchel- der), by Messrs. W. Reynolds, J. P. Robinson, P. Ban- nan, and Edgar J. Stables (A.T.T.8.), who gave that somewhat difficult song in a masterly and precise man- ner. Srnart's "When the wind blows in from the Sea" was next rendered by Miss Bertha Stafford and Mr.E. J. Stables, whose voices blending in a harmonious manner lent an extra charm to a song with abundance of taking music in it. Mr. J. P. Robinson's rendition of "Good , night, beloved," received well-merited applause, as also did Miss Bertha Stafford, who for so young an artiste ! exhibited considerable talent in singing "La Zingarella" (Campana), which is itself a most tuneful production. She was followed by Mr. James Batchelder, who, on the pianoforte rendered "Bohemian Girl" fantasia (Balfe), and " The Men of Harlech " fantasia. The fantastic music of the former and the well- known air of the latter formed two very plea- sant selections. Mr. Stables appeared next in " The Bandolerc." which though rather reci- tative in character, he sang in finished style. The popular air" Killarney" was next on the list and in this Mr. Reynold's remarkably clear alto voice was heard to good advantage, though its almost feminine tone pro- voked an occasional smile. Mr. W. H. Stonelv scored the greatest success in his violin selections, the first of which was " Pentasie Caprice" (VieuxtempA), the intri- cate passages in it being completely under the sway of the violinist. The last item preceding the interval was "The Gipsy's Laughing 'Frio, by Messrs. Stables, Robinson, and Bannan, who made the most of this bril- liant and sparkling piece. The second half of the programme was commenced by Messrs. W. Reynolds, J. P. Robinson, P. Barman, and E. J. Stables, who sang "Ye Banks and Braes," (Batchel- der) with pronounced success. Benedict's duet "The moon has raised her lamp above," found able interpre- ters in Messrs. Robinson and Stables, while "The songs !my mother sang" was rendered by Miss Bertha Stafford wko certainly equalled, if not surpassed, her ! previous efforts. Mr. Stoneley's undoubted ability as violinist Was further shdwn in a Concerto by him for which he was " bravoed " and encored. Another song entitled "Mary," was next sung by Mr. Robinson who acquitted himself very creditably. The remaining items, all of which were credits to the singers were, song " Trn- , requited Love (Cellier), Mr. Edgar J. Stables ; song, "The Lost Chard," Mr. Reynolds; part song (A. T. T. B.) "Salley in our alley," (Batchelder). The musical arrangements for the evening were under the direction of tiro. James Batchelder, P.M., who also acted as accompanist throughout the evening. About the way in which Mr. Batchelder performed both duties it is un- necessary to dilate, beyond saying that he gave every possible satisfaction.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AMATNITR PHOTOGRAPHIC SOCIISTY A:meetiug of the above Society was held in the Museum Committee Room on Wednesday evening, when a demonstration was given by Mr. A. C. Bald- win, of the Eastman's Photographic Company. The president of the Society, Mr. J. Fairhurs, occupied the chair. Mr. Baldwin at the commencement of his address. introduced to the notice of the .lociety a new camera recently brought out by the Company caded the Tourists Kodak. This admitted of Loth instantaneous and time exposure, and was fitted with a roller film which allowed of 48 exposures. He then proceeded to given instructions with regard to the toning ef Solio paper, and gave some excel- lent specimens of work done by that paper. He afterwards gave a demonstration upon the working of Nikko and platino-bromide paper which are made by the Company. He successfully developed a large number of contact exposures, and enlarge- ments in these two classes of paper, and:some very fine results were witnessed. The developer for this purpose which he recommended, was amidol, but he warned his hearers that if this developer were used, it would be necessary to have a correct ex- posure, as, it beingla single solution developer,there was very little latitude allowed. At the close of the meeting a hearty vote of thanks was accorded Mr. Baldwin for his demonstration, on the motion of the Chairman, seconded by Mr. Hesketh, and supported by Mr. Pritchard.</p>
