        <p>WARRINGTON. 801 BRIDGE STREET, 20nt MARCH, 1895. MRS. JOSEPH DOBSON begs to thank her numerous friends and customers for the generous sup- port accorded to her late husband for many years past. and begs to announce that she has TRANSFERRED the BUSI- NESS to her eldest son MR. J. Y 7. DOBSON, and trusts that the same patronage will be extended to him. Mrs. Dobson will attend at the above address on Wednesdays and Saturdays for the purpose of receiving accounts awing to the estate f her late husband. Warrington, 80, Bridge-street. 20th March, 1895. LADIFEI AND GENTLEMEN, Raving succeeded to the business so long, and sue- cessfullycarried on by my late father I beg to appeal to the public for a share of their p dronage and support. I intend carrying on the business on the same lines as my respested father, and trust with unremitting attention and by offer- ing the best qualities of meat obtainable at reasonable prices, to secure the confidence of the public which it shall be my earnest endeavour to merit. Town and country orders strictly attended to.— Yours faithfully, JOHN W. DOBSON. .THOMAS FRAIN, DEBT COLLECTOR and GENERAL COMMISSION AGENT, 39, GLADSTONE STREET, WARRINGTON. DEBTS BOUGHT, OR COLLECTED ON COMMISSION Special Arrangements, Sm. for the Collection of Wholesale ACCOUNTS. REFERENCE and RATES of COMMISSION on applicAtion. AGENT for the Imperial Live Stock, Accident, and Northern Plate Glass Insurance Co.</p>
        <p>THE MOSII PERFECT LIGHT. SUPERIOR TO ELECTRIC LIGHT, AT ONE-EIGHTH THE COST. SIX VALID REASONS For its Adoption. 1. It saves half your gas bill. 2. It gives you treble the present light. 3. It con be attached to existing gas fittings. 4. Tha light is clean, cool, steady. and brilliant. 5. It does away with the impuriti.s of gas. 6. It is pleasant, simple, and economical. Extract trom the Report el Prof. CARLTON LAMBERT, M.A., F.E.A.S. " The • Welsbaoh Light' Is nearly seven times as efficient in minatlng effect as ordinary gas burners, and more than four times as efficient as an I Amami.' Professor MAXIMULLEE writes "7, Norbam Gardens, Oxford, February;lo, 1894. "Professor Max Miller has much pleasure in stat- ing that he finds one Isicand-scent Burner gives enough light in his library, where he formerly re- quired three burners, and if the pressure of gas was low a lamp as well. In his drawing-room two burners give a splendid light. He has also an Incandescent Burner In the hall, which is most satisfactory. His gas bill last quarter, with these four burners In use, was £1 2.3 less than the same quarter last year, though the price of gas is higher." FOR PRIVATE HOUSES, SHOPS, SHOW ROO5fS; HOTELS, OFFICES, and PUBLIC BUILDINGS. Prices and full particulars on application to the following Agents. WARRINOTON CALDWELLS, LIMITED, 60, Horsernarlist-street. BARLESTOWN Mrs. E. NEEDHAM, ST. ETNIAVNII : R. G. BROOK, Wolverhampton Hoene. ' RUNCORN H. BRANCH, 109, Greenway•reast. WIDNES: H. BRANCH, Central Hotel.</p>
