        <p>18, PRESCOTT-STREET, ONE CLO WES-STREET, WEST GORTON, MANCHESTER, DECEMBER 7TH, 1894. DEAR SIR,—I beg to testify to the value of GWILYM Evans' QUININE BITTERS, and I think that you cannot recommend them too well. About a fortnight since I left my work—l commenced my illness by feeling starved, and a nasty stintting and shooting pain in my head; a doctor was called in, and lie said it was the llnfluenza, and I suffered for over a week of the pains in my head, and the doctor's medicine did me no good, till at last the mistress of this house (I am in lodgings here) fell ill with the same symptoms of illness till her husband advised us to try a bottle of your Bitters. He got a 16.14 d. bottle, and I may tell you the first &amp;se gave us relief. After this we got a 2s. 9d. bottle, and now we are all right and I am at my work now, and! have no doubt that if I had taken the Bitters at first I should have lost no work, and I owe my recovery to the bene- ficial virtues of your medicine, and I beg to tender to you my sincere thanks for producine such a valuable medicine to suffering humanity. I shall recommend it to all my friends, and I cannot speak too highly of it, as it is worth its weight in gold. (Signed) JAMES SAG AR. G WILY M EVANS' QUININE BITTERS is strongly recommended to all patients who are or have been suffering from INFLUENZA, and also patients who are convalescent after Fevers, Colds, Bronchitis, &amp;c., and are slew in recovering their accustomed strength and spirits. GWILYM EVANS' QUININE BITTERS. THE VEGETABLE TONIC. ADVANTAGES CLAIMED. 1. It is Purely Vegetable, and contains no Iron or Mercury. 2. It is a happy-combination of Glyinine with other Medicines. 3. It is scientifically prepared. 4. It enjoys the confidence of eminent Medical Men. 5. It strikes at the source of the Disease, and is superior to all other Bitters prepared, as testified by hundreds ef testimonials from all parts of the world. GWILYM EVANS' QUININE BITTERS. THE VEGETABLE TONIC. Sold in 2s. 9d. and 4s. 6d. bottles. Samples ls.lid.size Cr See the name " Gwilym Evans" on stamp, label, and bottle. This is important, as there are numerous. imitations. PROPRIETORS : QUININE BITTERS CO., LTD. LLINELLY, SOUTH WALES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON SWIMMING CLUB. ANNUAL MEETING. On Thursday evening last week the annual meeting of the Warrington Swimming Club was held in the Batt- S Hotel. There was a strong muster of membersoind Mr. J. Grace took the chair in the absence of the president. The Chairman, at thp. commencement of the meeting, read a report of the work of tke club during the pas year. He showed that they had been very successfut and had played nine polo matches, won seven, and lost two. In the final of the Liverpool and District Water Polo Association they had been the " runners-up." It had also been their endeavour to bring together all the talent of the swimming world they ,3ould for the benefit of the spectators, and they had been honoured by Having played in the Warrington bath. the North v. South of England polo match and also the Lan- cashire v. Yorkshire match. After all the expenses were paid they had a balance in hand from the previous year of £l6,a position which few clubs elsewhere could show. It was also announced that the club had arranged for the final in the" AthEletic News" Cup to be played at Warrington, and attempts were being constantly made to make the coming season's programme as attractive as the last. On the election of officers taking place, Mr. Fairfield Turner was elected captain of the polo team and Mr. T. Locker sub-captain. Mr. David Walsh was elected secretary in place of Mr. Grounds and S. Wilson, the latter of whom had resigned. Mr.H. Toft was elected treasurer and the following are the Committee, Messrs. G. Grace, J. Critchley, J. A. Sutton, R. T. Harding, C. H. Wood, G. Grounds, Stanley Wilson, G. Johnson, J. Beswick, G. Scotson, A. Booth, and F. Garside. The Mayor of Warrington was re-elected president and the following gentlemen were added to the list of vice-presi- dents, Mr. J. Houghton, Councillor H. Roberts, and Mr. Thomas Morris. F.G.S. It might also be mentioned that Mr. Grace has been elected a delegate for the Club and he has been placed upon the executive committee of Nor- thern Countries Swimming Association who have made him a member also of the Amateur Swimming Associa. tion. Vesuuncr.—On Tuesday morning, at the Lvmm Police Court, before Mr. James Gregson, a man named John Brown' of no settled abode, was charged with having begged alms in Broom Edge-lane, at 11 o'clock on Mon- day night. Sergeant Garner and P.C. Sandbach de- tailed the circumstances of the case, which showed that the defendant, whilst in a drunken state, had accosted persons and asked them for coppers; when they refused he abused them, using very bad language.—Defendant was committed to prison for seven days with hard labour.</p>
